/* Chapter 9 — canonical topics, questions, flashcards and exam prompts. */
IFL_DATA.registerChapter({"number":9,"title":"Murabaha and Musawamah","part":"part-iii","partRoman":"III","pages":[213,240],"minutes":80,"estimates":{"f1":80,"f2":75},"difficulty":"Intermediate","difficultyAlt":"intermediate","learningObjectives":[{"id":"lo-ch9-1","text":"Recall the conditions of a valid sale as applied to Murabaha.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch9-2","text":"Define Murabaha as a trust sale (Bai‘ al Amanah) and trace its classical treatment and purpose.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch9-3","text":"State the specific conditions of Murabaha: eligible goods, cost disclosure, additional expenses, profit margin.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch9-4","text":"Explain Murabaha–Mu’ajjal and why a credit price above cash price is not Riba.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch9-5","text":"Compare the three possible structures and explain Murabaha to Purchase Orderer (MPO) as a bundle of contracts.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch9-6","text":"Sequence the stages of MPO with the client as agent and identify Shari’ah risks at each stage.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch9-7","text":"Evaluate issues: buy-back, Khiyar, timing, defaults, rebates, rollover, share and commodity Murabaha.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch9-8","text":"Distinguish Musawamah from Murabaha and identify when each is suitable.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch9-9","text":"State the general conditions of a valid Bai' (sale) and explain the specific additional conditions that apply to Murabaha.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch9-10","text":"Distinguish Murabaha, Musawamah, Tawliyah and Wadhi'ah as forms of fiduciary and ordinary sale.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch9-11","text":"Explain why classical jurists saw Murabaha as a protective, restricted sale, and how modern 'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer' (MPO) differs from classical Murabaha.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch9-12","text":"Walk through the full MPO transaction structure - promise, agency, purchase, offer/acceptance, execution - and identify the Shari'ah risks (especially Bai' al 'Inah) at each stage.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch9-13","text":"Analyze contested operational issues in Murabaha: buy-back, Khiyar, timing of execution, default penalties, early-payment rebates, rollover, shares Murabaha and commodity Murabaha/Tawarruq.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch9-14","text":"Explain how Musawamah differs from Murabaha and when banks prefer to use it.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch9-15","text":"Apply the accounting treatment principles (AAOIFI) for Murabaha receivables, Hamish Jiddiyah and profit recognition.","origin":["f2"]}],"why":"Murabaha is the most widely used Islamic financing mode — and the most criticised. Understanding exactly where ownership, possession and risk pass, and which shortcuts (rollover, buy-back, agent-only paperwork) turn it into disguised interest, is essential for exams and for practice.","whyExpanded":"Murabaha (in its MPO form) is the single most widely used Islamic financing mode worldwide, and Musawamah is its close cousin for large or hard-to-cost transactions. Getting the structure right - genuine ownership, real risk-bearing, correctly sequenced promise/agency/sale contracts - is what separates a Shari'ah-compliant financing from a disguised interest loan (Bai' al 'Inah). This chapter is foundational for every subsequent financing-mode chapter and for understanding why Islamic banks are structured the way they are.","overview":"The chapter recaps valid-sale conditions, defines Murabaha as a fiduciary cost-plus sale, explains its classical basis and need, its specific conditions and credit form, then examines structures — direct trading, third-party agent, client as agent — and in detail Murabaha to Purchase Orderer. It discusses issues (buy-back, options, timing, default, rebates, rollover, shares, commodity Murabaha), precautions (Boxes 9.1–9.4) and Musawamah.","summary":"PLS modes may not suit all needs, so jurists allow trade-based modes like Murabaha, which becomes Murabaha–Mu’ajjal when used by banks: a stipulated profit margin over a known cost, with a fixed due date. It is legitimate if the financier bears the risk until possession passes and buys and sells under separate contracts. Despite its dangers, banks rely heavily on it — partly because depositors such as widows and the elderly need low-risk investment — so it must be used with utmost caution. A credit price above cash price is permissible; PLS is preferable. Modern Murabaha is MPO: a promise, an agency contract and the actual Murabaha under a master agreement. Banks must ensure genuine goods, pay suppliers directly or verify invoices, and avoid buy-back and rollover; default penalties go to charity.","summaryExpanded":"Chapter 9 establishes Murabaha and Musawamah as the two dominant forms of credit sale used by Islamic banks. It traces Murabaha's classical roots as a fiduciary, cost-disclosed sale meant to protect unskilled buyers, then details how modern banks structure it as 'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer' (MPO) - a bunch of three sequenced contracts (promise, agency, sale) designed so the bank genuinely takes ownership and risk before reselling, guarding above all against Bai' al 'Inah (disguised buy-back). The chapter works through a dense set of operational issues - Khiyar, timing, default penalties, early-payment rebates, the strict prohibition on rollover, shares Murabaha, and the author's caution about overusing commodity Murabaha/Tawarruq - before closing with Musawamah as a bargained-price alternative for large or hard-to-disclose transactions.","summarySection":"9.12","takeaways":["Murabaha = trust sale: disclose cost; agree margin; price fixed.","Not for currencies, gold/silver or debts; intangibles allowed.","MPO = promise + agency + Murabaha — three separate contracts.","Bank must own and bear risk before selling; goods must exist at execution.","No buy-back (‘Inah), no rollover; rescheduling without increase only.","Default: charity penalty; solatium only via court for actual loss.","Rebate: discretionary only (AAOIFI).","Musawamah: no cost disclosure; for big deals or undisclosed discounts.","Murabaha requires mandatory, honest cost disclosure (a 'Bai' al Amanah'/fiduciary sale); Musawamah requires no cost disclosure at all - otherwise both share the same underlying sale conditions.","Modern bank Murabaha (MPO) is legally three separate, sequenced contracts: a promise to purchase, an agency agreement to acquire the goods, and the actual Murabaha sale - concluded only once the bank owns the goods.","The central Shari'ah risk throughout MPO is Bai' al 'Inah (disguised buy-back): the bank must always verify the customer never already owned the goods, and must genuinely bear ownership risk before reselling.","A customer's promise to purchase can be made binding under contemporary scholarly opinion (Usmani, AAOIFI, OIC Fiqh Academy); breach entitles the bank to recover its actual loss only, never opportunity cost.","Deferred payment (Bai' Mu'ajjal) with a credit-price premium is not Riba because it is a trade of money for goods (not money for money), and fits the principle Al-Ghunm bil Ghurm (profit accompanies risk).","Rescheduling (same amount, later date) is permitted; rollover (adding fresh markup to a defaulted receivable) is explicit Riba and is prohibited.","Late-payment penalties must go to charity, not bank profit (absent a court-awarded solatium); early-payment rebates are generally disallowed by majority opinion, though AAOIFI permits a discretionary, non-stipulated rebate.","Commodity Murabaha (Tawarruq) is flagged by the author as a last-resort liquidity tool, not for routine use, given doubts about genuine transaction occurrence and its resemblance to interest-based financing if misused."],"takeawayOrigins":["f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2"],"checklist":["Can you list the specific conditions of Murabaha?","Can you explain why Murabaha–Mu’ajjal is not Riba?","Can you compare the three Murabaha structures?","Can you name the three contracts in MPO and their order?","Can you walk through the seven steps of MPO with client as agent?","Can you state the rules on defaults, rebates and rollover?","Can you apply Box 9.1 risk controls?","Can you distinguish Murabaha from Musawamah?"],"topics":[{"id":"t9.1","section":"9.1","title":"Introduction: Credit Sale Forms and MPO","pages":[213,214],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["murabaha","musawamah"],"intuition":"Islamic banks don’t just finance trade — they must actually trade. How?","simpleExplanation":"Islamic banks must be involved in buying and selling goods and bear business risk. Their clients need credit, so banks sell on credit (Bai‘ Mu’ajjal), usually as Musawamah (bargaining on price) or Murabaha (cost-plus with disclosed cost). Experts prefer PLS but Murabaha is used extensively. Modern bank Murabaha — Murabaha to Purchase Orderer — differs from classical Murabaha.","academicExplanation":["Conventional banks provide funds for trade; Islamic banks must be involved in sale and purchase according to Shari’ah trading rules, earning profit by bearing business risk. Credit sale takes forms including Musawamah (bargain on price, delivery, deferred payment) and Murabaha (bargain on profit margin over a disclosed cost). Although experts advise PLS and discourage excessive Murabaha, its permissibility is beyond doubt and all Islamic banks use it extensively. Modern Murabaha is concluded with a prior promise to buy — Murabaha to Purchase Orderer (MPO), also the basis of the AAOIFI standard. Focus areas: fixity of price, ownership, risk and possession before sale, timing and receivables."],"examExplanation":"Islamic banks trade and bear risk. Credit sale forms: Musawamah (price bargained) and Murabaha (margin over disclosed cost). Modern form = MPO (AAOIFI standard).","keyPoints":["Murabaha permissibility is beyond doubt; its extensive use is debated."],"definitions":[{"term":"Bai‘ Mu’ajjal","definition":"A credit sale: goods are delivered now and the price is paid later, in a lump sum or instalments, at a fixed date."},{"term":"Murabaha to Purchase Orderer (MPO)","definition":"Murabaha concluded after a customer’s request and promise to buy: the bank buys the asset from a third party and sells it to the customer at cost plus profit on deferred payment."}],"examples":[{"title":"Musawamah or Murabaha?","kind":"practice","text":"A bank sells a generator to a client for 1,150,000 payable in a year. If it tells the client its cost (1,000,000) and the agreed margin, the sale is Murabaha. If it just agrees a price without reference to cost, it is Musawamah (pp. 213–214)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Bank Murabaha is identical to classical Murabaha.","right":"Classical Murabaha was an ordinary trust sale; modern bank Murabaha is MPO — preceded by a promise, often with the client as agent, and used on deferred payment (pp. 213, 217)."}],"relatedTopics":["t9.3","t9.8"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"What distinguishes Murabaha from Musawamah?","options":["Murabaha is always cash","In Murabaha the seller discloses cost and parties bargain on the profit margin","Musawamah requires disclosure","No difference"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 213."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch9-t1"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch9-t1","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"9.1","sectionTitle":"Introduction","title":"Trade-Based Financing: Musawamah and Murabaha as Forms of Credit Sale","covers":["t9.1"],"overview":"Introduces why Islamic banks must actually engage in buying and selling (unlike conventional banks which simply lend), and positions Musawamah and Murabaha as the two main forms of credit sale (Bai' Mu'ajjal) banks use.","simpleExplanation":"Conventional banks just hand over money and charge interest. Islamic banks, by contrast, must actually buy and sell real goods to earn a legitimate trading profit. Two main styles of credit sale exist: Musawamah, where buyer and seller simply haggle over a final price, and Murabaha, a 'cost-plus' sale where the seller must openly disclose the original cost and add an agreed profit margin.","academicExplanation":"Because Islamic banks cannot simply lend at interest, they must be genuine parties to a sale-and-purchase transaction and bear real business risk, unlike conventional banks which only finance trading businesses without title risk. Credit sale (Bai' Mu'ajjal) takes two principal forms: (1) Musawamah - normal bargained-price sale with deferred payment, and (2) Murabaha - a cost-plus sale where the seller must disclose the original cost and all cost-related information. While Islamic economists generally prefer profit/loss-sharing modes and caution against overreliance on Murabaha, its permissibility is not disputed, and in practice it is the dominant financing technique used by virtually all Islamic banks globally. Critically, the modern banking technique - 'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer' (MPO) - differs from classical Murabaha: it is concluded on the basis of a prior promise to buy or a customer's request, a structure formalized in the AAOIFI Shari'ah Standard on Murabaha.","examExplanation":"Islamic banks must be real trading parties (title + risk), unlike conventional banks (pure financiers). Bai' Mu'ajjal (credit sale) = Musawamah (bargained price, no cost disclosure) + Murabaha (cost-plus, cost disclosed). Modern bank Murabaha = 'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer' (MPO), distinct from classical Murabaha because it starts from a customer's prior promise/request to buy.","keyPoints":["Islamic banks must participate in the actual sale/purchase process and bear business risk, unlike conventional banks which merely finance trading businesses.","Bai' Mu'ajjal (credit/deferred-payment sale) has two important forms used by banks: Musawamah (bargained price) and Murabaha (cost-plus, cost disclosed).","Islamic economists generally prefer PLS modes and caution against excessive use of Murabaha, but its permissibility is beyond doubt and it is used extensively by Islamic banks worldwide.","Modern bank Murabaha = 'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer' (MPO) - distinct from classical Murabaha because it begins with a prior promise/request from the customer to purchase."],"definitions":[{"term":"Bai' Mu'ajjal","definition":"Sale of goods or property against deferred payment (lump sum or instalments)."},{"term":"Murabaha to Purchase Orderer (MPO)","definition":"The modern bank Murabaha arrangement in which a bank, upon a customer's request/promise, purchases an asset from a third party and sells it to the customer on a deferred-payment cost-plus basis."}],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students often conflate classical Murabaha (a trade technique, not originally a financing mode) with modern bank Murabaha/MPO, which is a financing structure built around a customer's prior promise to purchase."],"distinctions":["Musawamah (price bargained, cost not disclosed) vs Murabaha (cost disclosed, profit margin agreed on top)."],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' al Amanah","Musawamah","Bai' Mu'ajjal"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.1","pages":[213,214]}}]},{"id":"t9.2","section":"9.2","title":"Conditions of Valid Bai‘ (Recap for Murabaha)","pages":[214,215],"tier":"core","concepts":["bai","qabd"],"intuition":"A Murabaha is first of all a sale — so every sale condition applies.","simpleExplanation":"Fourteen conditions: qualified parties; free consent; certainty of price, delivery and payment time; seller is owner or agent; transfer of title with ownership risks; subject exists; well-defined and owned; in physical or constructive possession; sale instant and absolute; lawful and valuable; specifically identified; certain delivery; one fixed price; unconditional (unless customary).","academicExplanation":["(1) Parties qualified. (2) Free mutual consent. (3) Certainty of price, date/place of delivery and time of payment. (4) Seller is owner or agent. (5) Mabi‘ alienable; transfer of title means the buyer assumes ownership risks — damage, destruction, pilferage, theft, obsolescence, price/market risk. (6) Subject exists — a bank cannot execute Murabaha on goods already consumed. (7) Well-defined and owned — A cannot sell a car he intends to buy from C. (8) In physical or constructive possession (e.g. a car in a garage under the buyer’s control). (9) Sale instant and absolute — ‘I sell on 1 February’ said on 1 January is void; a promise may be given. (10) Lawful and valuable. (11) Specifically identified — ‘100 bales from that building’ unidentified is void. (12) Certain delivery — not a snatched car. (13) One price stipulated — ‘$50 in one month, $55 in two’ is void unless one is chosen. (14) Unconditional, unless the condition is usual trade practice not prohibited."],"examExplanation":"14 conditions: capacity; consent; certainty (price, delivery, payment); ownership/agency; title with risk; existence; defined and owned; possession; instant; lawful; identified; certain delivery; one price; unconditional.","keyPoints":["Murabaha on consumed goods is invalid.","Unidentified bales: void (loss allocation impossible)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"The snatched car","kind":"textbook","text":"Selling a car that has been snatched, hoping to recover it, is void — delivery must be certain, not contingent on chance (p. 215)."},{"title":"100 bales “from that building”","kind":"textbook","text":"Selling 100 unidentified bales out of those in a building is void, because if the cotton is damaged no one can tell whose loss it is (p. 215)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t6.5","t5.4.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"On 1 January A says to B: ‘I sell my car to you on 1 February.’ This sale is:","options":["Valid","Void — a sale attributed to a future date; only a promise is possible","Salam","Istisna‘a"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 214."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch9-t2"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch9-t2","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"9.2","sectionTitle":"Conditions of Valid Bai'","title":"Conditions of a Valid Bai' (Recap)","covers":["t9.2"],"overview":"Recaps the fourteen general conditions for a valid sale (detailed fully in Chapter 6) that Murabaha, like every sale, must satisfy.","simpleExplanation":"Before getting into Murabaha's special rules, the book reminds us of the basic rules every valid sale must follow: the parties must be legally competent and freely consenting, the price/date/place must be certain, the seller must actually own (or have constructive possession of) the goods, the goods must exist and be lawful and clearly identified, delivery must be certain (not contingent on chance), the price must be fixed once and for all, and the sale can't be conditional or tied to a future date.","academicExplanation":"The chapter recaps fourteen general conditions of a valid Bai' (fully detailed in Chapter 6): (1) contracting parties must be legally qualified; (2) free and mutual consent; (3) certainty of price, delivery date/place, and payment timing; (4) the seller must be owner or agent of the owner; (5) the Mabi' (subject of sale) must be alienable, with title transfer implying assumption of ownership risk (damage, obsolescence, price risk); (6) the subject must exist at the time of sale; (7) the Mabi' must be well-defined and actually owned by the seller (cannot sell what one intends to acquire from a third party); (8) the subject must be in the seller's physical or constructive possession at the time of sale (constructive possession = ownership risk transferred and goods under buyer's control, even without physical delivery); (9) the sale must be instant and absolute, not attributed to a future date or contingent event (though a promise for future sale is permitted, distinct from the sale itself); (10) the subject must be lawful and of value (not pork, wine, etc.); (11) the subject must be specifically identified/distinguished from other units; (12) delivery must be certain, not dependent on chance; (13) a certain price must be stipulated once and for all (no 'if you pay in X, price is Y; if in Z, price is W' ambiguity - though the buyer can be given options to choose one definite price upfront); (14) the sale must be unconditional, unless the condition is an accepted trade custom not expressly prohibited.","examExplanation":"14 conditions of valid Bai' (from Ch.6, recapped here): competent parties; free consent; certainty of price/date/place/payment timing; seller owns or is agent of owner; alienable subject with title-transfer implying risk assumption; subject must exist at time of sale; subject well-defined and actually owned (can't sell what you don't yet own); physical or constructive possession required; sale must be instant/absolute (not future-dated or contingent - though a promise is allowed); subject lawful and of value; subject specifically identified; delivery certain, not by chance; one certain price fixed once and for all; sale must be unconditional (except accepted trade custom).","keyPoints":["Constructive possession (ownership risk transferred, goods under buyer's control, even without physical handover) satisfies the possession requirement - physical possession is not strictly necessary.","A sale attributed to a future date or contingent on a future event is void; a mere promise to sell in future is different and permitted, but rights/liabilities only arise when the sale is actually executed.","A seller cannot sell goods they do not yet own (e.g. A cannot sell B a car A intends to buy from C) - this directly underlies why a bank must actually acquire and own goods before reselling them in Murabaha.","Price must be fixed as a single, certain amount at the time of contract - conditional dual pricing (e.g. different price for different payment timeframes without pre-selection) voids the sale."],"definitions":[{"term":"Mabi'","definition":"The object/subject matter of a sale."},{"term":"Constructive Possession","definition":"A form of possession where the buyer has not taken physical delivery, but ownership risk has transferred and the goods are under the buyer's control, with all rights and liabilities passed to him."}],"conditions":["Parties must be legally qualified and give free, mutual consent.","Price, delivery date/place, and payment timing must all be certain.","The seller must own the object of sale (or be the owner's agent).","The subject of sale must exist, be lawful, of value, and specifically identified.","The subject must be in the seller's actual or constructive possession at the time of sale.","The sale must be instant and absolute - not attributed to a future date nor contingent on an event.","Delivery must be certain, not dependent on chance.","A single certain price must be stipulated once and for all.","The sale must be unconditional, except for conditions that are accepted, non-prohibited trade custom."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Selling What You Don't Own","kind":"textbook","text":"A sells to B a car which he intends to purchase from C (currently owned by C). Since the car is not owned by A at the time of sale, the sale is void - a foundational rule that explains why an Islamic bank must first acquire ownership of an asset before it can sell it to a Murabaha customer."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think a 'promise' to sell in future and a 'sale attributed to a future date' are the same thing - they are legally distinct. A promise creates no rights/liabilities until the actual sale is executed; a future-dated or contingent sale contract itself is void."],"distinctions":["Physical possession vs constructive possession - both satisfy the Shari'ah possession requirement, but constructive possession requires that ownership risk has genuinely passed to the possessor."],"relatedConcepts":["Khiyar al-Shart","Gharar","Bai' al 'Inah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.2","pages":[214,215]}}],"conditions":["Qualified parties","Free consent","Certainty of price, delivery, payment time","Seller owns (or is agent)","Buyer assumes ownership risks","Subject exists","Well-defined and owned","Physical or constructive possession","Instant and absolute","Lawful and valuable","Specifically identified","Certain delivery","One fixed price","Unconditional (unless customary)"]},{"id":"t9.3","section":"9.3","title":"Murabaha — a Bai‘ al Amanah (Trust Sale)","pages":[215,215],"tier":"core","concepts":["murabaha","trust-sales"],"intuition":"Why is Murabaha called a ‘trust’ sale?","simpleExplanation":"Some sales require the seller to declare his cost honestly: Tawliyah (at cost), Wadhi‘ah/Mohatah (at a discount) and Murabaha (at cost plus fixed profit). These are Buyoo‘ al Amanat — fiduciary sales. Musawamah, the most common sale, has no reference to cost. All can be spot or deferred.","academicExplanation":["Forms of Bai‘ effective with express mention of original cost — Tawliyah (resale at original price, no profit or loss), Wadhi‘ah or Mohatah (resale at a discount on cost) and Murabaha (sale with a fixed profit over cost) — require honest declaration of cost and are Buyoo‘ al Amanat (fiduciary sales). Musawamah is an ordinary sale at a mutually agreed price without reference to cost. All can be spot or deferred. In Murabaha the seller informs the buyer of the original cost and they agree a stipulated profit."],"examExplanation":"Trust sales (Buyoo‘ al Amanat): Tawliyah (at cost), Wadhi‘ah/Mohatah (below cost), Murabaha (cost + profit). Musawamah: no cost reference.","keyPoints":["Honest cost declaration is the defining feature."],"definitions":[{"term":"Tawliyah","definition":"Resale at the stated original cost, with no profit or loss."},{"term":"Wadhi‘ah / Mohatah","definition":"Resale at a discount on the original cost."},{"term":"Musawamah","definition":"Ordinary sale at a mutually agreed price with no reference to the seller’s cost."}],"examples":[{"title":"Four sales, one good","kind":"textbook","text":"Cost 100. Resold at 100: Tawliyah. Resold at 90: Wadhi‘ah (Mohatah). Resold at 110 with the cost disclosed: Murabaha. Resold at a bargained 110 with no mention of cost: Musawamah (p. 215)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"In Murabaha the parties bargain on the price.","right":"In Murabaha they bargain on the profit margin over a disclosed cost; bargaining on the price itself, without disclosing cost, is Musawamah (p. 215)."}],"relatedTopics":["t5.8","t9.11"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Resale at a discount from the original cost is called:","options":["Tawliyah","Wadhi‘ah/Mohatah","Murabaha","Musawamah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 215."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch9-t3"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch9-t3","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"9.3-9.4","sectionTitle":"Murabaha - a Bai' al Amanah / Bai' Murabaha in Classical Literature","title":"Murabaha as a Fiduciary Sale (Bai' al Amanah) and Classical Definitions","covers":["t9.3","t9.4"],"overview":"Places Murabaha within the family of 'fiduciary sales' (Buyoo' al Amanat) that require honest cost disclosure, and surveys classical jurists' definitions.","simpleExplanation":"There's a family of sales where the seller must honestly state the original cost: Tawliyah (resell at exactly the same price, no profit), Wadhi'ah/Mohatah (resell at a discount below cost), and Murabaha (resell at cost plus a disclosed, agreed profit). This is different from Musawamah, the ordinary sale where price is just bargained with no cost disclosure at all.","academicExplanation":"Sales that become effective with express mention of the original cost are termed Buyoo' al Amanat (fiduciary sales) in Fiqh literature, comprising Tawliyah (resale at stated original price, no profit/loss), Wadhi'ah/Mohatah (resale at a discount from original cost), and Murabaha (sale with a fixed profit margin over cost) - all can be spot or deferred. Musawamah, by contrast, is an ordinary sale with price mutually agreed without any reference to the seller's cost. Murabaha derives from 'Ribh' (gain/profit/addition) and was practiced in pre-Islamic times; Imam Malik records it in Al-Muwatta. Al-Marghinani (Hanafi) defines it as 'the sale of anything for the price at which it was purchased by the seller and an addition of a fixed sum by way of profit.' Ibn Qudama (Hanbali) defines it as 'the sale at capital cost plus a known profit; the knowledge of capital cost is a precondition in it,' illustrated by the seller stating 'my capital involved in this deal is so much... and I sell it to you for this cost plus a profit of X.' Imam Malik held that Murabaha involves no credit - it is completed by simultaneous exchange of goods and price - and the Maliki school as a whole considers it Naqis (defective/disfavored) due to its demanding conditions, though not prohibited. Imam Shafi'e (in Kitabul Umm) expanded the concept to include credit transactions. Because the buyer must know the original price, expenses and profit, Murabaha is fundamentally a 'contract of trustworthiness.'","examExplanation":"Fiduciary sales (Buyoo' al Amanat) = Tawliyah (same price, no profit), Wadhi'ah/Mohatah (discount from cost), Murabaha (cost + disclosed profit) - all require honest cost disclosure, unlike Musawamah (bargained price, no disclosure). Classical definitions: Al-Marghinani (Hanafi) - price paid + fixed profit addition; Ibn Qudama (Hanbali) - capital cost + known profit, cost knowledge is precondition. Imam Malik: no credit involved, simultaneous exchange; Malikis consider it Naqis (defective) though not prohibited. Imam Shafi'e extended it to credit transactions.","keyPoints":["Fiduciary sales (Buyoo' al Amanat) requiring honest cost disclosure: Tawliyah (resale at cost, no profit), Wadhi'ah/Mohatah (resale at a discount), and Murabaha (resale at cost plus disclosed profit).","Murabaha derives from 'Ribh' (gain/profit) and predates Islam; it is recorded by Imam Malik in Al-Muwatta, the first formally coded Hadith collection.","Al-Marghinani (Hanafi): Murabaha = original purchase price + a fixed profit addition. Ibn Qudama (Hanbali): capital cost + known profit, with cost knowledge as a strict precondition.","Imam Malik viewed classical Murabaha as involving no credit at all (simultaneous exchange); the Maliki school as a whole considers it Naqis (defective/disfavored, though not prohibited) due to its demanding conditions.","Imam Shafi'e expanded Murabaha to include credit transactions, a view later adopted broadly.","Because the buyer must know the exact original price, expenses, and profit margin, Murabaha is described as a 'contract of trustworthiness' (Amanah)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Buyoo' al Amanat","definition":"Fiduciary sales - a class of sale contracts (Tawliyah, Wadhi'ah/Mohatah, Murabaha) that become effective only with an honest, express declaration of the original cost by the seller."},{"term":"Tawliyah","definition":"Resale of goods at the stated original price, with no profit or loss to the seller."},{"term":"Wadhi'ah (Mohatah)","definition":"Resale of goods at a discount from the original cost."}],"conditions":["In Murabaha, the seller must disclose the true original cost; the buyer's knowledge of this cost is a strict precondition."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Tawliyah, Wadhi'ah and Murabaha are all fiduciary/cost-disclosed sales but differ in the seller's declared markup: Tawliyah = no markup, Wadhi'ah = negative markup (discount), Murabaha = positive markup (profit)."],"distinctions":["Fiduciary sales (Tawliyah, Wadhi'ah, Murabaha - cost disclosed) vs Musawamah (price bargained, cost never disclosed)."],"relatedConcepts":["Musawamah","Amanah","Riba"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.3-9.4","pages":[215,216]}}],"table":{"caption":"Sales classified by reference to cost","head":["Sale","Price basis","Cost disclosed?"],"rows":[["Tawliyah","At original cost","Yes"],["Wadhi‘ah / Mohatah","At a discount on cost","Yes"],["Murabaha","Cost plus agreed profit","Yes"],["Musawamah","Freely bargained price","No"]]}},{"id":"t9.4","section":"9.4","title":"Bai‘ Murabaha in Classical Literature","pages":[215,216],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["murabaha"],"intuition":"Was Murabaha a financing technique in classical Fiqh? No — it was a type of trade.","simpleExplanation":"Murabaha (from Ribh — profit) was practised before Islam and recorded by Imam Malik. Al-Marghinani: sale at the purchase price plus a fixed profit. Ibn Qudama: sale at capital cost plus known profit — lawful without controversy. Malik saw it as a spot exchange and Malikis disliked it for its many conditions; Shafi‘i extended it to credit. It is a contract of trustworthiness.","academicExplanation":["Murabaha derives from Ribh (gain, profit). Practised pre-Islam; mentioned by Imam Malik in Al-Mu’watta. Al-Marghinani: “sale of anything for the price at which it was purchased by the seller and an addition of a fixed sum by way of profit.” Ibn Qudama: sale at capital cost plus known profit, knowledge of cost a precondition — “I sell for cost 100 plus profit 10” — lawful without controversy. Imam Malik: completed by exchanging goods and price with agreed margin then and there — no credit. Malikis disliked it (difficult conditions) but did not prohibit it. Imam Shafi‘i (Kitabul Umm) extended it to credit. Buyer must know the original price, expenses and profit; Murabaha is a contract of trustworthiness."],"examExplanation":"Classical Murabaha: cost + known profit (Marghinani, Ibn Qudama); Malik — spot; Shafi‘i — extended to credit; contract of trust.","keyPoints":["Malikis considered it difficult (many conditions)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Ribh","definition":"Gain or profit — the root of the word Murabaha."}],"examples":[{"title":"Ibn Qudama’s formula","kind":"textbook","text":"“I sell to you at my cost of 100 plus a profit of 10.” Knowing the cost is the precondition, and such a sale is lawful without controversy (pp. 215–216)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"All classical schools treated Murabaha as a credit-financing technique.","right":"For Imam Malik it was a spot exchange with no credit; Imam Shafi‘i extended it to credit. Malikis disliked it for its many conditions but did not forbid it (p. 216)."}],"relatedTopics":["t9.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Who extended the concept of Murabaha to credit transactions?","options":["Imam Malik","Imam Shafi‘i","Ibn Khaldun","Abu Yusuf"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 216."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t9.5","section":"9.5","title":"The Need for Murabaha","pages":[216,217],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["murabaha"],"intuition":"What was Murabaha originally for?","simpleExplanation":"Classically, Murabaha protected inexperienced buyers from cunning traders: a buyer relied on an honest Murabaha dealer’s disclosed cost. Imam Ahmad preferred Musawamah because Murabaha’s trust invites false statements. Modern bank Murabaha is on deferred payment; cost disclosure matters less since the customer often locates the goods.","academicExplanation":["Al-Marghinani: Murabaha (and Tawliyah) protects innocent consumers lacking trading skill from tricks — the buyer pays an honest dealer an agreed addition over cost. Imam Ahmad: Musawamah is easier because Murabaha implies trust and detailed description; selfishness may lead to false statements or mistakes amounting to fraud. A Ja‘fari jurist expressed similar views. Modern Murabaha by banks is on deferred payment, creating a receivable; disclosure of cost is necessary but less contentious as the customer is involved in locating and purchasing goods."],"examExplanation":"Classical purpose: protect unskilled buyers via honest cost disclosure. Imam Ahmad preferred Musawamah. Modern: deferred-payment receivable; customer involved in sourcing.","keyPoints":["Imam Ahmad feared false cost statements."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Why Imam Ahmad preferred Musawamah","kind":"textbook","text":"Murabaha relies on trust and detailed description; the seller may be tempted to misstate cost or err, turning it into fraud, so ordinary sale is easier and safer (p. 216)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t9.11"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to Al-Marghinani, the main purpose of Murabaha was to:","options":["Provide bank financing","Protect innocent purchasers from exploitation by cunning traders","Avoid Zakat","Speculate on prices"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 216."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch9-t4"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch9-t4","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"9.5","sectionTitle":"The Need for Murabaha","title":"The Original Purpose of Murabaha: Protecting the Unskilled Buyer","covers":["t9.5"],"overview":"Explains the classical rationale for Murabaha - protecting inexperienced buyers from cunning traders - and notes classical jurists' reservations about relying on it too heavily.","simpleExplanation":"Classical jurists saw Murabaha as a tool mainly for people who didn't know how to negotiate well in the market - by going to a trusted, honest Murabaha dealer and paying a known markup, an inexperienced buyer avoided being cheated. Some jurists, like Imam Ahmad, actually preferred ordinary bargained sale (Musawamah) over Murabaha, because Murabaha depends heavily on the seller's honesty and creates temptation to lie about the true cost.","academicExplanation":"Al-Marghinani suggests Murabaha's (and Tawliyah's) purpose is protecting innocent consumers who lack trading expertise from cunning traders' tricks - such a buyer relies on a Murabaha dealer known for honesty rather than bargaining directly (Musawamah). Imam Ahmad explicitly preferred Musawamah over Murabaha, reasoning that Murabaha requires reposing trust in the seller and detailed disclosure, creating a real risk that 'selfishness may overcome the seller, persuading him to give a false statement.' A Jafari jurist expressed similar caution. The author notes that modern Murabaha is conducted mainly by banks on a deferred-payment basis, where a receivable becomes the customer's liability; disclosure of the bank's cost price is a necessary condition but rarely a live issue in practice, since the customer is typically involved in locating and negotiating the goods himself. The classical protective rationale, combined with Murabaha's demanding conditions, led Maliki jurists to regard it as Naqis (defective) - meaning its permissibility is not as unconditional as an ordinary sale. Since no explicit prohibition exists for experienced traders using it, contemporary jurists have accepted Murabaha as a mode of business/financing subject to fulfilling its juristic conditions.","examExplanation":"Classical rationale: Murabaha protects inexperienced/unskilled buyers from exploitation by relying on an honest dealer's cost disclosure rather than personal bargaining skill. Imam Ahmad preferred Musawamah over Murabaha because of the honesty/trust risk in cost disclosure. Maliki view: Murabaha is Naqis (defective) - permissible but not unconditionally so, given its demanding conditions. Contemporary jurists accept it as a financing mode since no explicit prohibition bars its use by experienced parties.","keyPoints":["Classical purpose of Murabaha: protecting unskilled/inexperienced buyers from exploitation, by trusting a known-honest dealer's cost disclosure instead of bargaining directly.","Imam Ahmad preferred ordinary sale (Musawamah) over Murabaha, citing the honesty/trust risk inherent in requiring the seller to accurately disclose cost.","Maliki jurists consider Murabaha Naqis (defective/disfavored) due to the difficulty of fulfilling all its conditions - though it is not prohibited.","Because no prohibition exists specifically for experienced traders or businesses using Murabaha, contemporary jurists have accepted it as a financing mode subject to meeting its juristic conditions."],"definitions":[{"term":"Naqis","definition":"Defective (in the Maliki juristic sense as applied to Murabaha) - meaning its permissibility is conditional and less absolute than an ordinary sale, due to the difficulty of fully satisfying its requirements."}],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think Murabaha's protective rationale means it cannot be used by sophisticated commercial clients or banks. The text clarifies there is no explicit prohibition for experienced parties - it is simply that the classical purpose was consumer protection."],"distinctions":[],"relatedConcepts":["Musawamah","Bai' al Amanah"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.5","pages":[216,217]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Classical purpose","b":"Modern use","text":"Classically Murabaha protected unskilled buyers through honest cost disclosure; in banking it creates a deferred receivable and the customer usually helps locate the goods, so cost disclosure is less of an issue (pp. 216–217)."}]},{"id":"t9.6","section":"9.6","title":"Specific Conditions of Murabaha","pages":[217,219],"tier":"core","concepts":["murabaha"],"intuition":"What can be sold by Murabaha, and what can be included in ‘cost’?","simpleExplanation":"Goods must be real (intangibles like rights are fine) — not currencies, gold/silver (Sarf) or debt documents. The seller must state the true cost plus customary value-adding expenses (“this has cost me…”, not “I bought at…”), disclose defects, benefits and payment terms (including if he bought on credit), and pass on supplier rebates. The margin is agreed and cannot rise later. The cost must be known in identical units. False statements give the buyer options.","academicExplanation":["(1) Goods real, not necessarily tangible — rights and royalties may be traded. (2) Currencies/monetary units (Sarf) cannot be sold by Murabaha. (3) Credit documents (debt) cannot — debt sale only via Hawalah, and profit on debt is Riba. (4) The seller states the original price and additional expenses that enhance value and are customarily added (transport, processing, packing), saying “this has cost me so much”, not “I purchased at this rate”. Hanafis include all expenses modifying the object (tailoring, dyeing) or incurred for it (transport, storage, commission); Malikis: add direct expenses and outsourced services, but not services the seller could have provided himself (packing, sales commission) — Shafi‘is agree; Hanbalis: all expenses with mutual consent if the breakdown is disclosed.","(5) Disclose all aspects — defects, additional benefits, mode of payment to the supplier; all schools agree the buyer must be told if the seller bought on credit; deliberate inflation of cost violates Murabaha; supplier rebates received even after the sale benefit the client (AAOIFI). (6) The margin is mutually agreed; the deferred price cannot be increased (only reduced by rebates). (7) No Majhul price: cost must be in identical units (dirhams, dinars or specific weights). (8) On incorrect cost statements: Malik — buyer may rescind unless the difference is returned; Hanafis — unqualified option to rescind; Hanbalis — binding after the difference is returned; Shafi‘is — two versions. (9) The buyer has an option even unstipulated for fraud; if discovered after use/destruction, Abu Hanifa and Muhammad: no deduction; Abu Yusuf and Ibn Abi Laila: deduction allowed.","Medieval Murabaha was trade, not financing; contemporary jurists accept it as a mode with limits of transparency and justice; Malikis considered it Naqis (defective), so its permissibility is not as absolute as ordinary sale. Since there is no prohibition for experienced traders, it can be an alternative to interest where Shari’ah boards allow (the CII lists uses in fixed investment, working capital, agriculture, commerce, mining and consumer durables)."],"examExplanation":"Eligible: real goods incl. intangibles; not currency/gold/silver or debt. Disclose true cost + customary expenses (‘cost me’), credit purchase, defects; pass rebates; margin fixed; cost in identical units; false statement → buyer’s option. School differences on expenses.","keyPoints":["Rights and royalties can be Murabaha subjects.","Buyer must be told if the seller bought on credit.","Supplier rebate after sale goes to the client.","Malikis call Murabaha Naqis (defective).","Malikis treat Murabaha as Naqis (defective): its permissibility is less absolute than ordinary sale.","The CII (1980) listed Murabaha uses in fixed investment, working capital, agriculture, commerce, mining and consumer durables."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"“It cost me” vs “I bought it at”","kind":"textbook","text":"If transport, processing or packing is added to cost, the seller must say “this has cost me so much”, not “I purchased it at this rate” — the latter would be false (p. 217)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A Murabaha seller need not tell the buyer that he himself bought on credit.","right":"All schools agree the buyer must be told if the original price was on credit, since credit prices are usually higher (p. 218)."}],"relatedTopics":["t9.3","t4.2.8","t9.9"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which of these can NOT be the subject of Murabaha?","options":["Machinery","Royalty rights","US Dollars","Raw materials"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"Currencies are subject to Sarf rules (p. 217)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch9-t5"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch9-t5","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"9.6","sectionTitle":"Specific Conditions of Murabaha","title":"Specific Conditions of Murabaha: Goods, Cost Disclosure and Price Certainty","covers":["t9.6","t9.6.1"],"overview":"Details the additional conditions specific to Murabaha beyond the general sale conditions: eligible goods, what expenses can be added to cost, mandatory disclosure, and the buyer's remedies if misled.","simpleExplanation":"On top of the general sale rules, Murabaha has extra requirements: the goods must be real (though not necessarily physical - rights can qualify) but cannot be currency, gold/silver, or debt instruments. The seller must truthfully state the original cost and can add certain genuine expenses (like transport or processing) but not fake ones, and must disclose if the original purchase was itself on credit. The profit margin, once agreed, can't be changed later (except for a supplier rebate). If the seller lies about the cost, different schools give the buyer different remedies - from an unconditional right to cancel, to requiring the seller just to refund the difference.","academicExplanation":"Specific conditions: (1) goods must be real but not necessarily tangible (rights/royalties qualify); (2) currency/monetary units (subject to Bai' al Sarf) cannot be sold via Murabaha since they require simultaneous exchange; (3) debt instruments/credit documents cannot be the subject of Murabaha (debt cannot be sold except via Hawalah rules, and profit on debt would be Riba); (4) the seller must truthfully state the original price and additional expenses, saying 'this article has cost me so much' (not 'I purchased this at such a rate', which could misrepresent), with schools differing on what expenses can be added to cost - Hanafis allow expenses that modify the object (tailoring, dyeing) plus incidental ones (transport, storage, commission); Malikis divide expenses into three tiers (directly cost-affecting = addable; post-profit-calculation services not self-provided = addable; services the seller could have but didn't provide = not addable); Shafi'es align with the Maliki final category; Hanbalis pragmatically allow all expenses by mutual consent if disclosed; (5) the seller must disclose all commodity aspects, defects, benefits, and critically whether the original purchase was itself on credit (since credit prices are typically higher) - a deliberately inflated original price violates Murabaha, and any rebate later received on the goods must be passed to the buyer; (6) the profit margin is mutually agreed and, once fixed, cannot be increased (except for supplier rebates, which reduce, not increase, the price); (7) the price basis cannot be Majhul (unspecified/uncertain) - it must be expressed in identical, specified units; (8) if the seller misstates the original cost, remedies vary by school: per Imam Malik, buyer may rescind unless seller refunds the difference (making the sale binding upon refund); Hanafis give an unqualified rescission option; Hanbalis treat the sale as binding once the difference is refunded; Shafi'es split between the two views; (9) the purchaser has an inherent right of Khiyar (option) even without explicit stipulation if defrauded by false statements about the article, price, expenses, or if the seller concealed that the original purchase was itself on deferred payment - though if the buyer has already used or the goods have been destroyed, Abu Hanifa and Muhammad deny any price deduction (Abu Yusuf and Ibn-abi-Laila allow deduction even then).","examExplanation":"Murabaha-specific conditions: goods must be real (tangibles or intangible rights, but NOT currency/gold-silver/debt instruments); seller must truthfully disclose cost + genuine expenses (schools differ on what expenses count - Hanafi broadest, Maliki/Shafi'e narrower, Hanbali most permissive with disclosure); must disclose if original purchase was itself on credit; profit margin fixed once agreed (only reducible via rebate, never increasable); price must not be Majhul (unspecified). If seller misstates cost: Maliki - buyer can rescind unless refunded; Hanafi - unqualified rescission right; Hanbali - binding once refunded; Shafi'e - split view. Buyer's Khiyar applies if defrauded, but is generally lost once goods are consumed/destroyed (per Abu Hanifa/Muhammad; Abu Yusuf/Ibn-abi-Laila disagree).","keyPoints":["Murabaha goods must be real (tangible or intangible, e.g. rights/royalties) but currency (Bai' al Sarf) and debt instruments/receivables cannot be sold via Murabaha.","The seller must state actual cost (\"this article has cost me so much\", not a potentially misleading \"I purchased this at such a rate\") and disclose whether the original purchase was itself on credit.","Schools differ on which expenses can be added to the cost basis - Hanafis are broadest (object-modifying + incidental expenses like transport/storage/commission); Malikis/Shafi'es exclude services the seller could have but didn't provide; Hanbalis allow all expenses if disclosed and mutually agreed.","The agreed profit margin, once fixed, cannot later be increased - it can only be reduced by a supplier rebate, which must be passed to the buyer.","The price basis must not be Majhul (unspecified/uncertain) and must use identical, defined units.","Remedies for the buyer if the seller misstates the original cost vary significantly by school - ranging from an unqualified right to rescind (Hanafi) to a binding sale once the price difference is refunded (Hanbali)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Majhul","definition":"Unspecified or unknown - a Majhul price or subject matter introduces impermissible uncertainty (Gharar) and cannot form the valid basis of a Murabaha sale."}],"conditions":["Currency/monetary units and debt instruments/receivables cannot be sold through Murabaha.","The seller must truthfully disclose the original cost, all genuine additional expenses, and whether the original purchase was itself on a credit basis.","A deliberately inflated original purchase price violates the Murabaha contract.","The agreed profit margin cannot be increased once fixed; it can only be reduced via a supplier rebate, which must be passed to the buyer.","The cost/price basis must be expressed in identical, specified units and must not be Majhul (unspecified)."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume all schools give an automatic right to cancel if the seller misstates the cost. In fact, remedies vary: Hanafis give an unqualified rescission right, while Hanbalis (and one Shafi'e view) treat the sale as binding once the price difference is refunded."],"distinctions":["Cost-related expenses that can be added to the Murabaha price basis (genuinely incurred, disclosed) vs services the seller could have but chose not to provide (generally not addable per Maliki/Shafi'e view)."],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' al Sarf","Hawalah","Khiyar al 'Aib"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6","pages":[217,218,219]}}],"table":{"caption":"Which expenses may be added to Murabaha cost (by school)","head":["School","Position"],"rows":[["Hanafi","All expenses modifying the object or incurred for its sake (tailoring, dyeing, transport, storage, commission)"],["Maliki","Direct expenses and outsourced services — yes; services the seller could provide himself (packing, sales commission) — no"],["Shafi‘i","Like Malikis — seller’s own services cannot be added"],["Hanbali","All expenses with mutual consent if the breakdown is disclosed"]]}},{"id":"t9.6.1","section":"9.6.1","title":"Bai‘ Murabaha and Credit Sale (Murabaha–Mu’ajjal)","pages":[219,220],"tier":"core","concepts":["murabaha","time-value"],"intuition":"Murabaha becomes a financing tool only when payment is deferred. Why is that not Riba?","simpleExplanation":"Credit sale (Bai‘ Mu’ajjal) is lawful if the period is fixed (Hidaya: the Prophet bought on credit pledging his breastplate). Combined with Murabaha it is cost plus profit with deferred payment. A credit price above cash price is not Riba because money is exchanged for goods (different kinds), and the parties bear price risk — consistent with Al-Ghunm bil Ghurm. One price must be fixed. Not allowed for gold, silver, currencies or receivables; allowed for screened shares.","academicExplanation":["Murabaha is important as an alternative to interest only on a deferred basis. Legality of postponement is a general feature of lawful sale (Bai‘ Mu’ajjal, lump sum or instalments; Majallah: Bai‘ bil Nasiah / bi al Ta’jil wa al Taqsit). Hidaya: valid for ready money or future payment if the period is fixed — uncertainty invites disputes. The seller may postpone payment for the buyer’s convenience, even forgo it. Hanafis, Shafi‘is and Hanbalis permit different cash and credit prices if one is settled; Malik forbade it but some Malikis allow; contemporary jurists almost unanimous. Rationale: in loans, like is exchanged for like ($100 for $100) so excess is Riba; in credit sale money is exchanged for goods, so price differences are not Riba. Also, interest is payable in any case, while in sale prices may move — if prices rise the buyer gains, if they fall the seller gains — consistent with Al-Ghunm bil Ghurm. The sale must be finalised at one price. Deferred Murabaha is not allowed for gold, silver or currencies (Sarf) nor receivables (profit on debt is Riba); Murabaha of screened shares is allowed."],"examExplanation":"Murabaha–Mu’ajjal = cost + profit with fixed deferred payment. Credit > cash price OK (goods for money, not like for like; price risk borne; Al-Ghunm bil Ghurm); one price fixed. Not for Sarf items or debt; OK for screened shares.","keyPoints":["Period of payment must be fixed.","Prices may move after sale — risk on both sides.","Murabaha of eligible shares (after screening) is allowed; of gold, silver, currency or debt is not."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Garment on credit, breastplate as pledge","kind":"textbook","text":"Hidaya cites the Prophet (pbuh) buying a garment from a Jew on credit for a fixed date and pledging his breastplate — credit sale is valid if the period is fixed (p. 219)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A credit sale price above cash price is just like loan interest.","right":"In a loan the like is exchanged for the like, so any excess is Riba; in credit sale money is exchanged for goods, prices may move either way and profit goes with loss (Al-Ghunm bil Ghurm) (p. 220)."}],"relatedTopics":["t6.5.3","t4.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why is the difference between purchase and credit sale price not Riba, according to Section 9.6.1?","options":["Because banks set it","Because money is exchanged for goods (not like for like) and price risk exists, consistent with Al-Ghunm bil Ghurm","Because it is small","Because it is disclosed"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 220."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch9-t6"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch9-t6","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"9.6.1","sectionTitle":"Bai' Murabaha and Credit Sale (Murabaha-Mu'ajjal)","title":"Murabaha-Mu'ajjal: Justifying the Credit-Cash Price Differential","covers":["t9.6.1"],"overview":"Explains the Shari'ah basis for postponed payment in Murabaha and why a higher deferred price is not Riba, plus the residual restriction on Murabaha for currencies/gold-silver/debt.","simpleExplanation":"Islamic law allows a sale to be paid for later instead of now, as long as the payment date is fixed - this is what makes 'Murabaha-Mu'ajjal' (cost-plus with deferred payment) meaningful as a bank financing tool. It's not the same as charging interest on a loan, because in a loan you're trading money for the same amount of money later (any excess would be Riba), but in a credit sale you're trading money for goods, and prices for goods can legitimately differ based on timing - if the buyer benefits by locking in today's price and paying later at a cheaper effective cost, that's fine; if the seller benefits by getting a higher price for waiting, that's also fine, and this fits the Fiqh principle 'Al-Ghunm bil Ghurm' (profit goes with loss/risk).","academicExplanation":"The legality of postponed payment is a general feature of lawful sales (Bai' Mu'ajjal), provided the payment period is fixed - supported by a Hadith describing the Prophet (pbuh) purchasing a garment from a Jew on a fixed future payment date, pledging his iron breast-coat. Uncertainty in repayment timing risks disputes and is therefore disallowed; deferral itself is at the seller's discretion since the price becomes his sole right upon execution, which he may postpone or even forgo. Most schools (Hanafi, Shafi'e, Hanbali, and per some Malikis, though Imam Malik himself forbade it) permit a different price for cash vs credit sales, provided one price is settled definitively at contract finalization; contemporary jurists are nearly unanimous on this point. The rationale: in a loan (where excess is Riba), the exchange is between a commodity and its like (e.g. $100 for $100) - any increase is impermissible Riba. In a credit sale, the exchange is between two different things: money for goods, then (in resale) goods for money - so a price differential is not Riba. Additionally, interest on a loan is payable regardless of circumstance, but in a sale contract, prices are inherently variable - if prices rise, the deferred-payment buyer benefits (locked in a cheaper price); if prices fall, the seller benefits (sold at a relatively higher price) - consistent with the Fiqhi principle 'Al-Ghunm bil Ghurm' (profit accompanies liability/risk). However, the sale contract must ultimately be finalized at one single price so the exact liability is clear. Murabaha on a deferred-payment basis remains barred for gold, silver, or currencies (subject to Bai' al Sarf) and for receivables/debt instruments (since profit over debt principal is Riba); however, Murabaha of Shari'ah-screened joint stock company shares is permitted.","examExplanation":"Deferred payment (Bai' Mu'ajjal) is valid if the payment date is fixed (uncertainty in timing is disallowed). Most schools permit a cash-price/credit-price differential (settled as ONE price at contract time) - this is not Riba because a sale exchanges money for goods (different things), unlike a loan which exchanges money for money (same thing, so any excess is Riba). Fits 'Al-Ghunm bil Ghurm' (profit goes with risk/liability). Still barred: Murabaha of gold/silver/currency (Bai' al Sarf) and of debt/receivables (profit on debt = Riba); Murabaha of Shari'ah-compliant shares is allowed.","keyPoints":["Postponed payment in a sale is valid Shari'ah practice provided the payment date/period is definitively fixed - uncertainty in timing risks dispute and is disallowed.","Most schools permit a cash price different from a credit price, as long as ONE definite price is settled at the time the contract is finalized - not a floating or multi-tier price.","The key distinction from Riba: a loan exchanges money for money of the same kind (any excess = Riba); a credit sale exchanges money for goods (different kinds), so a price differential reflects legitimate trade, not interest.","The Fiqhi principle 'Al-Ghunm bil Ghurm' (profit accompanies risk/liability) justifies the deferred-price premium, since the seller/buyer's gain or loss depends on genuine subsequent price movement risk.","Murabaha remains barred for gold, silver and currency (Bai' al Sarf rules require simultaneous exchange) and for debt/receivables (profit on debt is Riba); Murabaha of Shari'ah-screened company shares is permitted."],"definitions":[{"term":"Al-Ghunm bil Ghurm","definition":"A Fiqhi principle meaning 'profit goes with liability/risk' - legitimate profit must be earned in conjunction with bearing genuine risk, used to justify why a credit-sale price premium is not equivalent to interest."}],"conditions":["The period of deferred payment must be fixed, not left uncertain.","Only one definite price may be settled at the time the sale contract is finalized, even if a cash and a credit price were initially discussed as options.","Murabaha on a deferred-payment basis is not permitted for gold, silver, or currency, nor for receivables/debt instruments."],"principles":["Al-Ghunm bil Ghurm: profit is justified when it accompanies genuine risk/liability, distinguishing a legitimate credit-sale price premium from interest."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"The Prophet's Deferred-Payment Purchase","kind":"textbook","text":"The Hadith cited in Hidaya describes the Prophet (pbuh) purchasing a garment from a Jew and promising to pay the price at a fixed future date, pledging his iron breast-coat as security - cited as textual support for the permissibility of deferred-payment sale provided the period is fixed."}],"commonConfusions":["A common student error: assuming any price difference between cash and credit terms is disguised Riba. The book explains this is not so, provided the sale settles on one definite price at contract time - the differential reflects a legitimate trade-based price, not interest on a loan of money."],"distinctions":["A loan (money-for-money-of-same-kind; any excess is Riba) vs a credit sale (money-for-goods; a price differential is legitimate trade profit, not Riba)."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Bai' al Sarf","Hawalah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6.1","pages":[219,220]}}]},{"id":"t9.7","section":"9.7","title":"Possible Structures of Murabaha","pages":[220,222],"tier":"core","concepts":["murabaha","wakalah"],"intuition":"Who actually buys the goods — the bank, a third-party agent, or the client as agent? Each option shifts risk and Shari’ah exposure.","simpleExplanation":"Banks may set up trading subsidiaries. Three structures: (1) direct trading by bank management — most ideal for Shari’ah but managerially hard (suits bulk/trademarked goods, e.g. fertiliser via dealers); (2) purchase through a third-party agent — best for possession and risk (AAOIFI prefers it; client as agent only in dire need); package of MoU/promise, sale deed and promissory note; (3) client as agent — safest for the bank’s commodity risk but most likely to become a back door to interest; goods must come into the bank’s ownership and risk.","academicExplanation":["Trading requires expertise banks may lack; banks may establish special purpose trading/leasing companies with specialised staff, or trade via the client or a third-party agent.","9.7.1 Direct trading by bank management: most ideal for Murabaha essentials but may cause managerial problems and corruption without internal controls; suitable for high-value or trademarked goods bought in bulk for inventory (e.g. an agricultural subsidiary buying fertiliser/pesticides and selling via dealers) — prices closer to cash market.","9.7.2 Through a third party/agent: most likely to accomplish possession and commercial risk by the bank between purchase and sale; the bank bears risk until handover; the customer cannot guarantee transport risk (bank can mitigate by taking delivery at its godowns). AAOIFI: third-party agent is better; customer as agent only in dire need. Package: (i) MoU/agreement to sell — request and promise, possibly margin/reference rate, provided a definite price is fixed at execution; (ii) sale deed when goods are in the bank’s ownership and risk; (iii) promissory note (better after sale, since it says ‘against value received'); plus security, quality description, defects and default clauses.","9.7.3 Client as agent: safest for the bank against commodity risk but more likely to be a back door to interest; goods must come into the bank’s ownership and risk; the customer should disclose agency status to the supplier. If the bank only pays for goods the client already bought, it is a loan and profit is interest."],"examExplanation":"Structures: direct (ideal, managerially hard), third-party agent (best for risk/possession; AAOIFI preferred), client as agent (most used; back-door risk; client must disclose agency; bank must own and bear risk). Package: MoU/promise, sale deed, promissory note.","keyPoints":["AAOIFI: client as agent only in dire need.","Bank paying for goods already bought by client = loan with interest.","Promissory note preferably after sale."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Fertiliser through a subsidiary","kind":"textbook","text":"A bank’s agricultural subsidiary buys fertiliser in bulk, holds it as inventory and sells it to farmers through dealers on Murabaha. This is direct trading, the most ideal structure, with prices close to the cash market (pp. 220–222)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"If the bank simply pays the supplier for goods the client already received, it is Murabaha.","right":"Without the bank owning the goods, payment on the client’s behalf is a loan and any profit is interest (p. 222)."}],"relatedTopics":["t9.8","t13.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which structure does the AAOIFI standard consider better, allowing the client as agent only in dire need?","options":["Client as agent","Purchase through a third-party agent","No agent at all","Bank lending cash"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 221 (footnote)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch9-t7"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch9-t7","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"9.7","sectionTitle":"Possible Structures of Murabaha","title":"Possible Structures of Murabaha: Direct Trading, Third-Party Agent, or Client as Agent","covers":["t9.7"],"overview":"Compares the three operational structures Islamic banks can use to source Murabaha goods: direct bank trading, purchase via a third-party agent, or purchase via the client acting as the bank's agent.","simpleExplanation":"A bank needs to actually own goods before selling them on Murabaha, so it needs a way to buy them first. It has three choices: buy the goods itself directly (best in theory but risks turning bankers into retailers), buy through an independent third-party agent (a cleaner separation of risk), or appoint the customer itself as the bank's purchasing agent (the most common in practice, but riskiest for accidentally becoming a disguised loan if not handled carefully).","academicExplanation":"Because banks generally lack in-house trading expertise, three structuring options exist. (1) Direct trading by bank management: the most ideal option for fulfilling Murabaha essentials, but risks managerial problems and corruption from bankers engaging in retail trade; practically limited to bulk purchase of high-value or trademarked assets (e.g. a bank subsidiary purchasing fertilizer/pesticides for onward Murabaha sale to farmers via dealers), with the added benefit that sale prices can track cash market prices closely. (2) Bank purchases through a third-party agent: most likely to satisfy the Shari'ah requirement that the bank takes genuine possession and commercial risk between purchase from supplier and sale to client; AAOIFI recommends this option and advises against appointing the customer as agent except in cases of dire need. The bank remains liable for anything going wrong until handover to the client (mitigated by requiring delivery to the bank's own godowns); the package typically comprises an MoU/agreement to sell (client's request and promise to purchase, may include profit margin/reference rate), the sale deed (executed when the commodity is in the bank's ownership/risk), and a promissory note from the client. (3) Murabaha through the client as agent: the safest structure for banks to avoid commodity-based risk, but most vulnerable to becoming a disguised interest loan (a 'back door to interest') if not carefully structured - the foremost requirement is that goods genuinely come under the bank's ownership and risk, and the customer must disclose his agency status to the supplier. If the bank merely reimburses a client who directly purchased and received goods without the bank ever owning them, this is in substance a loan with interest disguised as profit. Because Islamic banks predominantly use structure (3) in practice, the chapter examines it in detail in the following sections.","examExplanation":"Three Murabaha sourcing structures: (1) Direct bank trading - most Shari'ah-ideal, but operationally risky/impractical at scale, limited to bulk/high-value goods; (2) Third-party agent purchase - AAOIFI-preferred, cleanly separates the bank's ownership/risk period; (3) Client-as-agent - most common in practice, cheapest for the bank operationally, but highest Shari'ah risk of becoming a disguised loan (back door to interest) if the bank doesn't genuinely take ownership and risk before reselling.","keyPoints":["Direct trading by bank management is the most Shari'ah-ideal option but risks operational/corruption problems from involving bankers in retail trade; practically limited to bulk/high-value/trademarked goods.","AAOIFI recommends purchase through an independent third-party agent over appointing the customer as agent, recommending the latter only in cases of dire need.","In the third-party-agent structure, the bank bears risk/liability for the goods from purchase until handover to the client - a risk that can be mitigated by requiring delivery to the bank's own godowns.","The client-as-agent structure is the most commonly used by Islamic banks in practice, but is also the most vulnerable to becoming Bai' al 'Inah / a disguised interest loan if the bank does not genuinely take ownership and bear risk before reselling.","If a bank merely reimburses a client who has already purchased and received goods directly (without the bank ever taking ownership/risk), this is, in substance, an interest-bearing loan disguised as a Murabaha profit."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["In any Murabaha structure, the bank must genuinely take ownership and bear the risk of the goods for the period between acquisition from the supplier and sale to the client."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think appointing the client as agent to purchase automatically makes the structure non-compliant. It is permissible, but requires extra care - the client must clearly act in an agency capacity (disclosed to the supplier), and the bank must genuinely take ownership/risk before reselling to avoid it becoming a disguised loan."],"distinctions":["Third-party agent purchase (AAOIFI-preferred, cleaner risk separation) vs client-as-agent purchase (most common in practice, but requires the most careful Shari'ah safeguards)."],"relatedConcepts":["Wakalah","Bai' al 'Inah","Murabaha to Purchase Orderer"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.7","pages":[220,221,222]}}],"subsections":[{"number":"9.7.1","title":"Direct Trading by Bank Management","page":221,"points":["Ideal but managerially hard","Bulk/trademarked goods"]},{"number":"9.7.2","title":"Bank Purchases Through a Third Party/Agent","page":221,"points":["Best for risk and possession","AAOIFI preference","MoU, sale deed, promissory note"]},{"number":"9.7.3","title":"Murabaha Through the Client as Agent","page":222,"points":["Safest for bank’s risk","Back door to interest if misused"]}],"table":{"caption":"Three Murabaha structures compared (pp. 220–222)","head":["Structure","Shari’ah strength","Practical issue"],"rows":[["Direct trading by the bank","Most ideal","Managerial burden, risk of corruption; suits bulk/trademarked goods"],["Purchase through a third-party agent","Best for possession and risk; AAOIFI prefers it","Bank bears transit risk until delivery"],["Client as the bank’s agent","Safest for the bank’s commodity risk","Most likely to become a back door to interest"]]}},{"id":"t9.8","section":"9.8","title":"Murabaha to Purchase Orderer (MPO)","pages":[222,224],"tier":"core","concepts":["mpo","murabaha"],"intuition":"Why don’t Islamic banks keep warehouses of goods? MPO lets them buy only what a client orders.","simpleExplanation":"In MPO the bank, on a client’s request, buys an asset from a third party and sells it to the client on deferred payment. It is used because banks don’t hold inventories, can’t stock every item, clients need specific goods, and regulators restrict trading. The client may nominate the supplier, but must not have already bought or committed to buy (else ‘Inah). Payment should go directly to the supplier; avoid double agency. Murabaha cannot replace a running finance facility.","academicExplanation":["MPO (Murabaha lil ‘amri bil Shira / Murabaha li Wa‘da bi Shira): upon a customer’s request, the bank purchases an asset from a third party and sells it on deferred payment — used by almost all Islamic banks and the IDB for trade finance. Needs: banks do not keep inventories (costs); the list of goods is long; clients need specific quality; regulators do not allow trading as core business (liquidity, asset/market risk, cartels).","AAOIFI: IFIs may purchase only in response to customers’ applications; the wish is not a promise unless made in due form (can be in the requisition). The customer may indicate the supplier, but the bank must ensure the supplier is a third party and the client has not already purchased or firmly committed; otherwise ‘Inah. A performance bond may be taken. The bank may not enter Musharakah with a promise to buy the other’s share by Murabaha; a promise to buy at market or agreed price by separate contract is allowed.","IFIs may also build inventories; buying, possessing and owning with risk and reward is required. Murabaha cannot substitute a running finance facility providing cash. Banks may set up asset management/trading subsidiaries holding inventory — higher margins, cheaper items. If the customer is agent, he buys on the bank’s behalf and takes possession; payment by the bank directly to the supplier; double agency (paying and purchasing) should be avoided; the goods remain at the financier’s risk until the client buys them for a deferred price."],"examExplanation":"MPO: bank buys on client’s request from a third party and sells on credit. Reasons: no inventories, many items, specific quality, regulation. Supplier third party; no prior purchase/commitment by client (else ‘Inah). Direct payment to supplier; no double agency. Not a running finance substitute.","keyPoints":["Client’s wish is not a promise unless in due form.","Performance bond for client-nominated supplier.","Avoid double agency (payment + purchase).","Reasons for MPO: banks avoid inventories, cannot stock every item, clients need specific goods, and regulators restrict banks from trading as core business.","Double agency (for payment and for purchase) should be avoided; the bank should pay the supplier directly."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Client-nominated supplier","kind":"textbook","text":"A client asks the bank to buy looms from Supplier S, whom he has chosen. The bank may do so, but must check that the client has not already bought or committed to buy from S — otherwise the deal would be ‘Inah — and may take a performance bond from S (pp. 222–224)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Under MPO the bank may never hold inventory.","right":"MPO permits buying only on request, but banks (or trading subsidiaries) may hold inventory; ownership and risk before sale are what matter (p. 223)."}],"relatedTopics":["t9.8.1","t9.8.2","t9.8.3","t6.11"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A client has already bought machinery from a supplier and asks the bank to ‘do a Murabaha’ to pay the supplier. This would be:","options":["Valid MPO","Bai‘ al ‘Inah / a loan with interest — not compliant","Musawamah","Salam"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 222–223."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch9-t8"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch9-t8","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"9.8-9.8.2","sectionTitle":"Murabaha to Purchase Orderer (MPO) / MPO - A Bunch of Contracts / Promise to Purchase in Murabaha","title":"MPO as a 'Bunch of Contracts' and the Binding Nature of the Promise to Purchase","covers":["t9.8","t9.8.1","t9.8.2"],"overview":"Explains why modern bank Murabaha (MPO) exists, its three-contract legal structure, and the contested question of whether a customer's promise to purchase is legally binding.","simpleExplanation":"Banks don't want to keep warehouses full of random goods waiting for customers - that's expensive, impractical, and regulators generally don't want banks acting as full-time traders. So instead, a customer first asks for specific goods, then the bank buys them and sells them on. This 'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer' is really three separate contracts stitched together: a promise by the customer to buy, an agency arrangement to go get the goods, and the actual sale itself. A key legal question is whether the customer's initial promise to buy is binding - most scholars today say yes, it can be made binding, and if the customer backs out, they must cover the bank's actual loss (but not lost potential profit).","academicExplanation":"MPO (Murabaha lil 'amri bil Shira / Murabaha li Wa'da bi Shira) exists because: (1) banks do not want to maintain diverse goods inventories (storage/holding costs); (2) the range of possible client-requested goods is too broad to stock in advance; (3) clients often need specific-quality goods whose source the bank may not know; (4) regulators generally restrict banks from trading as a core business (liquidity/asset-risk concerns, and to avoid cartelization). Consequently, AAOIFI permits IFIs to purchase items purely in response to a customer's wish/application - though this wish is not itself a binding promise unless made in due form (typically incorporated into the purchase requisition). The bank must verify the supplier is a genuine independent third party with no prior purchase/commitment from the client (otherwise it becomes Bai' al 'Inah); performance bonds can be taken to ensure supplier good faith. MPO comprises three distinct contracts: (1) a master contract defining the overall facility, followed by the client's promise/agreement to purchase - structured as a unilateral promise binding only the client, not a bilateral forward sale contract; (2) a separate agency contract, whereby an agent (client or third party) purchases and takes possession on the bank's behalf; (3) the actual Murabaha sale contract, concluded only once the bank owns the commodity. On the binding nature of promises: classical Fiqh generally holds mere promises as morally advisable but not legally enforceable (Abu Hanifa, Shafi'e, Ahmad, some Malikis), though some Maliki jurists hold that if the promisee has incurred expenses/liability relying on the promise, fulfillment becomes mandatory and court-enforceable. Taqi Usmani argues that in commercial dealings, where a party gives an absolute promise and the other incurs liabilities relying on it, enforcement is warranted; if parties agree the promise is binding, courts/arbitration may compel either performance or payment of actual damages (excluding opportunity cost) - a view aligned with AAOIFI's MPO Standard and OIC Fiqh Academy resolutions. Even a mutual (bilateral) promise is permissible in Murabaha only if Khiyar (option) is given to one or both parties - without it, a binding mutual promise would functionally resemble an ordinary sale contract requiring the seller to already possess the goods (which the bank does not yet, at the promise stage). Banks may also take Hamish Jiddiyah (earnest money) to ensure sincerity, and can reserve a Khiyar al-Shart (option to return) with the supplier that expires once the actual sale to the customer is concluded. Crucially, a mere promise must never be treated as a concluded sale - the actual sale requires proper offer and acceptance once the bank has possession and ownership.","examExplanation":"MPO exists because banks avoid holding inventory (cost, regulatory limits, unpredictable client needs). MPO = 3 contracts: (1) client's unilateral promise to purchase (not a bilateral forward sale); (2) separate agency contract to acquire the goods; (3) actual Murabaha sale, concluded only once bank owns the goods. Promise enforceability: classically non-binding (majority view), but Usmani/AAOIFI/OIC Fiqh Academy support binding promises in commercial Murabaha dealings where a party relies on the promise - breach entitles the bank to recover ACTUAL loss only, never opportunity cost. Mutual (bilateral) binding promise requires Khiyar for at least one party to remain valid (else it functions as a premature sale of goods the bank doesn't yet own).","keyPoints":["MPO exists mainly because banks avoid holding inventory (cost, unpredictable client needs, regulatory restriction on trading as core business, risk of cartelization).","A customer's mere 'wish/application' to buy is not automatically a binding promise unless made in due form - typically embedded in the purchase requisition.","MPO is structured as three separate contracts: (1) unilateral promise to purchase, (2) agency contract to acquire the goods, (3) the actual Murabaha sale (only once the bank owns the goods).","Classical Fiqh generally treats mere promises as non-binding/non-enforceable, but Usmani, AAOIFI, and the OIC Fiqh Academy support binding promises in commercial Murabaha dealings when the other party has relied on the promise and incurred liability.","If a customer breaches a binding promise, the bank can recover only its actual monetary loss - never the conventional 'opportunity cost' of lost potential profit.","A mutual (two-sided) binding promise in Murabaha is only permissible if Khiyar (option) is granted to at least one party - otherwise it functions like a premature sale of goods the bank does not yet possess.","A promise, however structured, must never be treated as itself a concluded sale - actual sale requires a proper subsequent offer and acceptance once the bank holds ownership and possession."],"definitions":[{"term":"Hamish Jiddiyah","definition":"Earnest money a bank may take from a customer at the promise stage to confirm sincerity to purchase; if the customer defaults, the bank may recover its actual loss from this amount (not opportunity cost)."}],"conditions":["The supplier in an MPO transaction must be a genuine independent third party with no prior purchase or firm commitment from the client - otherwise the transaction becomes prohibited Bai' al 'Inah.","A mutual (bilateral) binding promise in Murabaha requires that Khiyar (option) be granted to at least one party to remain valid.","The actual Murabaha sale contract can only be concluded once the bank has acquired ownership of the commodity."],"principles":["Fulfilling a promise is a moral/religious obligation; whether it is also legally (court) enforceable depends on whether the parties agreed the promise would be binding and whether the promisee relied on it to their detriment."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students often think 'binding promise' means the bank can treat the promise itself as the sale. It cannot - a promise, even a binding one, only obligates performance (or damages on breach); the actual sale contract is a distinct, later step requiring the bank to already own the goods."],"distinctions":["A unilateral (one-sided) promise (generally permissible and can be made binding) vs a bilateral (mutual) promise (permissible only if Khiyar is granted, else it functions as a premature sale)."],"relatedConcepts":["Wakalah","Bai' al 'Inah","Khiyar al-Shart"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8-9.8.2","pages":[222,223,224,225]}}]},{"id":"t9.8.1","section":"9.8.1","title":"MPO — A Bunch of Contracts","pages":[224,224],"tier":"core","concepts":["mpo","wakalah","wad"],"intuition":"An MPO is not one contract but three. Which, and in what order?","simpleExplanation":"(1) A master contract plus the client’s unilateral promise to purchase (binding him, not the bank); (2) an agency contract (client or third party buys and takes possession for the bank) — separate from the Murabaha; (3) the actual Murabaha, concluded only when the bank owns the goods. This raises questions: promise binding or not, remedies, sequencing, early/late payment, loss mitigation.","academicExplanation":["MPO comprises three distinct contracts: (1) a master contract defining the facility, followed by an agreement to purchase — a unilateral promise binding the client, not the bank, rather than a bilateral forward sale; (2) an agency contract under which the agent (client or third party) purchases the item and takes possession on the bank’s behalf, separate from the Murabaha agreement; (3) the actual Murabaha contract, concluded when the bank owns the commodity. Issues: unilateral or bilateral, binding or not; remedy if the client backs out; sequencing; early or delayed payment; loss mitigation; structure meeting stakeholder needs."],"examExplanation":"MPO = (1) master agreement + unilateral promise; (2) separate agency; (3) Murabaha after bank ownership.","keyPoints":["Promise binds client only.","Agency separate from Murabaha.","The agency contract must be separate from the Murabaha agreement.","The promise is unilateral: it binds the client, not the bank."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Three separate papers","kind":"practice","text":"Month 1: the client signs a master agreement and promises to buy. Month 2: a separate agency agreement appoints him to buy steel for the bank. Month 3: once the bank owns the steel, the client offers to buy and the bank accepts — the Murabaha itself (p. 224)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t9.8.2","t5.5.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In MPO, the ‘agreement to buy’ is best described as:","options":["A bilateral forward sale","A unilateral promise binding the client","The sale itself","A loan"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 224."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"processSteps":["Master agreement and client’s unilateral promise to purchase","Agency contract — agent buys and takes possession for the bank","Actual Murabaha — after bank owns the goods"]},{"id":"t9.8.2","section":"9.8.2","title":"Promise to Purchase in Murabaha","pages":[224,225],"tier":"core","concepts":["wad","hamish-jiddiyah","khiyar"],"intuition":"If the bank buys goods relying on the client’s promise and the client walks away, what happens?","simpleExplanation":"Classically promises were not enforceable (Abu Hanifa, Shafi‘i, Ahmad, some Malikis), but many jurists enforce them, and a Maliki view binds where expenses were incurred. Usmani: in commerce, an absolute promise on which the other incurs liabilities is enforceable — purchase or actual damages, no opportunity cost (OIC, AAOIFI). A mutual promise is allowed only with an option to one party. The bank may take Hamish Jiddiyah, and keep a Khiyar al-Shart with the supplier. The promise is not the sale.","academicExplanation":["Classical view: mere promises are not binding through courts. Other jurists enforce them; a Maliki view binds where the promisee incurred expenses or liability. Usmani: fulfilling promises is obligatory; in commercial dealings where an absolute promise led the other to incur liabilities and the parties agreed it binds, a court or arbitration may force purchase or actual damages — actual monetary loss excluding opportunity cost (OIC Fiqh Academy 5th session; AAOIFI MPO standard). A mutual (bilateral) promise in Murabaha is permissible only if an option (Khiyar) is given to one or both; otherwise it is like a sale of goods not possessed. The bank may take Hamish Jiddiyah. On a client’s request, the bank may buy from the supplier with Khiyar al-Shart (option to return within a period), expiring on sale to the customer — a risk mitigation tool. The actual sale must take place by offer and acceptance once the bank has ownership and possession."],"examExplanation":"Promise binding in commerce (Usmani, OIC, AAOIFI): specific performance or actual damages (no opportunity cost). Bilateral promise only with Khiyar. Hamish Jiddiyah; bank’s Khiyar al-Shart with supplier mitigates asset risk. Promise ≠ sale.","keyPoints":["Opportunity cost excluded from damages.","Khiyar with supplier as risk tool."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Option to return to the supplier","kind":"textbook","text":"The bank may buy from the supplier with Khiyar al-Shart (option to return) for a set period, expiring when it sells to the client — a tool against asset risk if the client backs out (p. 225)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A bilateral binding promise in Murabaha is always allowed.","right":"A mutual binding promise is like a sale of goods not yet owned, so it is permissible only if one or both parties keep an option (p. 225)."}],"relatedTopics":["t5.6","t5.6.1","t3.3.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"When is a mutual (bilateral) promise permissible in Murabaha per AAOIFI?","options":["Always","Only if an option (Khiyar) is given to one or both parties","Never","Only for governments"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 225."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t9.8.3","section":"9.8.3","title":"MPO with the Customer as the Bank’s Agent — Stages","pages":[225,229],"tier":"core","concepts":["mpo","wakalah","qabd"],"intuition":"Walk through a real bank Murabaha step by step — where can it go wrong?","simpleExplanation":"Seven steps: request; bank appoints client as agent; bank buys via client; bank pays supplier; client takes delivery for the bank; client offers to buy and bank accepts (title passes); client pays on deferred basis without rollover. Stages: pre-promise (genuine goods need, no prior contract with supplier, supplier not >50% owned by client, eligible goods), MoU, requisition (promise, HJ), agency (before purchase), purchase (rebates passed on; price changes approved), possession (goods in bank’s risk; not already used), offer and acceptance (price and dates fixed; relationship becomes debtor–creditor), security.","academicExplanation":["General structure: (1) request; (2) bank appoints client as agent; (3) purchase through the client; (4) bank pays supplier; (5) client takes delivery as agent; (6) client offers, bank accepts — title transfers; (7) deferred payment without rollover, discount or rebate. Stages: pre-promise understanding; promise; agency; possession; execution; post-execution. Relationships: principal–agent; promisor–promisee; buyer–seller (bank–supplier); seller–buyer (bank–client); creditor–debtor.","Pre-promise/facility approval: genuine trade of goods, not liquidity or cash finance; no prior contractual relationship between client and supplier (transferring an executed contract is ‘Inah); supplier a third party — not the customer, his agent or an entity >50% owned by him; commodity within Murabaha scope (no gold, silver, currencies); risk analysis of goods and client. Promise stage — master Murabaha facility agreement/MoU: limit, commodity, margin, schedule, security, specimens of requisition, delivery report, promissory note, collateral; many sub-Murabahas possible; agency agreement may be signed here. Purchase requisition: goods, supplier, cost, delivery date; ensure goods not already owned by client (else income to charity); promise to buy; performance bond; Hamish Jiddiyah — actual loss recoverable, not cost of funds or opportunity cost. Pay supplier directly; advance payment possible with higher margin.","Agency stage: agreement before purchase (otherwise ‘Inah); specific or general agency. Purchasing: per specifications; rebates passed on by reducing cost; price escalation to be approved; commodity change by consent; time limit, refund of cost without opportunity cost if delayed. Title and possession: bank must take ownership and actual or constructive possession — goods under the bank’s risk (OIC 6th session on forms of possession); the point of risk transfer must be clear (hence natural gas in pipes is not approved); goods must exist — using them in production before offer and acceptance creates a Shari’ah objection; inspection advisable; ownership expenses (Takaful) and pre-sale loss are the bank’s.","Execution: possession report and offer by client; bank’s acceptance with contract price and payment schedule; relationship becomes debtor–creditor; bank no longer liable for harm. Client confirms examination; AAOIFI: bank assigns to the client its recourse against the supplier for defects. Security: hypothecation, pledge, lien, mortgage, guarantees; the sold item itself may be security once possession is given; some boards allow interest-bearing securities (recourse to principal only), but it is preferable to encash them for compliant securities."],"examExplanation":"MPO with client as agent: request → agency → purchase via client → bank pays supplier → client possesses for bank → client offers/bank accepts → deferred payment (no rollover). Key checks: genuine goods; no prior client–supplier contract; supplier ≤50% client-owned; agency before purchase; bank bears risk until sale; goods exist at execution; direct payment; rebates passed on.","keyPoints":["Five relationships between bank, client and supplier.","Supplier must not be >50% owned by the client.","Goods used before offer and acceptance → objection.","Natural gas in pipes: risk-transfer point unclear.","Invoice date must fall between the agency agreement and the offer to purchase.","Before Murabaha execution, Takaful and any loss belong to the bank as owner.","A sold commodity may itself be pledged back to the bank once possession has passed to the client."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Invoice dates in MPO","kind":"textbook","text":"The agency agreement is signed on 1 March and the client’s offer to purchase on 20 March. A supplier invoice dated 25 February means the goods were bought before the agency, so the bank never owned them: the invoice must fall between the two dates (pp. 225–229)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t9.9","t9.10","t14.4.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"At what point does the relationship between the bank and client become debtor–creditor in MPO?","options":["When the MoU is signed","When the agency agreement is signed","When the bank accepts the client’s offer to purchase (Murabaha executed)","When the supplier is paid"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 229."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch9-t9"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch9-t9","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"9.8.3","sectionTitle":"MPO - The Customer as the Bank's Agent to Buy and Related Matters","title":"MPO Full Procedure: The Customer as the Bank's Purchasing Agent","covers":["t9.8.3"],"overview":"Walks through the complete, most commonly used MPO structure step by step - from facility approval through promise, agency, purchase, possession, execution, and security - as described in the book, including the primary Shari'ah safeguard against Bai' al 'Inah at each stage.","simpleExplanation":"This is the detailed, real-world version of how a bank does Murabaha when the customer buys the goods on the bank's behalf. First, the bank checks the deal is genuine and the customer hasn't already bought the goods themselves (that would be a disguised loan). Then the customer promises to buy from the bank later, gets appointed as the bank's purchasing agent, actually buys the goods (with the bank paying the supplier directly and taking on ownership risk), and only after the customer reports back and formally offers to buy from the bank does the real Murabaha sale happen - with the price and payment schedule locked in at that point.","academicExplanation":"The general structure: (1) customer requests purchase of a legally saleable commodity; (2) bank appoints the client as its purchasing agent; (3) bank purchases the commodity through the client-as-agent; (4) bank pays the vendor/supplier; (5) customer takes delivery on the bank's behalf as agent; (6) customer makes an offer to purchase, bank accepts, title transfers upon execution of Murabaha; (7) customer pays on a deferred basis without rollover, discount, or rebate. This unfolds through distinct stages, each critical to Shari'ah validity: Pre-Promise Stage (facility approval) - the transaction must involve genuine trade of goods (never used for pure cash/liquidity financing), and crucially there must be no prior contractual relationship between client and the nominated supplier (transferring an already-executed client-supplier contract to the bank is Bai' al 'Inah and prohibited); the supplier must not be the customer, his agent, or an entity >50% owned by the customer; gold/silver/currency (Bai' al Sarf items) are excluded. Promise Stage (Master Murabaha Facility Agreement/MoU) - sets the facility limit, commodity nature, profit margin, payment schedule, and security requirements; a single MoU can cover multiple purchase 'sub-Murabahas' over time. Purchase Requisition - the client submits a specific purchase request (goods, supplier if known, cost, delivery date), the bank verifies the client doesn't already own the goods (else income must be credited to charity), and can demand Hamish Jiddiyah (earnest money, recoverable only for actual loss, never opportunity cost) and a performance bond if the client nominates the supplier; payment is preferably made directly to the supplier (misuse risk if routed through the client) - advance payment to the supplier typically commands a higher profit margin than post-supply payment. Agency Stage - the agency agreement must be signed before the client purchases any goods (purchasing first would mean the goods are already the client's own property, again risking Bai' al 'Inah); can be 'specific' (one-off) or 'general' (recurring) agency. Purchasing Stage - client purchases per agreed specifications; supplier rebates must be passed to the client by reducing the cost basis; price escalations beyond the agreed limit require bank approval (bank may reject an off-agreement purchase); delayed purchase may allow the bank to demand refund of costs without opportunity cost. Acquisition of Title and Possession - the bank must take actual or constructive possession before Murabaha execution (per the OIC Fiqh Academy's 6th session resolution, possession may be physical or 'implied/constructive' - leaving the commodity at the possessor's disposal so he can deal with it as he wishes); the exact moment risk transfers from supplier to bank (and later bank to customer) must be clearly identifiable - which is why scholars generally disapprove Murabaha of natural gas moving through pipes (no clear possession-transfer point); goods must exist and be inspected before execution, and ownership-related costs (e.g. Takaful) before that point are the bank's responsibility. Execution Stage (Offer and Acceptance) - after taking possession as agent, the customer gives a possession report and formally offers to purchase; the bank's acceptance completes the transaction, converting the buyer-seller relationship into a debtor-creditor relationship, after which the bank bears no further liability for the goods; AAOIFI recommends the bank assign the customer the right of recourse against the supplier for defects. Security Stage - the bank may require collateral (hypothecation, pledge, lien, mortgage, guarantees) for the deferred price, including the sold commodity itself (once possession is with the customer, who then bears its risk/reward); some boards permit interest-bearing instruments as collateral (recourse limited to principal only), though encashing them for Shari'ah-compliant securities is preferred.","examExplanation":"Full MPO (client-as-agent) sequence: facility approval (verify genuine trade need, no prior client-supplier deal - avoid Bai' al 'Inah) -> promise/MoU (limit, margin, schedule) -> purchase requisition (Hamish Jiddiyah, performance bond if client-nominated supplier) -> agency agreement (must precede any client purchase) -> client purchases as agent, bank pays supplier directly, bank bears risk -> bank takes actual/constructive possession (clear risk-transfer point required) -> customer reports possession and makes offer -> bank accepts, Murabaha executed, title/risk transfers to customer, relationship becomes debtor-creditor -> customer pays per schedule (no rollover/discount) -> security/collateral held throughout for the deferred price. Key Shari'ah trip-wire at every stage: avoiding Bai' al 'Inah by ensuring goods were never already the client's before the bank's involvement.","keyPoints":["The agency agreement must always be signed BEFORE the client purchases any goods - purchasing first would mean the goods are already the client's own property, risking prohibited Bai' al 'Inah.","Payment should preferably go directly from the bank to the supplier, not routed through the client, to prevent misuse and ensure Shari'ah compliance.","Possession may be actual (physical) or constructive/implied (goods left at the possessor's disposal, under his control) - per the OIC Fiqh Academy's 6th session resolution.","The precise point at which ownership risk transfers (supplier-to-bank, then bank-to-customer) must be clearly identifiable - this is why Murabaha of natural gas moving through pipelines is generally disapproved.","Once Murabaha is executed (offer and acceptance), the relationship between bank and customer converts from buyer-seller to debtor-creditor, and the bank bears no further liability for the goods.","The sold commodity itself can serve as security for the deferred price once possession has passed to the customer, who then bears its risk and reward.","Hamish Jiddiyah (earnest money) may be taken to confirm the client's sincerity, but the bank may only recover its actual loss from it if the client defaults - never a conventional 'opportunity cost' or cost-of-funds charge."],"definitions":[{"term":"Specific Agency","definition":"An agency agreement covering a one-off, non-recurring purchase of a commodity for the bank."},{"term":"General Agency","definition":"An agency agreement covering recurring/consistent purchases of a commodity for the bank over time."}],"conditions":["There must be no prior contractual relationship between the client and the nominated supplier before the bank's involvement, to avoid Bai' al 'Inah.","The supplier must not be the customer himself, his agent, or an entity more than 50% owned by the customer.","The agency agreement must be signed before the client purchases the goods.","The bank must take actual or constructive possession, with a clear identifiable point of risk transfer, before executing the Murabaha sale.","Goods must exist and be verified (e.g. via physical inspection) at the time Murabaha is executed."],"principles":["Possession, per the OIC Fiqh Academy, may be physical or constructive/implied - what matters is that the commodity is left at the possessor's disposal, enabling him to deal with it as he wishes."],"processSteps":["Customer Need: The customer approaches the bank with a request to purchase a specific, legally saleable commodity, disclosing his need and (if known) a supplier.","Facility Approval: The bank verifies the trade is genuine, that the client has no prior purchase/commitment with the nominated supplier (to avoid Bai' al 'Inah), and that the goods are Murabaha-eligible (not gold/silver/currency/debt).","Promise and Master Agreement: The bank and client sign an MoU/master Murabaha facility agreement fixing the facility limit, profit margin, payment schedule and security requirements; the client's promise to purchase is typically included in the purchase requisition and, if agreed as binding, is enforceable to the extent of the bank's actual loss.","Agency Appointment: The bank appoints the client (or, preferably per AAOIFI, a third party) as its agent to purchase the specified commodity, via a separate agency agreement signed before any purchase takes place.","Purchase and Possession by the Bank: The agent purchases the commodity from the supplier; the bank pays the supplier directly; the goods come under the bank's ownership and risk (actual or constructive possession, with a clear risk-transfer point).","Customer's Offer to Purchase: Having taken delivery as agent and confirmed the goods exist and are satisfactory, the customer submits a possession report and makes a formal offer to purchase the goods from the bank at cost plus the agreed profit margin.","Bank's Acceptance and Execution: The bank accepts the offer; the Murabaha sale is concluded; ownership and risk transfer to the customer, and the relationship converts from buyer-seller to debtor-creditor.","Deferred Payment: The customer pays the agreed cost-plus price according to the fixed schedule, without rollover, discount, or rebate (absent a supplier rebate or bank-discretion early-payment rebate)."],"examples":[{"title":"Box 9.2 - Murabaha for Import Financing (summarized)","kind":"textbook","text":"An Islamic bank and importer sign a master Murabaha agreement and agency agreement; the customer, as agent, negotiates with a foreign supplier and requests the bank to open an L/C (with Takaful arranged on the bank's behalf). On shipment, documents flow through the negotiating bank to the Islamic bank; if the customer needs no credit, the bank sells at spot cost via Musawamah/spot Murabaha (L/C cost + charges + Takaful); if financing is needed, Murabaha is executed with profit charged from the date the bank's Nostro account was debited to the settlement date."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often think the customer's initial 'purchase requisition' itself constitutes the Murabaha sale. It does not - it is part of the promise/agency stage; the actual sale only occurs at the later offer-and-acceptance stage, after the bank has taken possession."],"distinctions":["The agency relationship (client buys FOR the bank, bank bears risk) vs the eventual sale relationship (bank sells TO the client, client then bears risk) - two distinct legal relationships within one overall MPO transaction."],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' al 'Inah","Wakalah","Hamish Jiddiyah","Khiyar"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[225,226,227,228,229]},"transactionSteps":[{"step":"Customer Need","description":"The customer approaches the bank requesting purchase of a specific, legally saleable commodity, and there must be no prior contractual relationship between the customer and the nominated supplier (to avoid Bai' al 'Inah)."},{"step":"Promise and Master Agreement","description":"The bank and customer sign an MoU/master Murabaha facility agreement fixing the facility limit, the profit margin, the payment schedule, and required security; the customer's promise to purchase (often embedded in the purchase requisition) can be made binding, enforceable to the extent of the bank's actual loss if breached."},{"step":"Agency Appointment","description":"The bank appoints the customer (or, preferably, a third party) as its agent to purchase the specified commodity, via a separate agency agreement signed before any purchase takes place."},{"step":"Bank Takes Ownership and Risk","description":"The agent purchases the commodity from the supplier; the bank pays the supplier directly; the goods come under the bank's actual or constructive possession, ownership, and risk, with a clearly identifiable transfer point."},{"step":"Customer's Offer to Purchase","description":"Having taken delivery as agent and verified the goods exist and are satisfactory, the customer submits a possession report and makes a formal offer to buy the goods from the bank at cost plus the agreed profit margin."},{"step":"Bank Accepts - Murabaha Executed","description":"The bank accepts the offer; ownership and risk transfer to the customer; the relationship converts from buyer-seller (agency) to debtor-creditor (Murabaha receivable)."},{"step":"Deferred Payment","description":"The customer pays the agreed cost-plus price on the fixed schedule, with no rollover, discount, or rebate (absent a genuine supplier rebate or bank-discretion early-payment rebate)."}]}],"processSteps":["Customer requests purchase of a lawful commodity","Bank appoints customer as its agent (separate agency agreement)","Customer buys the commodity on the bank’s behalf","Bank pays the supplier directly","Customer takes delivery as agent — goods at the bank’s risk","Customer offers to buy; bank accepts — title and risk pass to customer","Customer pays the fixed deferred price — no rollover, discount or rebate"],"table":{"caption":"Relationships between the parties in MPO (p. 226)","head":["Stage","Relationship"],"rows":[["Agency","Bank = principal, client = agent"],["Promise","Client = promisor, bank = promisee"],["Purchase","Bank = buyer, supplier = seller"],["Murabaha sale","Bank = seller, client = buyer"],["After sale","Bank = creditor, client = debtor"]]}},{"id":"t9.9","section":"9.9","title":"Issues in Murabaha: Two Sales in One, Timing, Shares and Commodity Murabaha","pages":[229,233],"tier":"core","concepts":["murabaha","tawarruq"],"intuition":"Critics raise several objections to bank Murabaha. Which are answered, and which remain concerns?","simpleExplanation":"Objection: binding promise + sale = two sales in one — answered: a unilateral promise is not a contract. Timing: execute only after the bank owns and possesses the goods; agency and sale must be separate. Shares Murabaha is allowed with screening, payment to brokers directly, no client as agent, bank bearing settlement-period price risk, and no sister-concern shares. Commodity Murabaha on exchanges (e.g. LME) via Tawarruq is tricky: doubts about actual transactions and risk transfer; should be used only in extreme cases.","academicExplanation":["‘Two sales in one’ objection (Ray): the binding promise takes the character of a sale. Answer: no major principle is violated; almost all contemporary jurists allow it; a unilateral promise does not become a formal contract.","9.9.3 Time of executing Murabaha: only after the bank gets ownership and possession and becomes responsible for loss or defects. Some edicts refer to sale before possession — creating credibility problems. Agency contracts for buying and immediately selling must not be part and parcel of the Murabaha; after possession by the agent, a separate offer and acceptance is needed.","9.9.7 Murabaha through shares: shares represent tangible assets; permissible with screening. Banks should pay brokers directly; the client should not be agent; after actual transfer (e.g. via central depository), the bank may sell; if settlement takes three days, the bank bears the price risk; shares must not be of the client’s sister concern (buy-back).","9.9.8 Commodity Murabaha: short-term placement via purchase/sale on international exchanges (e.g. LME), used for liquidity. Banks use Tawarruq: appoint a broker (often a conventional bank as agent) to buy metal from broker A for cash and sell on deferred payment to broker B the same day. Nobody checks whether actual transactions occurred or when risk passed; doubts about metal quantities; brokerage costs suggest no actual transactions. Tawarruq even in genuine form should be used only in extreme cases; not approved by all; widespread use is harmful."],"examExplanation":"Two-sales objection answered (unilateral promise). Execute after ownership/possession; separate agency and sale. Shares Murabaha: screening, direct broker payment, bank bears settlement risk, no sister concern. Commodity Murabaha/Tawarruq: doubtful risk transfer; extreme cases only.","keyPoints":["Share settlement period risk (≈3 days) borne by bank.","Organised commodity Tawarruq harmful if widespread.","Shares Murabaha: pay brokers directly, do not appoint the client as agent, wait for actual transfer (about three days, with price risk on the bank) and avoid shares of the client’s sister concerns."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"LME commodity Murabaha","kind":"textbook","text":"Banks appoint a conventional bank as agent to buy metal from broker A for cash and sell it to broker B on credit the same day; nobody checks if a real transaction or risk transfer occurred — the author urges using genuine Tawarruq only in extreme cases (p. 233)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t13.3.1","t14.4.4","t9.8.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In shares Murabaha, which practice does the author advise?","options":["Appoint the client as agent to buy shares","Pay brokers directly and sell only after actual transfer to the bank","Use shares of the client’s sister company","Sell before settlement"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 233."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch9-t12"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch9-t12","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"9.9.7-9.9.8","sectionTitle":"Murabaha Through Shares / Commodity Murabaha","title":"Murabaha Issues III: Shares Murabaha and Commodity Murabaha (Tawarruq)","covers":["t9.9"],"overview":"Covers two specialized modern applications: financing the purchase of Shari'ah-screened shares via Murabaha, and using commodity Murabaha (Tawarruq) on international exchanges purely for liquidity management, with the author's caution about the latter's overuse.","simpleExplanation":"Banks can finance the purchase of Shari'ah-compliant shares using Murabaha, as long as the bank actually pays and takes ownership of the shares first (not through the client as agent) and the shares aren't from a company linked to the client (which would again be a disguised buy-back). Separately, some banks use 'commodity Murabaha' on international metal exchanges (like the London Metal Exchange) purely to generate cash for liquidity purposes via Tawarruq - buying a metal for cash and immediately reselling it on credit to a different party. The author is skeptical of this practice: it's easy to abuse, hard to verify real transactions occurred, and should be used only as a last resort, not as a routine liquidity tool.","academicExplanation":"Shares Murabaha: since shares represent tangible ownership of a joint stock company's assets, their trading (and Murabaha sale) is permissible provided Shari'ah screening criteria are met. Extra care is required: the bank must pay brokers directly (the client should NOT be appointed purchasing agent for shares), wait for actual transfer (typically ~3 days, during which the bank itself bears price risk), and ensure the shares are not those of any 'sister concern' of the client (otherwise it becomes prohibited Bai' al 'Inah/buy-back). Commodity Murabaha: used by some Middle Eastern and Western banks as an international treasury liquidity mechanism via commodity exchanges (e.g. the London Metal Exchange), where banks use Tawarruq - appointing a broker to purchase a metal for cash and immediately resell it to a different broker on deferred payment. Practically, Islamic banks often use a conventional bank as an intermediary agent to buy from Broker A (cash) and sell to Broker B (deferred). The author flags significant concerns: uncertainty over whether genuine transactions actually take place and when risk transfers to the bank, doubts about whether sufficient physical metal exists to back transaction volumes, and brokerage costs eroding competitiveness (raising the risk that transactions may be purely nominal/paper exercises). The author's conclusion: genuine Tawarruq should be used only in extreme cases where no alternative exists to avoid interest, has not been approved by all scholars, and widespread routine use is harmful to the Islamic banking industry's long-term credibility.","examExplanation":"Shares Murabaha: permissible if shares pass Shari'ah screening; bank must pay brokers directly (client NOT agent for share purchase), bear ~3-day settlement price risk, and avoid buying shares of the client's own sister concerns (else Bai' al 'Inah). Commodity Murabaha (Tawarruq): used for international liquidity management via metal exchanges (e.g. LME) - buy metal for cash from Broker A, resell on deferred payment to Broker B (often via a conventional bank as intermediary agent). Author's caution: doubts about genuine transaction occurrence, sufficiency of underlying metal, brokerage cost erosion, and general scholarly disapproval of routine (non-emergency) use.","keyPoints":["In shares Murabaha, the bank must pay brokers directly and should NOT appoint the client as agent for purchasing the shares themselves.","The bank bears the price risk during the share settlement/transfer period (typically about three days) before Murabaha can be executed.","Shares of the client's own 'sister concern' cannot be the subject of Murabaha to the client - this would constitute prohibited Bai' al 'Inah/buy-back.","Commodity Murabaha (Tawarruq) on international commodity exchanges is used by some banks purely as a treasury liquidity mechanism, buying a metal for cash and reselling on deferred payment to a different party.","The author expresses significant skepticism about commodity Murabaha/Tawarruq: doubts about genuine transaction occurrence and risk transfer timing, insufficient underlying metal supply relative to transaction volumes, and brokerage costs eroding competitiveness.","The author's conclusion: even genuine Tawarruq should be reserved for extreme cases with no alternative to avoid interest; it lacks universal scholarly approval, and its widespread/routine use is harmful to the Islamic banking industry's long-term credibility."],"definitions":[{"term":"Commodity Murabaha (Tawarruq)","definition":"A treasury liquidity mechanism where a bank/broker purchases a commodity (e.g. a metal on the LME) for cash and immediately resells it to a different party on deferred payment, generating cash for the original purchaser - used for liquidity management but subject to significant scholarly caution about overuse."}],"conditions":["The client cannot be appointed as purchasing agent for shares Murabaha; the bank must pay brokers directly.","Shares subject to Murabaha must not belong to a 'sister concern' of the client, to avoid Bai' al 'Inah."],"principles":["Even where a mechanism (like Tawarruq) has scholarly acceptance in a genuine, restricted form, its widespread routine use to manufacture liquidity is a distinct concern from its bare permissibility."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume commodity Murabaha/Tawarruq is uncontroversially accepted because it is widely used internationally. The author explicitly notes it 'has not been approved by all scholars' and cautions against normalizing its use beyond genuine emergency liquidity needs."],"distinctions":["Shares Murabaha (bank buys real, Shari'ah-screened shares and bears real settlement risk) vs commodity Murabaha/Tawarruq (used purely to generate cash, raising doubts about whether genuine risk-bearing trade actually occurs)."],"relatedConcepts":["Tawarruq","Bai' al 'Inah","Screening Criteria"],"examRelevance":"detailed","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.7-9.9.8","pages":[233]}}],"subsections":[{"number":"9.9.3","title":"Time of Executing Murabaha","page":231,"points":["After ownership and possession","Separate agency and sale"]},{"number":"9.9.7","title":"Murabaha Through Shares","page":233,"points":["Screening","Direct broker payment","No sister concerns"]},{"number":"9.9.8","title":"Commodity Murabaha","page":233,"points":["LME Tawarruq","Doubts on actual transactions","Extreme cases only"]}],"debate":[{"issue":"Commodity Murabaha for liquidity management","criticism":"Implicit in practice: banks need a short-term compliant placement; metal Tawarruq on the LME provides it.","response":"The author: nobody checks whether actual transactions occur or when risk passes; metal quantities may be insufficient; brokerage costs suggest no real transactions. Tawarruq should be used only where no alternative exists; widespread use harms the industry.","takeaway":"Genuine asset transfer and risk are essential; organised Tawarruq is a last resort."}]},{"id":"t9.9.1","section":"9.9.1","title":"Avoiding Buy-back","pages":[230,230],"tier":"core","concepts":["inah"],"intuition":"What single check prevents a Murabaha from becoming ‘Inah?","simpleExplanation":"Bai‘ al ‘Inah (sell and resell between the same parties, cash then higher credit price) is a device to circumvent Riba and prohibited. Malaysian authorities accept it, but mainstream scholars elsewhere and AAOIFI do not. Banks must ensure the goods are not already owned by the client.","academicExplanation":["‘Inah is a double sale by which borrower and lender sell and resell an object, once for cash and again for a higher credit price, netting a loan with interest — a legal device to circumvent Riba, hence prohibited. Malaysian banking authorities consider it acceptable; mainstream experts from the Middle East and elsewhere do not. In MPO banks must be vigilant that the goods required are not already owned by the client (AAOIFI)."],"examExplanation":"‘Inah prohibited (mainstream, AAOIFI; Malaysia accepts). Check goods not already owned by client.","keyPoints":["Malaysian acceptance is a minority position."],"definitions":[{"term":"Bai‘ al ‘Inah","definition":"Sale and buy-back between the same parties — once for cash, once for a higher credit price — producing a loan with interest."}],"examples":[{"title":"Buy-back disguised as Murabaha","kind":"textbook","text":"A client sells his own stock of cotton to the bank for 1 million cash and buys it back on Murabaha for 1.1 million payable later. This is ‘Inah, a loan of 1 million at 10 %, which is why banks must confirm the goods are not already the client’s (p. 230)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Because Malaysian authorities accept ‘Inah, it is mainstream practice.","right":"Mainstream Shari’ah experts in the Middle East and elsewhere, and AAOIFI, consider it impermissible (p. 230)."}],"relatedTopics":["t6.11"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which jurisdiction does the author note as accepting Bai‘ al ‘Inah?","options":["Saudi Arabia","Malaysia","Pakistan","Bahrain"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 230."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch9-t10"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch9-t10","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"9.9.1-9.9.3","sectionTitle":"Avoiding Buy-back / Khiyar in Murabaha / Time of Executing Murabaha","title":"Murabaha Issues I: Buy-back (Bai' al 'Inah), Option to Rescind, and Timing of Execution","covers":["t9.9.1","t9.9.2"],"overview":"Covers three practical compliance issues: avoiding Bai' al 'Inah (buy-back), whether the customer has a right to rescind for defects, and when exactly the bank may legally execute the sale.","simpleExplanation":"Banks must be very careful the customer isn't already the owner of the goods being 'financed' - if so, the deal is really just Bai' al 'Inah (buy-back), a disguised interest loan, which mainstream scholars reject even though some Malaysian regulators accept it. On defects: most banks don't build in a formal right to cancel (Khiyar) but instead make the customer responsible for inspecting goods themselves, while shortfalls in quantity/spec remain the seller's problem. On timing: the sale can only legally happen after the bank actually owns and possesses the goods - selling before that point undermines the whole basis of Murabaha.","academicExplanation":"Bai' al 'Inah ('buy-back') is a double sale where borrower and lender sell and resell an object between them - once for cash, once for a higher credit price - net economic effect of an interest loan; it is a device to circumvent the Riba prohibition and is therefore prohibited by mainstream Shari'ah experts (though Malaysian banking authorities historically accepted it, a minority position). Banks conducting MPO must verify the requested goods are not already owned by the client. On Khiyar (option to rescind): most scholars do not consider an explicit Khiyar clause necessary in modern Murabaha; banks commonly stipulate that defects become the buyer's liability if he personally examines the goods or they were adequately described to eliminate ignorance, while any shortfall in quantity/specification remains the seller's liability (proportionally reducing the price, with a rescission right). Juristically, Khiyar al 'Aib (option of defect) and Khiyar al Wasf (option of quality) remain available to the client if goods prove defective or non-conforming before Murabaha execution, and can be returned to the supplier for replacement via the same or a new Murabaha. If option is fully available to the customer, the bank bears greater risk requiring more intensive market surveys, often impractical for banks in their current state - hence practice tends toward appointing the customer as purchasing/inspection agent, with the client responsible for verifying quality themselves, backed by a performance guarantee if he nominated the supplier. On timing: Murabaha may only be legally executed after the bank has actually taken ownership and possession and become responsible for loss/defects; some edicts reportedly permit sale before the bank takes possession, which the author flags as a credibility risk for Islamic banking. Agency contracts (client buying on the bank's behalf) must remain totally separate and independent from the Murabaha sale contract itself - a distinct offer and acceptance must follow after the client, as agent, has taken possession.","examExplanation":"Bai' al 'Inah = prohibited double-sale buy-back (disguised interest loan); mainstream scholars reject it (Malaysia is an exception in practice). Banks must verify goods aren't pre-owned by the client. Khiyar: not usually a stipulated formal clause in modern bank Murabaha - defects become buyer's liability if he inspected/was informed, but quantity/spec shortfalls remain seller's liability (price reduction + rescission right); Khiyar al 'Aib and Khiyar al Wasf remain available if goods are defective before execution. Timing: Murabaha execution must strictly follow the bank's actual possession/ownership; agency (client buying on bank's behalf) and the Murabaha sale must be kept as separate, sequential contracts with a fresh offer/acceptance after possession.","keyPoints":["Bai' al 'Inah ('buy-back') is a prohibited double-sale device (cash sale then higher-price credit resale between the same two parties) that produces the economic effect of an interest loan.","Mainstream Shari'ah scholars from the Middle East and elsewhere reject Bai' al 'Inah; Malaysian banking authorities have historically been an exception in accepting it.","Modern bank Murabaha contracts typically do not stipulate a formal Khiyar clause; instead, defect liability shifts to the buyer if he inspected the goods himself or was adequately informed, while quantity/specification shortfalls remain the seller's liability.","Khiyar al 'Aib (defect option) and Khiyar al Wasf (quality option) remain juristically available to the client for defective/non-conforming goods discovered before Murabaha execution.","Murabaha must be executed only after the bank has actually taken ownership and possession of the goods, becoming responsible for loss/defects - agency (purchase on the bank's behalf) and the Murabaha sale itself must remain separate, sequential contracts with distinct offer and acceptance."],"definitions":[{"term":"Bai' al 'Inah","definition":"A double sale by which a borrower and lender sell and resell an object between themselves - once for cash and again for a higher price on credit - with the net economic result of an interest-bearing loan; a legal device to circumvent the prohibition of Riba, considered impermissible by mainstream Shari'ah experts."}],"conditions":["The supplier/goods in an MPO transaction must not already be owned by the client - otherwise the transaction becomes prohibited Bai' al 'Inah.","Murabaha may be executed only after the bank has taken actual or constructive ownership and possession of the goods.","The agency contract (purchase on the bank's behalf) must be kept legally separate and independent from the Murabaha sale contract."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think the absence of an explicit Khiyar clause means the customer has no recourse for defective goods. In fact, Khiyar al 'Aib and Khiyar al Wasf remain available as a matter of Fiqh even without an explicit clause, for defects discovered before execution."],"distinctions":["Bai' al 'Inah (prohibited double-sale disguised loan) vs a legitimate MPO where the bank genuinely purchases from an independent third-party supplier and bears real risk."],"relatedConcepts":["Khiyar al 'Aib","Khiyar al Wasf","Wakalah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.1-9.9.3","pages":[229,230,231]}}]},{"id":"t9.9.2","section":"9.9.2","title":"Khiyar (Option to Rescind) in Murabaha","pages":[230,231],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["khiyar"],"intuition":"If the goods turn out defective, who is responsible in a bank Murabaha?","simpleExplanation":"Most scholars don’t consider Khiyar necessary in modern Murabaha. Banks stipulate that defects are the buyer’s liability once he has examined the goods, while shortfalls in quantity/specification remain the seller’s (price reduced proportionately; buyer may rescind). Before execution, defective goods can be returned. After inspection and execution, the bank may disclaim liability and assign warranty rights. Banks reduce their risk by making the client agent and taking a performance guarantee.","academicExplanation":["Most scholars do not consider Khiyar necessary in modern Murabaha. Some banks stipulate defects are the buyer’s liability if he examined the goods or they were described to eliminate ignorance; lack of quantity or specification remains the seller’s liability — price reduced proportionately and the buyer may rescind (Kuwait Finance House fatwa). Juristically, Khiyar al ‘Aib and Khiyar al Wasf are available; goods rejected before execution can be returned and replaced through the same or new Murabaha. After inspection and execution the bank may stipulate no liability (AAOIFI 4/9) and assign warranty rights. If options were available, the bank would carry larger risk and need market surveys; in practice the client is made agent for purchase and delivery, often nominates the supplier, and gives a performance guarantee on quality."],"examExplanation":"Khiyar al ‘Aib and al Wasf available; before execution return goods; after inspection and execution bank may disclaim liability and assign warranties; client as agent + performance guarantee reduce bank risk.","keyPoints":["Quantity/specification shortfall remains seller’s liability."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Short delivery","kind":"textbook","text":"Even if the buyer accepts liability for defects after inspection, a shortfall in quantity or specification remains the seller’s liability: the price is reduced proportionally and the buyer may rescind (Kuwait Finance House, p. 230)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t6.12"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"If goods are found inferior before the Murabaha is executed, what can happen?","options":["The client must still buy","The goods can be returned to the supplier and proper goods acquired","The bank charges a penalty","The promise lapses automatically"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 230."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t9.9.4","section":"9.9.4","title":"Defaults by the Clients","pages":[231,232],"tier":"core","concepts":["late-payment-penalty"],"intuition":"Without rescheduling at higher interest, how can a bank discourage deliberate late payment?","simpleExplanation":"Options: an acceleration clause (all instalments fall due on unjustified delay); a client undertaking to pay a late fee to charity (only for wilful defaulters). Only a court or independent body may award part as solatium to the bank for actual damage — not opportunity cost (SAB). Al Baraka Sudan’s fatwa lets banks keep a late fee at their realised profit rate; the author warns this is close to opportunity cost and hard to distinguish from interest-based trade finance.","academicExplanation":["Conventional rescheduling at higher rates motivates payment. Islamic options: stipulating that on delay without genuine reason all remaining instalments become due (AAOIFI 5/1); client’s undertaking to pay to charity on default. Consensus: banks may impose late fees on delinquent (not genuinely unable) clients, but proceeds go to charity; only a court or independent body can allocate a part as solatium. SAB: the legislature may empower courts to penalise defaulters and dilatory tactics, awarding part as solatium, with the State using the rest for charitable and public-interest projects. Compensation is for actual damage, not opportunity cost. Al Baraka Sudan’s Shari’ah board authorises late fees as income at the realised profit rate during default; the author: this is akin to opportunity cost and makes such Murabaha hard to differentiate from Western interest-based trade finance — differentiation is needed for credibility."],"examExplanation":"Default tools: acceleration clause; late fee to charity (wilful defaulters only); solatium only via court/independent body for actual damage. Al Baraka Sudan realised-rate fee criticised as akin to opportunity cost.","keyPoints":["Genuinely unable clients are not charged.","Author critical of realised-rate compensation.","Clients genuinely unable to pay are not charged a penalty.","Only a court or independent body may award part of a penalty as solatium to the bank."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Acceleration clause","kind":"textbook","text":"The agreement may say that if the client delays without genuine reason, all remaining instalments fall due — a discipline device without any increase in the debt (p. 231)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t7.13","t17.4.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Who may allocate part of a late-payment penalty to the bank as solatium, per the mainstream view?","options":["The bank itself","A court or independent body","The client","Any branch manager"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 231."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch9-t11"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch9-t11","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"9.9.4-9.9.6","sectionTitle":"Defaults by the Clients / Rebates on Early Payment / Rollover in Murabaha","title":"Murabaha Issues II: Default Penalties, Early-Payment Rebates, and the Prohibition on Rollover","covers":["t9.9.4","t9.9.5","t9.9.6"],"overview":"Explains how Islamic banks handle late payment (charity-directed penalties, not interest), why early-payment rebates are mostly disallowed as bank policy, and why 're-pricing' receivables (rollover) is explicit Riba.","simpleExplanation":"If a customer pays late, the bank can charge a penalty as a deterrent, but that money has to go to charity, not into the bank's own profit - because once the sale price is fixed, the bank has no further claim to extra money. Some scholars have gone further and allowed banks to keep late fees calculated as if the money had earned a return during the delay - but the book flags this as dangerously close to conventional interest. If a customer pays early, most scholars don't allow the bank to give a discount, though AAOIFI permits a rebate if it wasn't promised in the contract, at the bank's discretion. And 'rollover' - adding a new profit margin onto an unpaid Murabaha instead of just rescheduling - is explicit Riba, and flatly not allowed.","academicExplanation":"On defaults: unlike conventional banks (which reschedule debt at a higher rate), Islamic banks stipulate an acceleration clause (all remaining instalments become due upon unjustified delay) as a disciplinary device. Contemporary Shari'ah scholars have reached consensus that banks may impose late fees on delinquent clients (genuinely unable clients being exempted), but such penalty proceeds must go to charity - only a court or independent body can allocate any part as solatium (compensation) to the bank, per the Shariat Appellate Bench of Pakistan's Supreme Court (which allows legislatures to empower courts to award solatium from penalties, with the remainder for charitable/public-interest use). A more expansive Fatwah (Al Baraka Sudan) authorizes banks to treat late fees as their own income, calculated on the profit rate actually realized by the bank during the delay period (as if the delayed money had itself earned that return) - but the author cautions this is uncomfortably close to conventional opportunity-cost-based interest and threatens the credibility of the distinction between Islamic and Western trade financing. On early payment: the majority of contemporary scholars (OIC Fiqh Academy, Pakistan's Shariat Appellate Bench, Middle Eastern Shari'ah committees) do not permit a rebate for early payment in bank Murabaha, viewing it as functionally similar to conventional interest-based instalment discounting. AAOIFI's Murabaha Standard, however, allows a rebate if not already stipulated in the contract - purely at the bank's discretion, decided case-by-case with the Shari'ah advisor, never as routine practice. On rollover: 'rollover' means booking a fresh Murabaha against the unpaid receivable of a prior Murabaha, adding further mark-up to the defaulted amount - this is explicit Riba, since the bank has no right to re-price a receivable once ownership of the sold goods has already transferred to the client (the bank's claim is fixed at the original agreed price). Rescheduling (extending the payment period without increasing the amount) is allowed; re-pricing (rollover) is not. Any late-payment charge collected per the client's own undertaking goes to charity, not bank income; a genuinely fresh Murabaha (new goods) remains possible.","examExplanation":"Default: acceleration clause allowed (delay triggers full amount due); late fees allowed by consensus but must go to CHARITY, not bank profit (court/independent body may award partial solatium to bank); the Al Baraka Sudan Fatwah allowing banks to keep late fees as realized-profit-rate income is flagged as risky/interest-like by the author. Early payment: majority view disallows rebates (too interest-like); AAOIFI permits a discretionary, non-stipulated rebate. Rollover: booking a NEW Murabaha with added markup against an unpaid OLD Murabaha receivable = explicit Riba (bank cannot re-price a fixed receivable); rescheduling (same amount, later date) IS allowed; rollover (re-pricing) is NOT.","keyPoints":["Islamic banks may use an acceleration clause (full balance due on unjustified delay) as a discipline mechanism, since re-pricing/interest-style penalties are not available.","Late-payment penalties are permissible by contemporary scholarly consensus, but proceeds must go to charity, not bank profit - only a court/independent body can allocate any portion as solatium (compensation) to the bank.","The Al Baraka Sudan Fatwah permitting banks to retain late fees calculated on their realized profit rate during the delay period is presented critically - the author warns it resembles conventional opportunity-cost-based interest.","The majority of contemporary scholars disallow early-payment rebates in bank Murabaha (too similar to conventional discount-for-early-payment financing); AAOIFI allows a non-stipulated, purely discretionary rebate.","'Rollover' - booking a new Murabaha with added markup against a prior unpaid Murabaha receivable - is explicit Riba, because the bank has no right to re-price a receivable once the sold goods' ownership has already transferred.","Rescheduling (extending the payment date without increasing the amount owed) is permitted; rollover (re-pricing/adding markup) is not."],"definitions":[{"term":"Rollover (in Murabaha)","definition":"Booking a new Murabaha transaction against the unpaid receivable of a prior Murabaha, with further mark-up added to the defaulted amount - explicit Riba, since the bank has no right to re-price an already-fixed receivable."},{"term":"Solatium","definition":"Compensation that a court or independent body may award to a party (here, the bank) from penalty proceeds otherwise destined for charity, to offset actual loss or inconvenience caused by a defaulting party's delay tactics."}],"conditions":["Late-payment penalty proceeds must be used for charitable purposes; only a court or independent body may allocate a portion as solatium to the bank.","A Murabaha receivable, once fixed by the executed sale, cannot be re-priced or increased - rollover (adding fresh markup to a defaulted balance) is not permitted.","Rescheduling (extending the payment period without increasing the amount owed) is permitted."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students often conflate 'rescheduling' and 'rollover' - rescheduling (extending time, same amount) is permitted; rollover (adding a fresh profit margin to the unpaid amount) is explicit Riba and is not permitted."],"distinctions":["Late-payment charity penalty (permitted, goes to charity) vs conventional interest-based late fee (bank keeps the money as income) - the destination of the funds is the critical Shari'ah distinction.","Rescheduling (permitted: same total amount, later date) vs rollover (prohibited: new markup added to the receivable)."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Charity Account","Bai' al 'Inah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.4-9.9.6","pages":[231,232,233]}}],"debate":[{"issue":"May the bank keep late-payment charges as income?","criticism":"Al Baraka Sudan fatwa: banks may charge a late fee as income at the profit rate actually realised during the default period.","response":"The author: this is akin to conventional opportunity cost; such stretched Ijtihad makes Murabaha hard to distinguish from interest-based trade finance. Mainstream: fee to charity; any solatium only via court/independent body for actual damage.","takeaway":"For credibility, penalties go to charity; compensation only for proven actual loss."}]},{"id":"t9.9.5","section":"9.9.5","title":"Rebates on Early Payment","pages":[232,232],"tier":"core","concepts":["prepayment-rebate"],"intuition":"Can a client who pays early demand a rebate?","simpleExplanation":"The majority of scholars, the OIC Fiqh Academy, the SAB and Middle East boards do not allow contractual remission for early payment (like interest-based instalment sales). AAOIFI allows a rebate if not stipulated in the contract — at the bank’s discretion, case by case with the Shari’ah advisor, not as a practice.","academicExplanation":["Clients may wish to prepay and seek a rebate as in conventional banking. The majority of contemporary scholars, the OIC Fiqh Academy, Pakistan’s SAB, Middle East committees and scholars generally do not allow remission for early payment in bank Murabaha, considering it similar to interest-based instalment techniques. The AAOIFI standard allows a rebate not stipulated in the contract; the bank has discretion, but it should not become a practice and each case should be decided on merit with the Shari’ah advisor."],"examExplanation":"No contractual rebate (majority, OIC, SAB). AAOIFI: discretionary rebate if not stipulated; not a practice; Shari’ah advisor case by case.","keyPoints":["Discretion must not become custom."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Prepaying a Murabaha","kind":"textbook","text":"A client owing 1,200,000 in 12 months pays after 6 and asks for 60,000 off. A contractual rebate is not allowed (majority, OIC Fiqh Academy). Under AAOIFI the bank may give one at its discretion, case by case with its Shari’ah advisor, if it was never promised (p. 232)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A Murabaha contract may promise a rebate for early payment.","right":"Contractual rebates are not allowed; AAOIFI permits a discretionary rebate only if not stipulated, decided case by case with the Shari’ah advisor (p. 232)."}],"relatedTopics":["t7.12"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to AAOIFI, a rebate for early Murabaha payment is allowed when:","options":["It is written into the contract","It is not stipulated in the contract and is at the bank’s discretion","The client demands it","Never"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 232."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t9.9.6","section":"9.9.6","title":"Rollover in Murabaha","pages":[232,233],"tier":"core","concepts":["rollover","riba"],"intuition":"Can an unpaid Murabaha receivable be ‘rolled’ into a new Murabaha with more mark-up?","simpleExplanation":"No. Rollover — booking another Murabaha against unpaid receivables with further mark-up — is explicit Riba: the goods already belong to the client and the bank cannot re-price. Rescheduling without any increase is allowed; late-payment amounts go to charity. A fresh Murabaha is possible only through the sale of new goods.","academicExplanation":["Rollover means booking another Murabaha against receivables of a previous Murabaha that the client has not paid, adding further mark-up. This is explicit Riba: the bank is entitled to nothing over the debt created, ownership having passed to the client; the bank has no right of re-pricing. Rescheduling is allowed without increasing the original receivable; late-payment amounts per the client’s undertaking go to charity. A fresh Murabaha facility is possible through sale of new goods."],"examExplanation":"Rollover = explicit Riba (re-pricing sold goods/debt). Rescheduling without increase OK; charity for late amounts; new Murabaha only on new goods.","keyPoints":["Rescheduling ≠ re-pricing."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Reschedule, don’t re-price","kind":"textbook","text":"A client cannot pay an instalment. The bank may extend the schedule without raising the receivable, or later finance a fresh sale of new goods — but it may not book a new Murabaha on the old debt (pp. 232–233)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t6.10","t4.2.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A client cannot pay a Murabaha instalment; the bank books a new Murabaha on the same receivable with extra mark-up. This is:","options":["Permissible rescheduling","Rollover — explicit Riba","Tawarruq","Musawamah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 232."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"distinctions":[{"a":"Rescheduling","b":"Rollover","text":"Rescheduling extends time without increasing the receivable (allowed); rollover adds mark-up on the unpaid receivable (Riba)."}]},{"id":"t9.10","section":"9.10","title":"Precautions in Murabaha Operations (Boxes 9.1–9.4)","pages":[233,237],"tier":"core","concepts":["risk-management","murabaha"],"intuition":"What controls keep a Murabaha compliant and the bank safe?","simpleExplanation":"Murabaha brings asset, fiduciary, legal and Shari’ah compliance risks; strong documentation, AAOIFI standards and Shari’ah boards help. Box 9.1: promise + HJ for refusal; direct payment, invoice dating and inspection against ‘Inah; periodic offers against goods used before sale; Takaful for transit; charity undertaking for overdue; collateral for default; agent’s guarantee of supplier; related-party checks. Box 9.2: import Murabaha via L/C. Box 9.3: accounting. Box 9.4: export Murabaha steps.","academicExplanation":["Additional asset, fiduciary, legal and Shari’ah compliance risks; documentation under the legal department; adherence to AAOIFI; Shari’ah supervisory boards’ internal controls; mistiming in documentation may lose income.","Box 9.1 risk mitigation: customer refuses to buy after possession → promise and HJ; customer already bought (‘Inah) → direct payment via DD/PO, invoice dated not earlier than agency and not later than offer, other evidence (gate pass, stock register, truck receipt), physical inspection; goods used before offer → shorter intervals for offers, random inspection; transit destruction → bank owns risk, Takaful; overdue → charity undertaking; default → realise collateral; supplier non-performance → agent’s personal guarantee; related parties → check financial statements.","Box 9.2 import Murabaha: MoU and agency; customer negotiates as agent only after agency; L/C opened with Takaful on the bank’s behalf (cost borne by bank); exporter ships and documents reach the bank; customer negotiates FX rate; settlement — sight L/C without credit via spot Murabaha/Musawamah at L/C cost + charges + Takaful; or with financing — Murabaha executed, profit from the date Nostro was debited to settlement date; documents released and receivable recorded.","Box 9.3 accounting (AAOIFI): asset at historical cost; for binding promises, declines reflected; if costs may not be recovered, cash equivalent value with provision; post-acquisition discount reduces cost (revenue only if Shari’ah board decides); receivables at cash equivalent value; profit recognised at contracting if within the period, else proportionately (preferred) or when received; deferred profit offset against receivables; HJ a liability — returned in full for non-binding promises; actual loss deducted for binding; penalty to revenue or charity as the board deems; no extra penalty in insolvency.","Box 9.4 export Murabaha: agreement; exporter appointed agent; bank pays supplier; exporter buys and takes possession for the bank; exporter offers; bank accepts; exporter pays per schedule."],"examExplanation":"Risks: asset, fiduciary, legal, Shari’ah. Box 9.1 controls (promise+HJ; direct payment; invoice dates; inspection; Takaful; charity undertaking; collateral; agent guarantee; related parties). Box 9.2 import via L/C; Box 9.3 AAOIFI accounting (proportionate profit recognition preferred); Box 9.4 export steps.","keyPoints":["Invoice date must fall between agency agreement and offer.","Import Murabaha profit runs from Nostro debit date.","Proportionate allocation is the preferred profit recognition.","Box 9.3: Hamish Jiddiyah is a liability of the bank; supplier discounts reduce cost; profit on multi-period credit is preferably recognised proportionately."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Box 9.2: import Murabaha via L/C","kind":"textbook","text":"Master agreement and agency; client negotiates with the exporter as agent; bank opens the L/C with Takaful at its cost; on documents, either spot Murabaha/Musawamah (sight L/C) or deferred Murabaha with profit from the Nostro debit date (pp. 235–236)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t4.2.9","t14.3.4","t14.4.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"To guard against ‘Inah in MPO, Box 9.1 requires the supplier’s invoice to be dated:","options":["Before the agency agreement","Not earlier than the agency agreement and not later than the offer to purchase","After the Murabaha is executed","Any date"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 234."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch9-t13"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch9-t13","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"9.10","sectionTitle":"Precautions in Murabaha Operations","title":"Precautions, Risk Management and Practical Process Documentation in Murabaha","covers":["t9.10"],"overview":"Summarizes the additional risks Islamic banks face in Murabaha (asset, fiduciary, legal, Shari'ah compliance risk) and the practical risk-mitigation tools and process/accounting documentation the book illustrates (Boxes 9.1-9.4).","simpleExplanation":"Because Islamic banks actually own goods (even briefly) and rely on customers acting as their agents, Murabaha carries risks conventional lending doesn't: what if the customer refuses to buy after taking possession, what if goods get lost in transit, what if the customer used the goods before the sale was even finalized? The book lays out specific tools for each risk - like taking earnest money, requiring proof of purchase (invoices, delivery documents), physical inspections, and insurance (Takaful) - plus detailed accounting rules for how banks should record Murabaha assets, receivables and profit.","academicExplanation":"Murabaha exposes Islamic banks to additional asset risk, fiduciary risk (relying on the client as agent), legal risk, and Shari'ah compliance risk beyond conventional lending, requiring careful documentation (bank legal department), adherence to AAOIFI Shari'ah Standards, and strong Shari'ah supervisory board oversight. Box 9.1 (Risk Management in Murabaha) matches specific risks to mitigants: customer refusing to purchase after taking possession as agent -> binding promise + Hamish Jiddiyah (covers actual loss); customer having already purchased goods and seeking reimbursement (= Bai' al 'Inah risk) -> direct payment to supplier, invoice dated correctly (not before agency agreement, not after offer), additional evidence (gate pass, stock register, truck receipt), physical inspection; goods used by customer before offer/acceptance (so they don't 'exist' at Murabaha execution) -> shortened intervals for periodic offers, random physical inspection; transit destruction risk -> Takaful cover (goods are bank's risk during transit); overdue payment -> charity-bound undertaking from customer; default risk -> realizing securities/collateral; supplier non-performance -> agent's personal guarantee of supplier performance; related-party purchase/resale -> obtaining related-party disclosure from financial statements. Box 9.2 illustrates a detailed import-financing Murabaha process (master agreement + agency agreement, customer negotiates with foreign supplier as agent only after the agency agreement is signed, L/C opened with Takaful arranged, documents flow through the negotiating bank, then either spot Musawamah/Murabaha settlement or deferred Murabaha execution with profit calculated from the Nostro debit date). Box 9.3 sets out AAOIFI accounting treatment: Murabaha assets measured at historical cost (adjusted for value decline or netrealizable-value provisioning if recovery is doubtful); post-acquisition discounts reduce cost of goods (not automatically treated as revenue, unless the Shari'ah board decides otherwise); Murabaha receivables recorded at cash-equivalent value; profit recognized at contracting for cash/current-period credit sales, or via proportionate allocation (preferred) or cash-received-basis for multi-period credit; early settlement discounts may be deducted at or after settlement; Hamish Jiddiyah is a bank liability, returned in full for a nonbinding promise even if the asset is resold at a loss, but the actual loss is deducted from it for a binding promise (with any shortfall recorded as a receivable from the defaulting client); procrastination penalties are revenue or charity per Shari'ah board/arbitration decision; insolvent clients cannot be charged any additional penalty amount. Box 9.4 gives a parallel seven-step export-financing Murabaha process (exporter as agent purchasing goods on the bank's behalf, bank pays supplier, exporter takes possession and offers to purchase from the bank, bank accepts, exporter pays per schedule).","examExplanation":"Murabaha-specific risks: asset risk (goods risk during bank ownership window), fiduciary risk (client-as-agent reliability), legal/documentation risk, Shari'ah compliance risk. Key mitigants (Box 9.1): Hamish Jiddiyah for refusal-to-purchase; direct-to-supplier payment + correctly dated invoices + physical inspection for Bai' al 'Inah risk; Takaful for transit risk; charity-bound undertaking for late payment; collateral realization for default; agent's personal guarantee for supplier non-performance. Accounting (Box 9.3, AAOIFI): historical cost measurement; receivables at cash-equivalent value; profit recognized at contracting (cash/current period) or via proportionate allocation (preferred) for multi-period credit; Hamish Jiddiyah is a liability, fully returned for nonbinding promises, net of actual loss for binding ones.","keyPoints":["Murabaha exposes banks to added asset, fiduciary, legal, and Shari'ah compliance risk compared to conventional lending, requiring strong documentation and Shari'ah supervisory oversight.","Key risk mitigants: binding promise + Hamish Jiddiyah (covers actual loss) for refusal to purchase; direct-to-supplier payment and correctly dated invoices/delivery evidence to guard against Bai' al 'Inah; Takaful cover for transit risk; charity-bound customer undertakings for late payment.","AAOIFI accounting treatment: Murabaha assets are measured at historical cost (adjusted for decline in value); receivables are recorded at cash-equivalent value.","Profit recognition: recognized at contracting for cash sales or credit within the current financial period; for multi-period credit, the proportionate allocation method is preferred (recognizing profit gradually over the credit term), though cash-received-basis recognition is also allowed.","Hamish Jiddiyah is treated as a bank liability: fully returned to the customer for a nonbinding promise (even if the asset is later sold at a loss to someone else), but net of the bank's actual loss for a binding promise, with any shortfall recorded as a receivable from the defaulting client.","An insolvent client cannot be charged any additional penalty amount by the Islamic bank."],"definitions":[{"term":"Proportionate Allocation Method","definition":"The preferred AAOIFI method for recognizing Murabaha profit on multi-period credit sales, spreading profit recognition across the credit term rather than all at once, regardless of whether cash has actually been received."}],"conditions":["Murabaha invoice dates must not be earlier than the agency agreement date, nor later than the customer's declaration/offer to purchase.","Hamish Jiddiyah is returned in full to the customer under a nonbinding promise, even if the goods are later resold at a loss; under a binding promise, only the actual loss is deducted from it.","An Islamic bank cannot charge an insolvent client any additional penalty amount."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Box 9.4 - Murabaha Financing for Exports (7 steps)","kind":"textbook","text":"1. Exporter and bank sign an agreement to enter Murabaha. 2. Exporter is appointed agent to purchase goods on the bank's behalf. 3. Bank pays the supplier/vendor. 4. Exporter purchases the goods on the bank's behalf and takes possession. 5. Exporter offers to purchase the goods from the bank. 6. Bank accepts the offer; sale is concluded. 7. Exporter pays the agreed price to the bank per the agreed schedule."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume Hamish Jiddiyah is always forfeited on customer default. It is only used to cover the bank's ACTUAL loss under a binding promise; under a nonbinding promise it is returned in full regardless of what happens to the goods."],"distinctions":["A nonbinding promise (Hamish Jiddiyah fully refundable regardless of resale outcome) vs a binding promise (Hamish Jiddiyah net of the bank's actual loss, with any shortfall becoming a receivable)."],"relatedConcepts":["Hamish Jiddiyah","Takaful","Bai' al 'Inah"],"examRelevance":"detailed","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.10","pages":[233,234,235,236,237,238]}}],"table":{"caption":"Box 9.1 — Risk management in Murabaha","head":["Risk","Mitigation"],"rows":[["Customer refuses to buy after possession as agent","Promise to purchase; Hamish Jiddiyah for actual loss"],["Customer already bought the goods (‘Inah)","Direct payment to supplier; invoice dated after agency and before offer; other evidence; physical inspection"],["Goods used before offer and acceptance","Shorter offer intervals; random inspection"],["Destruction in transit before sale","Bank bears risk; Takaful cover"],["Overdue","Customer’s undertaking to pay to charity"],["Default","Realise securities/collateral"],["Supplier non-performance","Agent guarantees supplier performance personally"],["Purchase from/resale to associates","Related-party information from financial statements"]]}},{"id":"t9.11","section":"9.11","title":"Musawamah (Bargaining on Price)","pages":[234,234],"tier":"core","concepts":["musawamah"],"intuition":"What if the seller can’t, or doesn’t want to, disclose cost?","simpleExplanation":"Musawamah is an ordinary sale where the price is bargained without reference to the seller’s cost. The seller need not reveal cost; all other Murabaha conditions apply. It suits cases where cost can’t be ascertained precisely, and where banks obtain supplier discounts on retail goods (since actual profit isn’t disclosed, it must be Musawamah, not Murabaha).","academicExplanation":["Musawamah is a general sale in which price is bargained without reference to price paid or cost; the seller is not obliged to reveal cost; other Murabaha conditions apply. It can be used where the seller cannot precisely ascertain costs. It may be cash or credit; banks generally use deferred-payment Musawamah, adding their margin without disclosing cost and profit. Where IFIs obtain a discount from the supplier on retail goods not disclosed to the customer, the sale should be Musawamah, not Murabaha."],"examExplanation":"Musawamah: price bargained, no cost disclosure; other conditions same as Murabaha; use when cost unascertainable or undisclosed supplier discounts.","keyPoints":["Undisclosed supplier discount → Musawamah."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Supplier discount on retail goods","kind":"textbook","text":"If a bank obtains a discount from the supplier that it does not disclose to the customer, the sale must be done as Musawamah, not Murabaha (p. 234)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t9.3","t9.11.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A bank gets an undisclosed volume discount from a supplier on retail goods. Which sale should it use?","options":["Murabaha","Musawamah","Tawliyah","Salam"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 234."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch9-t14"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch9-t14","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"9.11-9.11.1","sectionTitle":"Musawamah (Bargaining on Price) / Musawamah as a Mode of Financing","title":"Musawamah: Bargaining on Price as a Financing Mode","covers":["t9.11","t9.11.1"],"overview":"Explains Musawamah's definition, how it operates as a bank financing technique without cost disclosure, and when banks prefer it over Murabaha.","simpleExplanation":"Musawamah is just ordinary haggling - buyer and seller agree on a final price without the seller ever revealing what they originally paid. Banks generally prefer Murabaha because it's easier to standardize and manage (transparent profit margin tied to a benchmark), but Musawamah is better for big one-off deals where disclosing exact costs isn't practical, or when a bank has gotten a supplier discount it doesn't want to reveal to the customer.","academicExplanation":"Musawamah is a general, regular sale in which price is bargained between buyer and seller with no reference to the seller's cost - unlike Murabaha, the seller is under no obligation to reveal cost; all other Murabaha-type conditions still apply. It suits sellers who cannot precisely ascertain the cost of goods they're offering. Juristically, Musawamah can be cash or credit, but bank use is generally deferred-payment, where banks bargain the final price (embedding their margin) without disclosing cost/profit breakdown. Where an Islamic financial institution obtains a supplier discount on retail goods without disclosing it to the customer, that sale must be structured as Musawamah, not Murabaha (since Murabaha strictly requires accurate cost/profit disclosure). Businesses generally use Musawamah (interested only in final pricing), while Islamic banks predominantly use Murabaha, because a benchmark-tied profit margin is administratively easier to manage, more transparent for regulators, and minimizes corruption risk (profit is always tied to a disclosed cost basis). Musawamah is better suited to large single transactions where minute cost details aren't practical or aren't desired to be disclosed - illustrated by a hypothetical aircraft purchase ($500m) where the bank buys at $450m and sells at $500m (adding a $50m margin) without disclosing the $450m cost to the airline. The agency structure can also apply to Musawamah, though direct bank purchase is preferable, particularly for expensive assets; for high-value assets the structure is 'Musawamah to Purchase Orderer,' while for smaller/routine goods, banks or subsidiaries may maintain inventory for Musawamah sale as demanded. All conditions regarding ownership, possession, business risk, subject matter, price payment, and default treatment apply identically to Musawamah as to Murabaha - the sole difference is that cost/profit margin disclosure is not required, and bargaining focuses on the final price only.","examExplanation":"Musawamah = bargained final price, NO cost disclosure obligation (vs Murabaha's mandatory cost disclosure); all other Murabaha conditions (ownership, possession, risk-bearing before sale) apply identically. Banks prefer Murabaha generally (easier benchmark-tied margin management, more transparent, lower corruption risk), but use Musawamah for large single transactions (e.g. aircraft) or where a supplier discount shouldn't be disclosed to the customer. Structure options mirror Murabaha: 'Musawamah to Purchase Orderer' for high-value assets, inventory-based Musawamah sale for smaller/routine goods.","keyPoints":["Musawamah = an ordinary bargained-price sale where the seller has NO obligation to disclose the original cost - the sole structural difference from Murabaha, which requires mandatory cost disclosure.","All other conditions (ownership/possession/risk-bearing by the seller before sale, subject matter rules, default treatment) apply identically to Musawamah as to Murabaha.","If a bank receives an undisclosed supplier discount/rebate on retail goods, the resale to the customer must be structured as Musawamah, not Murabaha, since Murabaha strictly requires accurate cost/profit disclosure.","Banks predominantly prefer Murabaha over Musawamah because a benchmark-tied, cost-disclosed profit margin is administratively easier to manage, more transparent to regulators, and carries lower corruption risk.","Musawamah is particularly suited to large single transactions (e.g. aircraft purchase) where detailed cost disclosure is impractical or undesired, and to situations where a bank's actual cost/margin should remain confidential."],"definitions":[{"term":"Musawamah","definition":"A general kind of sale in which the price of the traded commodity is bargained between seller and buyer without any reference to the price paid or cost incurred by the seller."}],"conditions":["All conditions of a valid sale and of Murabaha-type transactions (ownership, possession, business risk before sale, subject matter eligibility, default treatment) apply equally to Musawamah, except the requirement to disclose cost."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Aircraft Purchase via Musawamah","kind":"textbook","text":"An airline needs credit purchase of an aeroplane costing about $500 million. An Islamic bank may purchase the aircraft for $450 million and sell it to the airline after adding a $50 million profit (factoring in the credit period and payment schedule), without needing to disclose the $450 million cost basis - the airline is interested only in the final agreed price."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think Musawamah is a 'lesser' or informal version of Murabaha. It is an equally valid, independent sale contract with identical ownership/risk requirements - the only difference is the absence of a cost-disclosure obligation."],"distinctions":["Murabaha (mandatory cost disclosure, cost-plus pricing) vs Musawamah (no cost disclosure, purely bargained final price) - otherwise governed by identical Shari'ah conditions on ownership, possession and risk."],"relatedConcepts":["Murabaha","Bai' al Amanah","Musawamah to Purchase Orderer"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.11-9.11.1","pages":[234,238]},"calculations":[{"title":"Aircraft Financing via Musawamah — Cost-Plus Economics (Undisclosed)","formula":"Sale Price = Acquisition Cost + Negotiated Profit — but unlike Murabaha, neither figure need be disclosed to the buyer, only the final price","inputs":[{"label":"Bank's acquisition cost","value":"$450,000,000"},{"label":"Profit added (reflecting credit period and payment schedule)","value":"$50,000,000"}],"steps":["Bank purchases the aircraft from the seller for $450 million, taking ownership and risk before any resale.","Bank and airline bargain a final Musawamah sale price: $450M + $50M = $500 million.","Implied profit = Sale Price − Cost = $500M − $450M = $50 million.","Effective markup on cost = $50M ÷ $450M ≈ 11.1%."],"result":"$500 million final sale price; $50 million profit to the bank, an effective markup of about 11.1% on its cost.","interpretation":"Because this is Musawamah, not Murabaha, the bank is under no obligation to reveal its $450 million cost basis — only the bargained $500 million price is disclosed to the airline. In a Murabaha for the same aircraft, the $450M cost and $50M profit margin would both have to be disclosed.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.11-9.11.1","pages":[234,238]}}]}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Murabaha","b":"Musawamah","text":"Murabaha requires disclosure of cost and agreement on margin; Musawamah bargains the final price without disclosure. Ownership, possession and risk conditions are the same."}]},{"id":"t9.11.1","section":"9.11.1","title":"Musawamah as a Mode of Financing","pages":[238,238],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["musawamah"],"intuition":"Why do banks prefer Murabaha but businesses prefer Musawamah?","simpleExplanation":"Banks prefer Murabaha because benchmarks are easy to apply, regulators can manage return structures, and corruption is less likely (margin tied to cost). Musawamah suits huge single transactions (e.g. an airline buying a $500m plane that the bank buys for $450m and sells with $50m profit) or where many cost details can’t be disclosed. Bank should preferably buy expensive assets directly; ownership, possession and risk rules are the same.","academicExplanation":["Businesses use Musawamah, earning through pricing; banks mostly use Murabaha because benchmarks are easier to apply, return structures easier for management and regulators, and corruption minimal as margin is tied to cost. Musawamah suits single huge transactions decided at top level — e.g. an airline needs a $500 million aeroplane; the bank buys for $450 million and sells for $500 million considering credit period; cost details are not needed. Agency may be used, but the bank should preferably buy expensive assets directly, involving clients in selecting supplier and specifications — ‘Musawamah to Purchase Orderer’ for huge assets; subsidiaries may hold inventory of less expensive goods. Conditions of ownership, possession, risk, subject matter, price and default treatment are the same; only cost/profit disclosure is not required."],"examExplanation":"Banks prefer Murabaha (benchmarks, regulation, less corruption). Musawamah for huge single deals (airline example) or undisclosable costs; same ownership/risk rules.","keyPoints":["Airline example: bank buys at $450m, sells at $500m.","Musawamah to Purchase Orderer for expensive assets."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Aircraft Musawamah","kind":"textbook","text":"A Middle East airline needs a $500 million aeroplane on credit. An Islamic bank buys it for $450 million and sells it to the airline adding $50 million profit considering the credit period; the airline is interested only in the final price."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t9.11","t14.3.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why do Islamic banks generally prefer Murabaha over Musawamah?","options":["Musawamah is prohibited","Benchmarks are easier to apply and corruption chances minimal since margin ties to cost","Murabaha needs no ownership","Regulators ban Musawamah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 238."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"distinctions":[{"a":"Why banks prefer Murabaha","b":"Where Musawamah fits","text":"Murabaha allows easy benchmarking, regulation and less corruption because margin is tied to cost; Musawamah suits single large deals (e.g. an aircraft) or cases where cost details cannot be given (p. 238)."}]}],"questions":[{"id":"q9.1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which is the defining feature of Murabaha?","options":["Profit and loss sharing","Disclosure of cost and agreement on a profit margin","Leasing of usufruct","Advance payment for future delivery"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 215.","topicId":"t9.3","chapter":9,"section":"9.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define Murabaha","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.3","pages":[215,215]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.2","type":"multi","prompt":"Which may be the subject of a Murabaha? (Select all.)","options":["Machinery","Royalty rights","Gold coins on deferred payment","Screened shares","A promissory note"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3],"explanation":"Gold (Sarf) and debt documents are excluded; shares allowed with screening (pp. 217, 220).","topicId":"t9.6","chapter":9,"section":"9.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify eligible Murabaha goods","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6","pages":[217,219]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.3","type":"order","prompt":"Order the steps of MPO with the customer as the bank’s agent.","items":["Customer requests purchase","Bank appoints customer as agent","Customer purchases on bank’s behalf; bank pays supplier","Customer takes delivery as agent","Customer offers to buy; bank accepts","Customer pays the deferred price"],"explanation":"p. 225.","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Sequence MPO","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[225,229]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.4","type":"scenario","prompt":"A client, acting as the bank’s agent, collected raw materials and began using them in production before sending the bank an offer to purchase. What is the problem?","options":["None","The goods must exist at execution; using them before offer and acceptance creates a Shari’ah objection","The bank must charge more","The agency lapses automatically"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 228–229.","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Identify MPO compliance failures","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[225,229]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.5","type":"tf","prompt":"A client that owns 60% of the nominated supplier can be the counterparty supplier in an MPO.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"The supplier must not be an entity more than 50% owned by the customer (p. 227).","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply supplier independence rule","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[225,229]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.6","type":"application","prompt":"Under a binding promise, a bank bought goods for $50,000; the client refused to buy; the bank resold them for $48,500 and holds $3,000 Hamish Jiddiyah. How much should it refund?","options":["$0","$1,500","$3,000","$1,000"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Actual loss $1,500 deducted; refund $1,500. (Practice example — generated for learning.)","topicId":"t9.8.2","chapter":9,"section":"9.8.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply Hamish Jiddiyah rules","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.2","pages":[224,225]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.7","type":"identify","prompt":"Booking a new Murabaha with additional mark-up on an unpaid Murabaha receivable is:","options":["Rescheduling","Rollover (Riba)","Musawamah","Hawalah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 232.","topicId":"t9.9.6","chapter":9,"section":"9.9.6","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify rollover","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.6","pages":[232,233]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.8","type":"comparison","prompt":"Which statement correctly compares Murabaha and Musawamah?","options":["Only Musawamah requires the seller to own the goods","Murabaha requires cost disclosure; Musawamah does not; other conditions are the same","Musawamah cannot be on credit","Murabaha has no fixed price"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 234.","topicId":"t9.11","chapter":9,"section":"9.11","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Compare Murabaha and Musawamah","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.11","pages":[234,234]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.9","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the author criticise the Al Baraka Sudan approach to late fees?","options":["It gives nothing to the bank","Charging the realised profit rate is akin to opportunity cost, blurring the difference from interest-based finance","It violates AAOIFI accounting","It requires court approval"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 232.","topicId":"t9.9.4","chapter":9,"section":"9.9.4","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate default compensation","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.4","pages":[231,232]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.10","type":"match","prompt":"Match each Murabaha risk to its mitigation (Box 9.1).","pairs":[["Client refuses to buy","Promise + Hamish Jiddiyah"],["Goods destroyed in transit","Takaful cover (bank’s risk)"],["Client already bought the goods","Direct payment and invoice-date checks"],["Overdue payment","Charity undertaking"]],"explanation":"Box 9.1, pp. 234–235.","topicId":"t9.10","chapter":9,"section":"9.10","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply Murabaha risk controls","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.10","pages":[233,237]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.11","type":"tf","prompt":"Natural gas supplied through pipelines is generally approved for Murabaha because constructive possession is easy to establish.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Scholars normally do not approve it — the point of risk transfer cannot be identified (p. 228).","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Apply risk-transfer requirement","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[225,229]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.12","type":"short","prompt":"Explain why the ‘two sales in one’ objection to MPO is rejected by contemporary jurists.","keywords":["unilateral","promise","contract","ownership"],"correctAnswer":"The objection holds that the binding promise plus the sale deed equals two contracts in one. Jurists reply that the client’s promise is unilateral and does not become a formal contract; the implications of a promise and a sale differ, ownership passes only at the later Murabaha, and no major Shari’ah principle is violated.","explanation":"Section 9.9.","topicId":"t9.9","chapter":9,"section":"9.9","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate objections to MPO","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9","pages":[229,233]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.13","type":"scenario","prompt":"An airline wants to buy a $500m aircraft on credit and is only interested in the final price. Which mode fits best according to Section 9.11.1?","options":["Murabaha with full cost disclosure","Musawamah (possibly to purchase orderer)","Salam","Qard"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 238.","topicId":"t9.11.1","chapter":9,"section":"9.11.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Select mode","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.11.1","pages":[238,238]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.14","type":"mcq","prompt":"On whose request and promise is modern bank Murabaha (MPO) based?","options":["The supplier","The customer (purchase orderer)","The central bank","The Shari’ah board"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 213.","topicId":"t9.1","chapter":9,"section":"9.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define MPO","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.1","pages":[213,214]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.15","type":"scenario","prompt":"On 1 January A tells B: “I sell you my car on 1 February.” What is the status?","options":["Valid forward sale","Void — a sale must be instant; A may give a promise and execute the sale on 1 February","Valid Salam","Valid if B pays now"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 214.","topicId":"t9.2","chapter":9,"section":"9.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the instant-sale condition","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.2","pages":[214,215]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.16","type":"match","prompt":"Match each trust sale to its pricing.","pairs":[["Tawliyah","At cost, no profit or loss"],["Wadhi‘ah / Mohatah","At a discount on cost"],["Murabaha","Cost plus agreed profit"]],"explanation":"p. 215.","topicId":"t9.3","chapter":9,"section":"9.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Classify trust sales","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.3","pages":[215,215]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.17","type":"identify","prompt":"Which jurist defined Murabaha as “the sale of anything for the price at which it was purchased by the seller and an addition of a fixed sum by way of profit”?","options":["Ibn Qudama","Al-Marghinani","Imam Malik","Ibn Rushd"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 216.","topicId":"t9.4","chapter":9,"section":"9.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall classical definitions","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.4","pages":[215,216]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.18","type":"tf","prompt":"According to Imam Malik, Murabaha involves no credit — goods and price with profit are exchanged there and then.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"Imam Shafi‘i extended it to credit (p. 216).","topicId":"t9.4","chapter":9,"section":"9.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Compare classical views","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.4","pages":[215,216]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.19","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to Al-Marghinani, what was the original purpose of Murabaha?","options":["To finance governments","To protect inexperienced buyers from cunning traders through honest cost disclosure","To replace Salam","To avoid Zakat"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 216.","topicId":"t9.5","chapter":9,"section":"9.5","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the classical rationale","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.5","pages":[216,217]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.20","type":"multi","prompt":"Which expenses may be added to Murabaha cost according to the Malikis? (Select all.)","options":["Expenses that directly affect the object","Outsourced services like transport and storage","Packing done by the seller himself","Sales commission for the seller’s own work"],"correctAnswer":[0,1],"explanation":"Malikis exclude services the seller could have provided himself (pp. 217–218).","topicId":"t9.6","chapter":9,"section":"9.6","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply school views on cost","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6","pages":[217,219]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.21","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank receives a supplier rebate a month after selling the goods to its client on Murabaha. Who benefits?","options":["The bank keeps it","The client, since the cost basis falls","Charity","The supplier"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"AAOIFI (p. 218).","topicId":"t9.6","chapter":9,"section":"9.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the rebate rule","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6","pages":[217,219]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.22","type":"comparison","prompt":"How do the schools differ when a Murabaha seller misstates his cost?","options":["All void the sale","Malik: buyer may rescind unless the difference is returned; Hanafis: unqualified option to rescind; Hanbalis: binding after the difference is returned","All require a penalty","No remedy exists"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 218.","topicId":"t9.6","chapter":9,"section":"9.6","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare remedies for misstatement","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6","pages":[217,219]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.23","type":"tf","prompt":"Murabaha on a deferred basis is not allowed for gold, silver or currencies.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"They fall under Bai‘ al Sarf (p. 220).","topicId":"t9.6.1","chapter":9,"section":"9.6.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall excluded subjects","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6.1","pages":[219,220]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.24","type":"short","prompt":"Why is the higher credit price in Murabaha–Mu’ajjal not Riba?","keywords":["like for like","goods","Ghunm","fixed"],"correctAnswer":"In a loan, like is exchanged for like, so any excess is Riba. In a credit sale, money is exchanged for goods — different counter values — and the whole price is for the item. Prices may rise or fall, so profit goes with loss (Al-Ghunm bil Ghurm). The price must be fixed once at the contract and never increased afterwards.","explanation":"p. 220.","topicId":"t9.6.1","chapter":9,"section":"9.6.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain credit pricing","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6.1","pages":[219,220]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.25","type":"identify","prompt":"Which Murabaha structure is safest for the bank’s commodity risk but most likely to become a back door to interest?","options":["Direct trading by the bank","Third-party agent","Client as the bank’s agent","Commodity exchange"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 222.","topicId":"t9.7","chapter":9,"section":"9.7","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Compare Murabaha structures","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.7","pages":[220,222]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.26","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank pays a supplier for machinery that the client had already received and owns. What has happened, according to Chapter 9?","options":["A valid Murabaha","A loan to the client; any profit is interest (and ‘Inah if resold)","A Salam","An Ijarah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 222.","topicId":"t9.7","chapter":9,"section":"9.7","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Identify disguised loans","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.7","pages":[220,222]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.27","type":"multi","prompt":"Why do Islamic banks use MPO instead of holding inventory? (Select all.)","options":["Inventory costs are high","The list of goods is too long to stock","Clients need specific goods","Regulators restrict trading as core business","Shari’ah forbids inventory"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,3],"explanation":"Inventory is allowed; the reasons are practical (p. 222).","topicId":"t9.8","chapter":9,"section":"9.8","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the need for MPO","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8","pages":[222,224]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.28","type":"tf","prompt":"The bank may enter a Musharakah with the client on the promise that one partner will buy the other’s share by Murabaha.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Not allowed; a partner may promise to buy the other’s share at market or agreed price by a separate contract (p. 223).","topicId":"t9.8","chapter":9,"section":"9.8","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply AAOIFI limits on MPO","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8","pages":[222,224]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.29","type":"order","prompt":"Order the three contracts in MPO.","items":["Master agreement with the client’s unilateral promise to purchase","Agency contract to buy and take possession for the bank","Actual Murabaha contract once the bank owns the goods"],"explanation":"p. 224.","topicId":"t9.8.1","chapter":9,"section":"9.8.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Sequence MPO contracts","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.1","pages":[224,224]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.30","type":"application","prompt":"In MPO the bank wants protection if the client refuses to buy. Which tools are allowed? ","options":["Charge cost of funds","Binding unilateral promise with recovery of actual loss, Hamish Jiddiyah, or buying from the supplier with an option to return","Automatic interest penalty","Selling the goods before buying them"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 224–225.","topicId":"t9.8.2","chapter":9,"section":"9.8.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply risk tools in MPO","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.2","pages":[224,225]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.31","type":"match","prompt":"Match each MPO stage to the parties’ relationship.","pairs":[["Agency","Principal and agent"],["Purchase from supplier","Buyer and seller (bank and supplier)"],["Murabaha sale","Seller and buyer (bank and client)"],["After sale","Creditor and debtor"]],"explanation":"p. 226.","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Map relationships across MPO","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[225,229]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.32","type":"scenario","prompt":"The client signed the purchase contract with the supplier before the agency agreement was signed with the bank. What is the problem?","options":["None","The goods already belong to the client, so the deal becomes Bai‘ al ‘Inah","The price is uncertain","It becomes Ijarah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 228.","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply sequencing rules","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[225,229]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.33","type":"tf","prompt":"An invoice for Murabaha goods should be dated after the agency agreement and before the client’s offer to purchase.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"Box 9.1 (p. 234).","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall documentation checks","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[225,229]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.34","type":"comparison","prompt":"How do contemporary jurists answer the objection that MPO is “two sales in one”?","options":["They accept the objection","A unilateral promise is not a contract; it has different legal effects, so there is only one sale","They ban MPO","They require two prices"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 230.","topicId":"t9.9","chapter":9,"section":"9.9","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate the two-sales objection","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9","pages":[229,233]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.35","type":"multi","prompt":"Which precautions apply to shares Murabaha? (Select all.)","options":["Pay brokers directly","Do not appoint the client as agent","Wait for actual transfer of shares to the bank","Avoid shares of the client’s sister concerns","Sell before settlement to save time"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,3],"explanation":"p. 233.","topicId":"t9.9","chapter":9,"section":"9.9","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply precautions in shares Murabaha","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9","pages":[229,233]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.36","type":"identify","prompt":"Which body’s position is that Bai‘ al ‘Inah is impermissible, contrary to Malaysian practice?","options":["AAOIFI and mainstream Middle-East scholars","Only the IMF","Bank Negara","No one"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"p. 230.","topicId":"t9.9.1","chapter":9,"section":"9.9.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall views on ‘Inah","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.1","pages":[230,230]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.37","type":"scenario","prompt":"After inspection, the client finds 10% of the goods missing. What remedy applies even if the client accepted liability for defects?","options":["None","Price reduced proportionally and option to rescind — quantity shortfalls stay with the seller","Full refund only","A new Murabaha"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 230.","topicId":"t9.9.2","chapter":9,"section":"9.9.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply Khiyar in Murabaha","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.2","pages":[230,231]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.38","type":"mcq","prompt":"When should the Murabaha sale be executed?","options":["At the time of the promise","Only after the bank owns and possesses the goods and bears their risk","Before the agency contract","When the client pays"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 231.","topicId":"t9.9.4","chapter":9,"section":"9.9.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall timing of execution","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.4","pages":[231,232]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.39","type":"application","prompt":"A solvent client deliberately delays payment. Which of these may the bank do?","options":["Add the penalty to its income","Enforce an undertaking to pay a sum to charity and, through a court, seek actual-loss compensation","Increase the Murabaha price","Book a rollover"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 231–232.","topicId":"t9.9.4","chapter":9,"section":"9.9.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply default remedies","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.4","pages":[231,232]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.40","type":"tf","prompt":"The bank may reschedule an unpaid Murabaha receivable without increasing it.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"Rescheduling yes, re-pricing no (p. 233).","topicId":"t9.9.6","chapter":9,"section":"9.9.6","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish rescheduling from rollover","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.6","pages":[232,233]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.41","type":"multi","prompt":"Which risk mitigants appear in Box 9.1? (Select all.)","options":["Direct payment to the supplier by DD/PO","Physical inspection of goods","Takaful during transit","Related-party checks","Charging opportunity cost"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,3],"explanation":"pp. 234–235.","topicId":"t9.10","chapter":9,"section":"9.10","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Murabaha risk mitigants","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.10","pages":[233,237]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.42","type":"identify","prompt":"Under Box 9.3, how should Hamish Jiddiyah be recorded by the bank?","options":["As revenue","As a liability","As equity","As a provision"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 237.","topicId":"t9.10","chapter":9,"section":"9.10","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall accounting for token money","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.10","pages":[233,237]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q9.43","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank receives an undisclosed trade discount from a retail supplier. Which sale should it use?","options":["Murabaha","Musawamah","Salam","Tawliyah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 234.","topicId":"t9.11","chapter":9,"section":"9.11","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Choose between Murabaha and Musawamah","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.11","pages":[234,234]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q-ch9-1","type":"mcq","prompt":"How does an Islamic bank's involvement in trading differ fundamentally from a conventional bank's?","explanation":"The chapter states Islamic banks must be involved in the sale/purchase process per Shari'ah trading rules and are entitled to profit by undertaking business risk, unlike conventional banks which simply finance trading businesses.","topicId":"t9.1","chapter":9,"section":"9.1","topicLabel":"Trade-Based Financing: Musawamah and Murabaha as Forms of Credit Sale","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"State the general conditions of a valid Bai' and explain the specific conditions of Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.1","pages":[213]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Islamic banks only provide funds without ever taking title to goods","Islamic banks must be genuine parties to sale and purchase, taking title and bearing business risk like real-sector businesses","Islamic banks are prohibited from ever selling goods","There is no meaningful difference"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch9-2","type":"tf","prompt":"Constructive possession (ownership risk transferred, goods under buyer's control) is sufficient to satisfy the Shari'ah possession requirement even without physical delivery.","explanation":"True - the chapter explains that constructive possession, widely accepted by Shari'ah scholars, satisfies the possession requirement even without physical handover.","topicId":"t9.2","chapter":9,"section":"9.2","topicLabel":"Conditions of a Valid Bai' (Recap)","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the general conditions of a valid Bai'.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.2","pages":[214]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":true},{"id":"q-ch9-3","type":"scenario","prompt":"A sells to B a car which A intends to purchase from C, who still owns it. Under the conditions of valid Bai', what is the status of this sale?","explanation":"The subject of sale must be owned by the seller at the time of sale; since A does not own the car (C does), the sale is void - this directly underlies why a bank must own goods before Murabaha resale.","topicId":"t9.2","chapter":9,"section":"9.2","topicLabel":"Conditions of a Valid Bai' (Recap)","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"State the general conditions of a valid Bai'.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.2","pages":[214]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Valid, since A has a genuine intention to acquire the car","Void, because A does not own the car at the time of sale","Valid, provided A pays C before B pays A","Valid only if B consents in writing"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch9-4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Match each fiduciary sale type to its pricing rule: (1) Tawliyah; (2) Wadhi'ah/Mohatah; (3) Murabaha.","explanation":"Tawliyah = resale at the stated original price, no profit or loss; Wadhi'ah/Mohatah = resale at a discount from cost; Murabaha = sale with a fixed profit margin over cost.","topicId":"t9.3","chapter":9,"section":"9.3","topicLabel":"Murabaha as a Fiduciary Sale and Classical Definitions","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish Murabaha, Musawamah, Tawliyah and Wadhi'ah.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.3","pages":[215]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"matching","options":["1-resale at original price (no profit), 2-resale at a discount, 3-resale at cost plus disclosed profit","1-resale at a discount, 2-resale at cost plus profit, 3-resale at original price","1-resale at cost plus profit, 2-resale at original price, 3-resale at a discount","All three are the same as Musawamah"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch9-5","type":"identify","prompt":"Which classical jurist held that Murabaha, as originally understood, involves no credit and is completed by simultaneous exchange of goods and price?","explanation":"According to Imam Malik, Murabaha is conducted and completed by exchanging goods and price, including a mutually agreed profit margin, then and there - no credit involved.","topicId":"t9.4","chapter":9,"section":"9.4","topicLabel":"Murabaha as a Fiduciary Sale and Classical Definitions","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish Murabaha, Musawamah, Tawliyah and Wadhi'ah.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.4","pages":[216]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Al-Marghinani","Ibn Qudama","Imam Malik","Imam Shafi'e"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch9-6","type":"short","prompt":"What was the classical rationale for Murabaha, per Al-Marghinani, and why did Imam Ahmad prefer Musawamah instead?","explanation":"This reflects the classical protective rationale and Imam Ahmad's stated preference discussed in section 9.5.","topicId":"t9.5","chapter":9,"section":"9.5","topicLabel":"The Original Purpose of Murabaha","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain why classical jurists saw Murabaha as a protective, restricted sale.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.5","pages":[216]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Al-Marghinani held Murabaha's purpose was to protect innocent, unskilled buyers from cunning traders by letting them rely on a known-honest dealer's cost disclosure rather than personal bargaining. Imam Ahmad preferred Musawamah because Murabaha requires trust in the seller's honest cost disclosure, creating risk that selfishness could lead to a false statement or exploitation."},{"id":"q-ch9-7","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following CANNOT be the subject matter of a Murabaha sale, per the chapter?","explanation":"Currency/monetary units cannot be sold through Murabaha (Bai' al Sarf requires simultaneous exchange), and debt instruments cannot be Murabaha's subject matter (debt cannot generally be sold, and profit on debt is Riba). Rights/royalties and tangible goods ARE eligible.","topicId":"t9.6","chapter":9,"section":"9.6","topicLabel":"Specific Conditions of Murabaha","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the specific additional conditions that apply to Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6","pages":[217]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Rights and royalties","Currency and monetary units (subject to Bai' al Sarf)","Debt instruments/credit documents representing someone's debt","Tangible consumer goods"],"correctAnswer":[1,2]},{"id":"q-ch9-8","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which school's view on addable Murabaha expenses is most permissive, allowing all expenses by mutual consent provided they are disclosed?","explanation":"The Hanbalis' view is described as more pragmatic - all expenses can be added with mutual consent, provided the buyer is informed of the breakdown.","topicId":"t9.6","chapter":9,"section":"9.6","topicLabel":"Specific Conditions of Murabaha","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the specific additional conditions that apply to Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6","pages":[217,218]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Hanafi","Maliki","Shafi'e","Hanbali"],"correctAnswer":3},{"id":"q-ch9-9","type":"tf","prompt":"A Murabaha contract can validly offer the buyer two different prices - one for cash payment, another (higher) if payment is deferred - without the buyer having to select one at contract signing.","explanation":"False - a certain price must be stipulated once and for all; while the bank can offer options, the buyer must select ONE definite price at the time the contract is finalized. Leaving it ambiguous voids the sale.","topicId":"t9.6.1","chapter":9,"section":"9.6.1","topicLabel":"Murabaha-Mu'ajjal: Justifying the Credit-Cash Price Differential","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the general conditions of a valid Bai' and Murabaha-Mu'ajjal.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6.1","pages":[220]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch9-10","type":"short","prompt":"Explain, using the book's reasoning, why a higher credit price in Murabaha-Mu'ajjal is not equivalent to Riba on a loan.","explanation":"This captures the reasoning in section 9.6.1 distinguishing credit-sale pricing from interest on a loan.","topicId":"t9.6.1","chapter":9,"section":"9.6.1","topicLabel":"Murabaha-Mu'ajjal: Justifying the Credit-Cash Price Differential","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain why a credit sale premium is not Riba.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6.1","pages":[219,220]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"In a loan, the exchange is between a commodity and its like (e.g. $100 for $100), so any excess is Riba. In a credit sale, the exchange is between two different things - money for goods, then goods for money - so a price differential does not amount to Riba. This aligns with the Fiqhi principle Al-Ghunm bil Ghurm (profit accompanies risk), since prices are inherently variable and either party may gain or lose depending on subsequent price movement."},{"id":"q-ch9-11","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which Murabaha sourcing structure does AAOIFI recommend over appointing the customer as purchasing agent?","explanation":"AAOIFI's standard recommends the third-party-agent option as better, and advises against appointing the customer as agent except in situations of dire need.","topicId":"t9.7","chapter":9,"section":"9.7","topicLabel":"Possible Structures of Murabaha","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Walk through the full MPO transaction structure.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.7.2","pages":[221]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Direct trading by bank management only","Purchase through an independent third-party agent","No purchasing structure - only direct cash payment to the client","Musawamah only, never Murabaha"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch9-12","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank simply transfers funds to a client who has already purchased and received goods directly from a supplier, then labels the transaction 'Murabaha.' What is the correct Shari'ah characterization?","explanation":"The chapter is explicit: if the bank does not purchase and own the items and only makes payment for goods already purchased/received by the client, this is a remittance of money on the client's behalf - a loan, and any profit on it is interest.","topicId":"t9.7","chapter":9,"section":"9.7.3","topicLabel":"Possible Structures of Murabaha","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Walk through the full MPO transaction structure and identify Shari'ah risks.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.7.3","pages":[222]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["A valid Murabaha, since the bank eventually receives its profit margin","A valid Musawamah","In substance a loan with the 'profit' amounting to interest, since the bank never took ownership or risk of the goods","A valid Diminishing Musharakah"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch9-13","type":"mcq","prompt":"What are the three distinct contracts that comprise a modern MPO transaction?","explanation":"MPO comprises: (1) a master contract/promise to purchase, (2) a separate agency contract for purchasing/taking possession, (3) the actual Murabaha contract, concluded once the bank owns the commodity.","topicId":"t9.8.1","chapter":9,"section":"9.8.1","topicLabel":"MPO as a 'Bunch of Contracts'","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Walk through the full MPO transaction structure.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.1","pages":[224]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Musharakah, Mudarabah, and Ijarah","A promise/master contract, an agency contract, and the actual Murabaha sale contract","Salam, Istisna'a, and Murabaha","Wakalah, Kafalah, and Hawalah"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch9-14","type":"scenario","prompt":"A customer gives a binding promise to purchase goods a bank acquires on his request, then backs out after the bank has already purchased the goods and incurred a $2,000 loss reselling them elsewhere (the bank's expected profit had the sale gone through was $3,000). Per Usmani's view (as presented), what can the bank recover from the customer?","explanation":"Usmani's view (adopted in AAOIFI's Standard) is that a binding promise's breach entitles recovery of actual monetary loss only, explicitly excluding opportunity cost.","topicId":"t9.8.2","chapter":9,"section":"9.8.2","topicLabel":"The Binding Nature of the Promise to Purchase","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Walk through the full MPO transaction structure and identify Shari'ah risks.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.2","pages":[225]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The full $3,000 expected profit","The $2,000 actual loss only, not the $1,000 of lost opportunity/expected profit","Nothing, since promises are never enforceable","$5,000 (loss plus expected profit)"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch9-15","type":"mcq","prompt":"Arrange the following MPO (client-as-agent) steps in the correct order: (A) Customer offers to purchase from the bank; (B) Agency agreement signed; (C) Bank accepts offer, Murabaha executed; (D) Client purchases goods as agent and takes possession; (E) Customer submits purchase requisition/promise.","explanation":"Correct order: purchase requisition/promise (E) -> agency agreement (B) -> client purchases as agent (D) -> customer offers to purchase (A) -> bank accepts, Murabaha executed (C). The agency agreement must precede the actual purchase.","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","topicLabel":"MPO Full Procedure","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Walk through the full MPO transaction structure.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[225,226,227,228,229]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"ordering","options":["E, B, D, A, C","B, E, D, C, A","E, D, B, A, C","D, E, B, C, A"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch9-16","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why must the agency agreement in a client-as-agent MPO structure be signed BEFORE the client purchases the goods?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly warns that purchasing before the agency agreement means the goods are already the client's own property, risking Bai' al 'Inah.","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","topicLabel":"MPO Full Procedure","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify the Shari'ah risks at each stage of MPO.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[228]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["It is a mere administrative formality with no Shari'ah significance","If the client purchases first, the goods would already be his property before the bank's involvement, turning the deal into prohibited Bai' al 'Inah","Banking regulators require it for tax purposes only","It has no bearing on Shari'ah compliance"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch9-17","type":"short","prompt":"Why do Shari'ah scholars generally disapprove of Murabaha involving natural gas moving through pipelines?","explanation":"This reflects the pipeline-gas example given in section 9.8.3 to illustrate the requirement for a clear risk-transfer point.","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","topicLabel":"MPO Full Procedure","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify the Shari'ah risks at each stage of MPO.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[228]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Because there is no clearly identifiable point at which possession and ownership risk of the gas transfers from the supplier to the bank, and subsequently from the bank to the client - the precise transfer moment required for valid Murabaha cannot be established."},{"id":"q-ch9-18","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is Bai' al 'Inah?","explanation":"Bai' al 'Inah is a double sale by which borrower and lender sell and resell an object between them - once for cash, again for a higher credit price - with the net result of an interest loan.","topicId":"t9.9.1","chapter":9,"section":"9.9.1","topicLabel":"Buy-back, Option to Rescind, and Timing of Execution","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Analyze contested operational issues in Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.1","pages":[229]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["A partnership contract with profit-sharing","A double sale (cash then higher-price credit resale between the same parties) that produces the effect of an interest loan","A forward sale with advance payment","A lease ending in ownership transfer"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch9-19","type":"tf","prompt":"Even without an explicit Khiyar clause in the contract, Khiyar al 'Aib and Khiyar al Wasf remain juristically available to a Murabaha customer for defective or non-conforming goods discovered before execution.","explanation":"True - the chapter states these options are available from a juristic point of view even without explicit stipulation, if goods are defective or not per specification before Murabaha execution.","topicId":"t9.9.2","chapter":9,"section":"9.9.2","topicLabel":"Buy-back, Option to Rescind, and Timing of Execution","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Analyze contested operational issues in Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.2","pages":[230]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":true},{"id":"q-ch9-20","type":"mcq","prompt":"Where must proceeds from late-payment penalties in Murabaha typically go, per contemporary Shari'ah consensus?","explanation":"Contemporary consensus authorizes late fees to discourage default, but proceeds must be used for charitable purposes; only a court/independent body can allocate part as solatium to the bank.","topicId":"t9.9.4","chapter":9,"section":"9.9.4","topicLabel":"Default Penalties, Rebates, and Rollover","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Analyze contested operational issues in Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.4","pages":[231,232]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Directly into the bank's profit as compensation for delay","To charity, unless a court or independent body allocates part as solatium to the bank","Back to the customer as a rebate","To the central bank as a regulatory fine"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch9-21","type":"short","prompt":"Why does the author caution against the Al Baraka Sudan Fatwah that allows banks to keep late fees calculated on their realized profit rate during the delay period?","explanation":"This reflects the author's explicit caution in section 9.9.4.","topicId":"t9.9.4","chapter":9,"section":"9.9.4","topicLabel":"Default Penalties, Rebates, and Rollover","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Analyze contested operational issues in Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.4","pages":[232]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Because charging a rate based on the bank's actual realized return during the delay period is conceptually very close to the conventional concept of opportunity cost, making it difficult to meaningfully distinguish such Murabaha contracts from Western interest-based trade financing - which threatens the credibility of the Islamic finance distinction."},{"id":"q-ch9-22","type":"tf","prompt":"AAOIFI's Standard on Murabaha unconditionally requires banks to grant a rebate for early payment.","explanation":"False - AAOIFI allows a rebate only if it was not already stipulated in the contract, and it remains at the bank's discretion, not an obligation.","topicId":"t9.9.5","chapter":9,"section":"9.9.5","topicLabel":"Default Penalties, Rebates, and Rollover","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Analyze contested operational issues in Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.5","pages":[232]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch9-23","type":"scenario","prompt":"A client defaults on a Murabaha receivable. The bank books a new Murabaha against the unpaid amount and adds a further mark-up to compensate for the delay. Is this permissible?","explanation":"This is rollover, not rescheduling - adding mark-up to a defaulted receivable is explicit Riba, since the bank cannot re-price a receivable after ownership of the sold goods has already transferred to the client.","topicId":"t9.9.6","chapter":9,"section":"9.9.6","topicLabel":"Default Penalties, Rebates, and Rollover","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Analyze contested operational issues in Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.6","pages":[232,233]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, this is standard rescheduling","No - this is 'rollover,' which is explicit Riba since the bank has no right to re-price a fixed receivable","Yes, provided the client agrees in writing","Yes, if approved by the Shari'ah board"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch9-24","type":"mcq","prompt":"In shares Murabaha, why should the client NOT be appointed agent for purchasing the shares?","explanation":"The chapter states banks should make payment directly to brokers and the client should not be appointed agent for purchasing shares, to properly maintain the bank's ownership and risk-bearing role.","topicId":"t9.9","chapter":9,"section":"9.9.7","topicLabel":"Shares Murabaha and Commodity Murabaha (Tawarruq)","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Analyze contested operational issues in Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.7","pages":[233]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Clients are legally barred from ever acting as agents","To maintain proper ownership/risk-bearing by the bank and avoid Shari'ah compliance issues; the bank should pay brokers directly","Because shares cannot be traded under Islamic law at all","Because only conventional banks can buy shares"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch9-25","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the author's overall stance on commodity Murabaha (Tawarruq) used for treasury liquidity management?","explanation":"The author states Tawarruq should be used in extreme cases only, has not been approved by all scholars, and widespread use is harmful to the Islamic banking industry in the long run.","topicId":"t9.9","chapter":9,"section":"9.9.8","topicLabel":"Shares Murabaha and Commodity Murabaha (Tawarruq)","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Analyze contested operational issues in Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.8","pages":[233]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Fully endorsed by all scholars for routine daily use","Should be used only in extreme cases where no alternative exists to avoid interest; widespread routine use is harmful to the industry's credibility","Completely prohibited under all circumstances","Preferred over Murabaha and Musawamah for all financing"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch9-26","type":"mcq","prompt":"Match each Murabaha risk to its Box 9.1 mitigation tool: (1) Customer refuses to purchase after taking possession as agent; (2) Transit destruction risk; (3) Overdue payment.","explanation":"Box 9.1 pairs: refusal to purchase -> binding promise + Hamish Jiddiyah; transit destruction -> Takaful cover; overdue payment -> charity-bound customer undertaking.","topicId":"t9.10","chapter":9,"section":"9.10","topicLabel":"Precautions and Risk Management in Murabaha","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the accounting treatment principles for Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.10","pages":[234,235]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"matching","options":["1-Hamish Jiddiyah/binding promise, 2-Takaful cover, 3-Charity-bound customer undertaking","1-Takaful cover, 2-Charity undertaking, 3-Hamish Jiddiyah","1-Physical inspection, 2-Related-party disclosure, 3-Performance bond","1-Performance bond, 2-Hamish Jiddiyah, 3-Physical inspection"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch9-27","type":"short","prompt":"Per AAOIFI's accounting treatment (Box 9.3), which method of profit recognition is preferred for Murabaha credit sales spanning multiple financial periods?","explanation":"This matches the AAOIFI accounting treatment summarized in Box 9.3.","topicId":"t9.10","chapter":9,"section":"9.10","topicLabel":"Precautions and Risk Management in Murabaha","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the accounting treatment principles for Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.10","pages":[236,237]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"The proportionate allocation method, which spreads profit recognition across the credit term (may be adopted whether or not cash has actually been received), is described as the preferred method - as an alternative, profit may be recognized as and when cash is received."},{"id":"q-ch9-28","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the core structural difference between Musawamah and Murabaha?","explanation":"Musawamah is a bargained-price sale with no reference to the seller's cost; Murabaha requires disclosing the cost and the agreed profit margin. All other conditions are otherwise the same.","topicId":"t9.11","chapter":9,"section":"9.11","topicLabel":"Musawamah: Bargaining on Price as a Financing Mode","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain how Musawamah differs from Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.11","pages":[234]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Musawamah cannot be used for deferred payment","In Musawamah the seller need not disclose the original cost; in Murabaha the seller must disclose cost and profit margin","Musawamah is only for real estate","Murabaha does not require the seller to own the goods"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch9-29","type":"scenario","prompt":"An Islamic bank purchases retail goods and receives a supplier discount it does not want to disclose to the retail customer. Which sale structure should it use to remain Shari'ah-compliant?","explanation":"The chapter states that where actual profit/discount is not brought to the customer's notice, the sale must be conducted through Musawamah, not Murabaha, which strictly requires accurate cost disclosure.","topicId":"t9.11.1","chapter":9,"section":"9.11.1","topicLabel":"Musawamah: Bargaining on Price as a Financing Mode","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain how Musawamah differs from Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.11","pages":[234]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Murabaha, since the discount can simply be omitted from the disclosure","Musawamah, since it does not require cost/profit disclosure","Salam, since payment is in advance","Istisna'a, since the goods are manufactured"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch9-30","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why do Islamic banks predominantly prefer Murabaha over Musawamah for routine, smaller-scale financing?","explanation":"The chapter explains that direct use of a benchmark is relatively easier in Murabaha, making it easier for management and regulators to manage the return rate structure, and reducing corruption risk since profit is always tied to a disclosed cost.","topicId":"t9.11.1","chapter":9,"section":"9.11.1","topicLabel":"Musawamah: Bargaining on Price as a Financing Mode","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain how Musawamah differs from Murabaha and when banks prefer to use it.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.11.1","pages":[238]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Musawamah is not permitted under Shari'ah","A benchmark-tied, cost-disclosed profit margin in Murabaha is administratively easier to manage, more transparent to regulators, and carries lower corruption risk","Musawamah requires more paperwork than Murabaha","Musawamah cannot be structured with an agency arrangement"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch9-31","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which condition of a valid Bai' does the following violate? 'A says to B on 1st January: I sell my car to you on 1st February.'","explanation":"The sale must be instant and absolute; a sale attributed to a future date is void, though the parties may exchange a mere promise or understanding instead, with rights and liabilities emerging only once actually executed.","topicId":"t9.2","chapter":9,"section":"9.2","topicLabel":"Conditions of a Valid Bai' (Recap)","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"State the general conditions of a valid Bai'.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.2","pages":[214]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Certainty of price","Sale must be instant and absolute - not contingent on a future date","Constructive possession","Khiyar al-Shart"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch9-32","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank enters into a Musharakah with a client, with an upfront agreement that the client will buy out the bank's share through Murabaha at the end. Is this permissible per AAOIFI?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly states the bank is not allowed to enter into a Musharakah arrangement with the client with the promise that one party will buy the other's share through Murabaha; instead, a partner can promise via a separate contract to buy the other's share at market price or a mutually agreed price at the time of sale.","topicId":"t9.8","chapter":9,"section":"9.8","topicLabel":"Murabaha to Purchase Orderer (MPO)","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Identify Shari'ah risks in combining MPO with other contracts.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8","pages":[223]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, this is standard practice","No - AAOIFI prohibits combining a Musharakah with a promise that a partner will buy the other's share through Murabaha; a separate promise to buy at market/agreed price at the time of sale is permissible instead","Yes, provided profit-sharing is disclosed","Yes, if the client is also the bank's agent"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch9-33","type":"tf","prompt":"If an Islamic bank takes an interest-bearing security (e.g. an FDR) as collateral against a Murabaha receivable, some Shari'ah boards allow it, but the bank's recourse is limited to the principal amount only.","explanation":"True - some Shari'ah boards allow taking interest-bearing securities as collateral, but in such a case the bank will have recourse to the extent of principal only; it is preferable the customer encash such instruments for Shari'ah-compliant securities.","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","topicLabel":"MPO Full Procedure","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain security/collateral rules in Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[229]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":true},{"id":"q-ch9-34","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following is a FOREIGN CURRENCY variant of Murabaha used by Islamic banks for trade financing, per the chapter?","explanation":"Foreign-currency variants include sight L/C spot Murabaha, sight L/C deferred Murabaha, and usance L/C Murabaha. Simple Murabaha, advance payment Murabaha and suppliers' credit Murabaha are local-currency variants.","topicId":"t9.10","chapter":9,"section":"9.10","topicLabel":"Precautions and Risk Management in Murabaha","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify the different structures of Murabaha used for trade financing.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.10","pages":[236]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Simple Murabaha","Suppliers' credit Murabaha","Sight L/C deferred Murabaha","Advance payment Murabaha"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch9-35","type":"short","prompt":"Per AAOIFI's accounting treatment (Box 9.3), how is a Murabaha asset measured after acquisition, and what happens if there's an indication its cost may not be recovered?","explanation":"This matches the accounting treatment for asset measurement in Box 9.3.","topicId":"t9.10","chapter":9,"section":"9.10","topicLabel":"Precautions and Risk Management in Murabaha","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the accounting treatment principles for Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.10","pages":[236]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"After acquisition, the asset is measured at historical cost, with any decline in value reflected at the end of the financial period. If there is an indication of nonrecovery of the cost of goods, the asset shall instead be measured at cash equivalent value (net realizable value), by creating a provision for the decline in value reflecting the difference between acquisition cost and cash equivalent value."}],"flashcards":[{"id":"f9.1","front":"Murabaha","back":"A trust (fiduciary) sale at disclosed cost plus an agreed profit margin; when price is deferred it is Murabaha–Mu’ajjal.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t9.3","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.3","pages":[215,215]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.2","front":"Buyoo‘ al Amanat (fiduciary sales)","back":"Tawliyah (at cost), Wadhi‘ah/Mohatah (below cost), Murabaha (cost + profit) — all require honest cost disclosure.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t9.3","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.3","pages":[215,215]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.3","front":"Items that cannot be sold by Murabaha","back":"Currencies, gold and silver (Bai‘ al Sarf) and debt/credit documents (profit on debt is Riba).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.6","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6","pages":[217,219]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.4","front":"How must a Murabaha seller state cost including expenses?","back":"“This article has cost me so much” — not “I purchased at this rate”; customary value-adding expenses may be added.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.6","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6","pages":[217,219]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.5","front":"Supplier rebate received after a Murabaha sale","back":"The client/buyer is entitled to benefit from it (AAOIFI).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.6","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6","pages":[217,219]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.6","front":"Why is a Murabaha credit price above the cash price not Riba?","back":"Money is exchanged for goods (not like for like); price risk exists on both sides — consistent with Al-Ghunm bil Ghurm; one price is fixed.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t9.6.1","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6.1","pages":[219,220]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.7","front":"Three Murabaha structures (Section 9.7)","back":"Direct trading by bank; purchase via third-party agent (AAOIFI preferred); client as agent (most used, back-door risk).","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t9.7","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.7","pages":[220,222]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.8","front":"Three contracts in MPO","back":"(1) Master agreement + client’s unilateral promise; (2) separate agency contract; (3) actual Murabaha after bank owns the goods.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t9.8.1","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.1","pages":[224,224]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.9","front":"Remedy when an MPO client breaks the promise","back":"Specific performance or actual damages (actual monetary loss, no opportunity cost/cost of funds), recoverable from Hamish Jiddiyah.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.8.2","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.2","pages":[224,225]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.10","front":"Seven steps of MPO with client as agent","back":"Request → agency → purchase via client → bank pays supplier → client possesses for bank → client offers/bank accepts → deferred payment (no rollover).","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[225,229]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.11","front":"Maximum ownership link between client and supplier in MPO","back":"Supplier must be a third party — not the customer, his agent or an entity more than 50% owned by the customer.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[225,229]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.12","front":"Rollover in Murabaha","back":"Booking a new Murabaha with extra mark-up on an unpaid receivable — explicit Riba. Rescheduling without increase is allowed.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t9.9.6","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.6","pages":[232,233]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.13","front":"Default tools in Murabaha","back":"Acceleration clause; charity undertaking (late fee to charity); collateral; solatium only via court/independent body for actual damage.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t9.9.4","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.4","pages":[231,232]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.14","front":"Shares Murabaha — key precautions","back":"Screened shares; pay brokers directly; client not agent; bank bears price risk during settlement; not sister-concern shares.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t9.9","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9","pages":[229,233]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.15","front":"Commodity Murabaha (LME Tawarruq) — author’s concern","back":"Doubt whether actual transactions and risk transfer occur; use only in extreme cases; widespread use harms the industry.","category":"Capital markets","topicId":"t9.9","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9","pages":[229,233]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.16","front":"Murabaha vs Musawamah","back":"Murabaha: cost disclosed, margin bargained. Musawamah: price bargained, no disclosure. Same ownership, possession and risk rules.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t9.11","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.11","pages":[234,234]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.17","front":"AAOIFI preferred profit recognition in Murabaha","back":"Proportionate allocation of profit over the credit period (whether or not cash is received); deferred profit offset against receivables.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t9.10","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.10","pages":[233,237]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.18","front":"Tawliyah vs Wadhi‘ah","back":"Tawliyah: resale at cost. Wadhi‘ah (Mohatah): resale at a discount on cost.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t9.3","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.3","pages":[215,215]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.19","front":"Malik vs Shafi‘i on Murabaha","back":"Malik: a spot exchange, no credit (Malikis dislike it but allow it). Shafi‘i: extended it to credit.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t9.4","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.4","pages":[215,216]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.20","front":"Misstated cost in Murabaha — remedies","back":"Malik: rescind unless difference returned. Hanafi: unqualified right to rescind. Hanbali: binding after difference returned.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.6","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6","pages":[217,219]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.21","front":"Credit purchase by the Murabaha seller","back":"Must be disclosed to the buyer — all schools agree, since credit prices are higher.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.6","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6","pages":[217,219]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.22","front":"Which Murabaha structure does AAOIFI prefer?","back":"Purchase through a third-party agent; the client as agent only in dire need.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t9.7","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.7","pages":[220,222]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.23","front":"Bilateral binding promise in Murabaha","back":"Allowed only if an option (Khiyar) is kept by one or both parties; otherwise it is a sale of unowned goods.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.8.2","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.2","pages":[224,225]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.24","front":"MPO relationships","back":"Principal/agent → promisor/promisee → buyer/seller (with supplier) → seller/buyer → creditor/debtor.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[225,229]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.25","front":"Invoice-date check in MPO","back":"Invoice must be dated after the agency agreement and before the client’s offer to purchase; otherwise ‘Inah risk.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[225,229]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.26","front":"Acceleration clause in Murabaha","back":"On unjustified delay, all remaining instalments become due — discipline without increasing the debt.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.9.4","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.4","pages":[231,232]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.27","front":"Box 9.2 — sight L/C settlement options","back":"If no credit needed: spot Murabaha/Musawamah at L/C cost + charges + Takaful. If credit needed: deferred Murabaha, profit from the Nostro debit date.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t9.10","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.10","pages":[233,237]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f9.28","front":"When to use Musawamah instead of Murabaha","back":"Single large deals (e.g. aircraft) or when cost details cannot be disclosed, including undisclosed supplier discounts.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t9.11.1","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.11.1","pages":[238,238]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-1","front":"What are the two main forms of credit sale (Bai' Mu'ajjal) used by Islamic banks?","back":"Musawamah (normal bargained-price sale) and Murabaha (cost-plus sale with disclosed cost and agreed profit margin).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t9.1","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.1","pages":[213]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-2","front":"What does 'constructive possession' mean, and why does it matter for a bank executing Murabaha?","back":"It means the buyer has not taken physical delivery, but ownership risk has transferred and the goods are under his control - it satisfies the Shari'ah possession requirement even without physical handover.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.2","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.2","pages":[214]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-3","front":"What are Buyoo' al Amanat, and which three sale types belong to this family?","back":"Fiduciary sales requiring honest cost disclosure: Tawliyah (resale at cost, no profit), Wadhi'ah/Mohatah (resale at a discount), and Murabaha (resale at cost plus disclosed profit).","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t9.3","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.3","pages":[215]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-4","front":"How did Imam Malik view classical Murabaha, and how did Imam Shafi'e's view differ?","back":"Imam Malik held that Murabaha involves no credit - it's completed by simultaneous exchange. Imam Shafi'e (in Kitabul Umm) expanded the concept to include credit transactions.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t9.4","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.4","pages":[216]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-5","front":"Why did Imam Ahmad prefer Musawamah over Murabaha?","back":"Because Murabaha requires reposing trust in the seller and detailed cost disclosure, creating a real risk that selfishness could lead the seller to make a false statement about cost.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t9.5","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.5","pages":[216]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-6","front":"Can currency, gold/silver, or debt instruments be sold through Murabaha?","back":"No - currency/gold/silver are subject to Bai' al Sarf (requiring simultaneous exchange), and debt instruments cannot be sold via Murabaha because any profit on debt would be Riba.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.6","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6","pages":[217]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-7","front":"Once a Murabaha profit margin is agreed and fixed, can it later be increased?","back":"No - it can only be reduced (via a supplier rebate passed to the buyer), never increased.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.6","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6","pages":[218]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-8","front":"How do the Hanafi and Hanbali schools differ on remedies when a Murabaha seller misstates the original cost?","back":"Hanafis give the buyer an unqualified right to rescind the sale; Hanbalis consider the sale binding once the seller refunds the difference between the correct and stated cost.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t9.6","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6","pages":[218]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-9","front":"What does the Fiqhi principle 'Al-Ghunm bil Ghurm' mean, and how does it apply to Murabaha-Mu'ajjal?","back":"\"Profit goes with liability/risk.\" It justifies why a deferred (credit) price can legitimately differ from the cash price - the party accepting the risk of price movement over time earns or loses accordingly, unlike a fixed-return loan.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t9.6.1","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6.1","pages":[220]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-10","front":"Why is a credit-sale price premium not Riba, while excess on a loan is?","back":"A loan exchanges money for money of the same kind (any excess is Riba); a credit sale exchanges money for goods (different kinds), so a price differential reflects legitimate trade, not interest - provided only one definite price is settled at contract time.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t9.6.1","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6.1","pages":[219,220]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-11","front":"Name the three possible structures for sourcing goods in bank Murabaha.","back":"(1) Direct trading by bank management, (2) purchase through a third-party agent, (3) purchase through the client as the bank's agent.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t9.7","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.7","pages":[220,221,222]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-12","front":"Which Murabaha sourcing structure does AAOIFI recommend, and which does it discourage except in dire need?","back":"AAOIFI recommends purchase through an independent third-party agent, and recommends against appointing the customer as agent except in cases of dire need.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.7","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.7.2","pages":[221]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-13","front":"What does 'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer' (MPO) mean, and what three contracts does it comprise?","back":"An arrangement where the bank, upon customer request, purchases an asset from a third party and sells it to the customer on deferred payment. It comprises: (1) the client's promise to purchase, (2) an agency contract to acquire the goods, (3) the actual Murabaha sale contract.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t9.8","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8-9.8.1","pages":[222,224]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-14","front":"If a customer breaches a binding promise to purchase in MPO, what can the bank recover?","back":"Only its actual monetary loss - never the conventional 'opportunity cost' of lost potential profit.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.8.2","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.2","pages":[225]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-15","front":"Under what condition is a mutual (bilateral) binding promise permissible in a Murabaha sale?","back":"Only if Khiyar (option) is given to one or both parties - otherwise it would function like an ordinary sale contract requiring the seller to already possess the goods, which the bank does not yet at the promise stage.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.8.2","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.2","pages":[225]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-16","front":"What is Bai' al 'Inah, and why is it prohibited?","back":"A double sale where borrower and lender sell and resell an object between them - once for cash, once at a higher price on credit - with the net effect of an interest loan; it is a legal device to circumvent the prohibition of Riba.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t9.9.1","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.1","pages":[229]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-17","front":"In the client-as-agent MPO structure, why must the agency agreement be signed BEFORE the client purchases any goods?","back":"If the client purchases first, the goods would already be his own property before the bank's involvement, turning the transaction into prohibited Bai' al 'Inah.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[228]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-18","front":"What happens to the bank-customer relationship once Murabaha is executed (offer and acceptance completed)?","back":"It converts from buyer-seller (during the agency/purchase stage) into debtor-creditor (the customer owes the deferred Murabaha price), and the bank bears no further liability for the goods.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[229]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-19","front":"Why do Shari'ah scholars generally disapprove of Murabaha involving natural gas moving through pipelines?","back":"Because there is no clear, identifiable point at which possession and ownership risk of the gas transfers from the supplier to the bank, and then from the bank to the customer.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[228]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-20","front":"What is the difference between rescheduling and rollover in Murabaha, and which is permitted?","back":"Rescheduling (extending the payment period without increasing the amount owed) is permitted; rollover (booking a new Murabaha with added markup against an unpaid receivable) is explicit Riba and is not permitted.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.9.6","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.6","pages":[232]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-21","front":"Where must late-payment penalty proceeds go in Murabaha, and who can allocate part of it to the bank?","back":"Proceeds must go to charity; only a court or independent resolution body can allocate a portion as solatium (compensation) to the bank.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.9.4","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.4","pages":[231,232]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-22","front":"What is the majority scholarly view on early-payment rebates in bank Murabaha, and what does AAOIFI allow?","back":"The majority of contemporary scholars do not allow rebates for early payment (too similar to conventional discounting). AAOIFI's Standard allows a rebate only if it was not already stipulated in the contract, at the bank's discretion.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t9.9.5","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.5","pages":[232]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-23","front":"In shares Murabaha, why should the bank pay brokers directly rather than appointing the client as purchasing agent?","back":"To maintain proper Shari'ah compliance and ensure the bank genuinely bears the settlement price risk (typically about three days) before reselling; appointing the client risks blurring genuine ownership/risk transfer.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t9.9","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.7","pages":[233]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-24","front":"What is the author's overall verdict on commodity Murabaha (Tawarruq) as used by some banks for treasury liquidity?","back":"It should be used only in extreme cases where no alternative exists to avoid interest; it lacks universal scholarly approval, and its widespread/routine use is harmful to the Islamic banking industry's long-term credibility.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t9.9","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.8","pages":[233]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-25","front":"Under AAOIFI accounting rules, how is Hamish Jiddiyah treated if the customer's promise was nonbinding vs binding, and he defaults?","back":"Nonbinding promise: Hamish Jiddiyah is returned to the customer in full, even if the asset is resold at a loss. Binding promise: the bank's actual loss is deducted from it, with any shortfall recorded as a receivable from the client.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t9.10","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.10","pages":[237]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-26","front":"What is the one core structural difference between Murabaha and Musawamah?","back":"Murabaha requires the seller to disclose the original cost and agreed profit margin; Musawamah requires no cost disclosure at all - the price is simply bargained. All other sale conditions (ownership, possession, risk before sale) apply identically to both.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t9.11","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.11","pages":[234]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-27","front":"Why do Islamic banks predominantly prefer Murabaha over Musawamah for routine financing?","back":"A benchmark-tied, cost-disclosed profit margin is administratively easier to manage, more transparent for regulators, and carries lower corruption risk than a purely bargained Musawamah price.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t9.11.1","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.11.1","pages":[238]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-28","front":"Why is a sale contract attributed to a future date (e.g. 'I sell you my car on 1st February') void under the general conditions of Bai'?","back":"Sale must be instant and absolute - it cannot be contingent on a future date or event. The parties may exchange an understanding or promise instead, but rights and liabilities only emerge once the sale is actually executed on that future date.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.2","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.2","pages":[214]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-29","front":"What is the difference between an 'agreement to sell' and a 'sale agreement' at the MPO promise stage?","back":"A 'sale agreement' transfers ownership rights to the buyer upon signing. An 'agreement to sell' (used at the MPO promise/MoU stage) is merely a promise to sell a commodity in the future and does not convey ownership rights at all.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[226]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-30","front":"Can a bank enter into a Musharakah with a client on the promise that one partner will buy out the other's share through Murabaha?","back":"No - AAOIFI prohibits combining a Musharakah with a promise that one party will buy the other's share through Murabaha (spot or deferred). A partner may instead promise, via a separate contract, to buy the other's share at market price or a mutually agreed price at the time of sale.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t9.8","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8","pages":[223]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-31","front":"What kinds of security can a bank take against the deferred Murabaha price, and what caution applies to interest-bearing instruments as collateral?","back":"Hypothecation charges, pledges of goods/marketable securities, liens on deposits, mortgages, bank/personal guarantees, or the sold commodity itself. If interest-bearing securities (TDRs/FDRs) are taken, the bank's recourse is limited to the principal only - it is preferable the customer encash them for Shari'ah-compliant securities instead.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[229]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-32","front":"What local-currency and foreign-currency variants of Murabaha does the chapter list for trade financing?","back":"Local currency: simple Murabaha, advance payment Murabaha, suppliers' credit Murabaha. Foreign currency: sight L/C spot Murabaha, sight L/C deferred Murabaha, usance L/C Murabaha.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t9.10","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.10","pages":[236]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch9-33","front":"Why might a bank charge a higher profit margin when it makes advance payment to the supplier in an MPO transaction?","back":"Advance payment to the supplier (rather than post-supply payment) ties up the bank's funds earlier and increases its exposure, so it is advisable for the bank to charge a higher profit margin than in the case of post-supply payment.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[227]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}],"exam":[{"id":"e9.1","kind":"long","prompt":"Explain Murabaha to Purchase Orderer (MPO) as practised by Islamic banks. Discuss its stages and the Shari’ah safeguards required at each.","expectedStructure":["Define Murabaha and MPO","Why MPO is needed","Three contracts","Stages: pre-promise, MoU, requisition, agency, purchase, possession, execution, security","Relationships among parties","Key safeguards (no ‘Inah, direct payment, risk before sale, goods exist)","Post-execution: no rollover; default; rebate"],"keyConcepts":["Wa‘d","Wakalah","constructive possession","Hamish Jiddiyah"],"essentialPoints":["Supplier ≤50% client-owned","Invoice dating","Debtor–creditor after acceptance"],"commonMistakes":["Treating promise as sale","Letting bank sell before owning","Allowing rollover"],"topicId":"t9.8.3","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.8.3","pages":[225,229]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e9.2","kind":"difference","prompt":"Differentiate between Murabaha and Musawamah and explain when each is suitable for banks.","expectedStructure":["Definitions","Disclosure","Benchmark use","Suitability examples","Common conditions"],"keyConcepts":["trust sale","bargaining"],"essentialPoints":["Undisclosed discount → Musawamah","Airline example"],"commonMistakes":["Saying Musawamah needs no ownership"],"topicId":"t9.11","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.11","pages":[234,234]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e9.3","kind":"short","prompt":"What are the specific conditions of Murabaha?","expectedStructure":["Eligible goods","Cost and expenses disclosure (school views)","Disclosure of credit purchase/defects/rebates","Fixed margin and price","Consequences of false statement"],"keyConcepts":["Majhul price","Naqis"],"essentialPoints":["‘Cost me’ wording"],"commonMistakes":["Allowing currency Murabaha"],"topicId":"t9.6","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6","pages":[217,219]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e9.4","kind":"scenario","prompt":"A client defaults on Murabaha instalments and asks the bank to extend the period. The bank proposes a new Murabaha with additional mark-up. Advise the bank.","expectedStructure":["Identify rollover","Why it is Riba","Permissible options: rescheduling, acceleration clause, charity penalty, collateral","Compensation via court for actual loss"],"keyConcepts":["rollover","late-payment penalty"],"essentialPoints":["No re-pricing"],"commonMistakes":["Suggesting bank keeps penalty"],"topicId":"t9.9.6","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.6","pages":[232,233]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e9.5","kind":"viva","prompt":"Can an Islamic bank give a rebate to a Murabaha client who pays early?","expectedStructure":["Majority view","AAOIFI discretionary rebate"],"keyConcepts":["prepayment rebate"],"essentialPoints":["Not stipulated; case by case"],"commonMistakes":["Saying it must be contractual"],"topicId":"t9.9.5","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.9.5","pages":[232,232]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e9.6","kind":"long","prompt":"Discuss the risks in Murabaha operations and how an Islamic bank mitigates them while staying Shari’ah compliant.","expectedStructure":["Nature of Murabaha risk: asset, fiduciary, legal, Shari’ah","Client refusal: promise, Hamish Jiddiyah, option to return","‘Inah risk: direct payment, invoices, related parties","Goods consumed before sale: inspection, offer timing","Transit risk: Takaful at bank’s cost","Default: charity undertaking, acceleration, collateral; no rollover"],"keyConcepts":["Box 9.1","Hamish Jiddiyah","Bai‘ al ‘Inah","rollover","Takaful"],"essentialPoints":["Bank must own and bear risk before sale","Penalty to charity, not income","Rescheduling without re-pricing"],"commonMistakes":["Transferring transit risk to the client","Charging cost of funds","Letting client buy before agency"],"topicId":"t9.10","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.10","pages":[233,237]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-4","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"List and explain the essential conditions for a valid Bai' (sale) contract.","expectedStructure":["State that qualified parties and free mutual consent are required","Cover certainty of price/date/place of delivery","Cover ownership/possession, existence, and specific identification of the subject matter","Cover that the sale must be instant, absolute and unconditional"],"keyConcepts":["Bai'","Free consent","Constructive possession","Certainty of price"],"essentialPoints":["The seller must own the object of sale, or act as an agent of the owner, and hold actual or constructive possession of it.","The subject of sale must exist at the time of sale, be specifically identified, and be lawful and of value.","The price must be certain and fixed once and for all — it cannot later change, even on late payment.","A sale attributed to a future date or contingent on a future event is void; only a promise, not the sale itself, can precede the executed contract."],"commonMistakes":["Forgetting that constructive possession (control and transferred risk) is sufficient — physical delivery is not always required.","Confusing a binding promise to sell with the sale contract itself."],"topicId":"t9.2","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.2","pages":[214]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-5","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Explain Murabaha and the specific conditions that distinguish it from an ordinary sale.","expectedStructure":["Define Murabaha as a fiduciary (Bai' al Amanah) cost-plus sale","State the specific conditions (real subject matter, no currency/debt, cost and expenses disclosure, seller's honesty)","Note the purpose (protecting an unskilled buyer) and modern deferred-payment use"],"keyConcepts":["Murabaha","Bai' al Amanah","Cost disclosure"],"essentialPoints":["Murabaha is a sale at the seller's disclosed original cost plus a stipulated, mutually agreed profit margin.","The goods traded must be real (though not necessarily tangible); currency and debt instruments cannot be sold through Murabaha.","The seller must truthfully state the original price and any additional legitimate expenses, and must be just and true to his words.","Classical jurists saw Murabaha's purpose as protecting buyers who lack the skill to bargain (Musawamah) well themselves."],"commonMistakes":["Describing Murabaha simply as 'buy low, sell high' without the cost-disclosure/trustworthiness element that defines it.","Assuming the profit margin in Murabaha must be lower than in Musawamah — the book draws no such comparison of margins, only of disclosure."],"topicId":"t9.6","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.6","pages":[217]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-6","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Distinguish Murabaha from Musawamah, and explain when a bank would prefer to use Musawamah instead.","expectedStructure":["State the core difference (cost disclosure)","Explain the shared general conditions","Give situations favoring Musawamah (uncertain cost, large single transactions, undisclosed discounts)"],"keyConcepts":["Murabaha","Musawamah","Cost disclosure"],"essentialPoints":["In Murabaha the seller discloses cost and adds a stated profit; in Musawamah the parties simply bargain on a final price with no cost reference.","All general conditions of a valid Bai', and Murabaha's specific conditions, apply equally to Musawamah.","Banks prefer Musawamah when they cannot precisely ascertain their own cost, for very large single transactions, or where they have obtained an undisclosed supplier discount that should not be passed on as 'cost'."],"commonMistakes":["Assuming Musawamah is somehow less Shari'ah-compliant than Murabaha — the book treats both as fully valid forms of Bai'.","Missing that determining a fair profit benchmark is harder in Musawamah since cost is never disclosed."],"topicId":"t9.11","chapter":9,"source":{"chapter":9,"section":"9.11","pages":[234]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}]});
